\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Justice Department Files Lawsuit Challenging Idaho  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; DOJ says Idaho law conflicts with federal law covering hospitals that receive Medicare\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sadie Gurman and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 August 2022\par 21:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Justice Department on Tuesday filed a lawsuit challenging Idaho's near total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting up the Biden administration's first legal battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access since the Supreme Court in June overturned Roe v. Wade and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated the constitutional right to end a pregnancy }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit says Idaho's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions unlawfully conflict with a federal law that requires hospitals accepting Medicare to provide emergency treatments, which can sometimes include \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If a patient comes into the emergency room with a medical emergency jeopardizing the patient's life or health, hospitals must provide the treatment necessary to stabilize that patient," {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.idd.50547/gov.uscourts.idd.50547.1.0_1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Attorney General Merrick Garland said }}}\uc2 . "This includes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when that is the necessary treatment."\par \par \uc2 Idaho has a ban set to take effect later this month. The law has exceptions allowing doctors to perform abortions to save the life of a pregnant woman or in cases of rape or incest that have been reported to law enforcement. It explicitly excludes cases in which a physician believes that a woman will harm herself if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't performed.\par \par \uc2 Penalties for providers who violate Idaho's law range from two to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland had vowed to take legal action to protect access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Supreme Court's ruling left a patchwork of state laws, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups had been urging the Biden administration to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/attorney-general-merrick-garland-says-government-could-sue-states-over-abortion-access-11658354465"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 look for all available legal avenues }}}\uc2  to preserve at least some availability of the procedure in states seeking to eliminate it.\par \par \uc2 Idaho Gov. \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 , a Republican, said in a statement that the Biden administration was "overreaching" after the Supreme Court left the issue of regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states.\par \par \uc2 "I will continue to work with [Idaho] Attorney General Lawrence Wasden to vigorously uphold state sovereignty and defend Idaho's laws in the face of federal meddling," Mr. Little said.\par \par \uc2 Idaho is one of 13 states that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-prepare-to-quickly-implement-abortion-trigger-laws-11655385028"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed so-called trigger laws }}}\uc2  in recent years that were designed to take effect immediately or quickly after a decision overturning Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the June decision Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, a majority of the court said there was no provision in the Constitution that implicitly protected the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , returning the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 Idaho's law is similar to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dozen or so other laws }}}\uc2  that have been going into effect in states around the country, all of which provide exceptions for the life of the mother and some of which also allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the case of rape, incest or potential severe health issues for the mother or fetus.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have challenged a number of these laws in state courts, in part arguing that these exceptions are vaguely worded and could put them at legal risk in providing care for miscarriages and life-threatening pregnancy complications. Planned Parenthood and an Idaho \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider filed a lawsuit against the trigger law in the Idaho Supreme Court days after the Supreme Court's decision in June. They claim the law violates protections for privacy and equal protection in the state constitution and that its language is too vague for providers to make determinations about what care they can legally provide.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's lawsuit is the first of its kind since the department formed a task force led by Associate Attorney General Vanita Gupta to evaluate state laws that hinder women's ability to seek abortions in other states where the procedure remains legal or that ban federally approved medication that terminates a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The department's lawsuit relies on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hospitals-must-provide-abortions-in-emergency-situations-biden-administration-says-11657569637"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act }}}\uc2 , which requires hospitals to provide treatment to save a patient's life, as well as to prevent organ dysfunction or serious impairment of bodily function. Federal officials have said they would enforce that law, and the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  recently issued guidance intended to reduce confusion among hospitals and doctors about when an emergency \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\par \par \uc2 Justice Department officials sued because they viewed Idaho's law as among the most restrictive.\par \par \uc2 Asked why the Justice Department chose Idaho as its first target, Mr. Garland said that "we have in front of us a statute that seems to us on its face to directly contradict EMTALA that is about to take effect that will threaten the health of women who come to the emergency room in a really dire medical situation."\par \par \uc2 EMLATA also is at the center of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-sues-biden-administration-over-abortion-medical-emergency-guidelines-11657836049"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lawsuit filed by the state of Texas }}}\uc2 against the Biden administration saying the \uc2 HHS\uc2  guidance runs counter to the court's Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's lawsuit says Idaho's law conflicts with EMLATA by preventing doctors from performing abortions "even when a doctor determines that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the medically necessary treatment to prevent severe risk to the patient's health and even in cases where denial of care will likely result in death for the pregnant patient."\par \par \uc2 Even if successful, the lawsuit may have limited impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Idaho, because it only addresses the availability of the procedure in emergency situations, legal experts said.\par \par \uc2 "The law places medical professionals in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/doctors-struggle-with-state-abortion-restrictions-at-odds-with-federal-law-11657445400?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an impossible situation }}}\uc2 ," Ms. Gupta said Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "They must either withhold stabilizing treatment\u8230\'20\'26or risk felony prosecution and license revocation. The law will chill providers' willingness to perform abortions in emergency situations and will hurt patients by blocking access to medically necessary healthcare."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland noted that there had been widespread reports of delays and denials of treatment to women experiencing emergencies, and he signaled the possibility of additional litigation along the same lines.\par \par \uc2 "As a place to start, this is pretty low risk both legally and politically compared with the other options that may have been on the table," said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 . "There are stories coming out from lots of states where these emergency protections are very narrow. It may be the Department of Justice looking at this and saying, this is a crisis in the making if these states are enacting these rules."\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sadie Gurman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:sadie.gurman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sadie.gurman@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-files-lawsuit-challenging-idaho-abortion-law-11659463607"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Justice Department Files Lawsuit Challenging Idaho \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220802ei82004mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
