\par \par \uc2 Canvassers with Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and opposes the amendment. PHOTO: Joe Barrett/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 As a result, Kansas has become a destination for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the South and the Midwest in the past year, according to Iman Alsaden, chief medical officer of Planned Parenthood Great Plains, which serves Arkansas, Kansas, Oklahoma and Missouri. Dr. Alsaden said since the Dobbs decision, she has seen patients drive from places like Galveston, Texas, and Mississippi to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Money has poured into the campaign, including from some of the most powerful national groups on both sides. The Value Them Both Coalition, which opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and supports the constitutional amendment, has raised about $4.7 million this year, according to campaign-finance reports submitted July 18. \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, one of the country's largest antiabortion groups, has pledged to spend at least $1.3 million on the campaign.\par \par \uc2 Value Them Both in one video ad tells voters that Kansas could become "a haven in the United States for unrestricted, unlimited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " if the amendment fails.\par \par \uc2 The group has organized grass-roots efforts in all 105 counties, with some 1,200 volunteers conducting door-knocks as well as partnerships with many organizations and faith groups, said Danielle Underwood, a spokeswoman for Value Them Both.\par \par \uc2 Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and opposes the amendment, has raised some $6.5 million this year, including more than $1 million from various groups associated with Planned Parenthood, according to campaign-finance reports. Ashley All, a spokeswoman for Kansans for Constitutional Freedom, said the group raised $100,000 the day the Supreme Court decision was released, more than three times what it had raised up until that point in June. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
