\par\par \uc2 Health Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Birth-Control Access Bill Passed by House; Contraception legislation follows effort to protect same-sex and interracial marriage\b0\par\par\uc2 By Natalie Andrews and Eliza Collins \par \uc2 807 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2022\par 22:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The House on Thursday passed a bill that would protect access to contraception on a federal level, as Democratic lawmakers say they fear the recent Supreme Court ruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ending the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  could endanger {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-in-favor-of-same-sex-marriage-1435180972"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other protections. }}}\par \par \uc2 The bill passed 228-195, with eight Republicans siding with Democrats, while two Republicans voted present. It follows another piece of legislation that passed this week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-to-vote-on-protections-for-same-sex-and-interracial-marriage-11658239485"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to protect same-sex and interracial marriage }}}\uc2 , in which about four dozen Republicans joined Democrats in support.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are seen as the underdogs in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/midterm-elections-the-neighborhoods-reshaping-political-power-across-america-11655152764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November's midterm elections }}}\uc2  in the House, and they have been rolling out a series of votes related to privacy rights. Many Republicans have accused Democrats of playing politics by setting up votes on rights that aren't in danger and crafting bill text that GOP lawmakers can't accept due to concerns about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or other issues.\par \par \uc2 "Should we have a session on the birds and the bees?" House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (D., Calif.) said Thursday about her response to Republicans who opposed the contraception bill. "You don't want birth control, but you want control of women."\par \par \uc2 The legislation protects access to any contraceptive device, including all contraceptive products approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , including intrauterine devices known as IUDs and emergency contraception such as Plan B.\par \par \uc2 GOP lawmakers who opposed the contraceptives bill said it could open the door to broader \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access or potentially force medical workers to provide contraception even if it goes against their religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Mariannette Miller-Meeks\uc2  (R., Iowa), a physician, said the legislation "eliminates conscience protection laws and singles out that all providers would be required to administer contraceptives despite their moral or religious beliefs" because the bill lacks language protecting physicians. Democrats say the bill forces the government, not individual providers, to enforce the right to contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Miller-Meeks, along with GOP Reps. Nancy Mace of South Carolina and Ashley Hinson of Iowa, on Wednesday introduced legislation to improve access to over-the-counter birth-control pills that have been approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "I have and will continue to support women through increased access to healthcare resources like these," said Ms. Mace, who voted for the Democrats' bill. She showed up at votes with tape on the back of her jacket that read: "My state is banning exceptions, protect contraception."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Mace, who said she is against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of rape, incest and danger to the life of the mother, said access to emergency contraceptives was especially important in states that had banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A Democrat-led effort in the Senate to pass a similar contraception bill by unanimous consent failed Thursday afternoon, when Sen. \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  (R., Iowa) objected. Senate Democrats haven't announced a plan to hold a floor vote. The legislation, along with the same-sex marriage bill, would need the support of at least 10 Republicans to advance in the evenly divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 According to the National Survey of Family Growth, 2017-2019, about 65% of women between the ages of 15 and 49 in the U.S. were using contraception. Gallup public-opinion polls show access to contraceptives is backed by more than 9 in 10 Americans.\par \par \uc2 In 1965, the Supreme Court ruled in Griswold v. Connecticut that the U.S. Constitution protects the liberty of married couples to buy and use contraceptives without government restriction, overturning a Connecticut law that banned birth control. A later ruling guaranteed access to all people regardless of marital status.\par \par \uc2 Some conservative state legislatures have discussed contraception access in the wake of the Supreme Court ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Idaho state Rep. Brent Crane\u8212\'20\'14the Republican chair of the House State Affairs Committee, which oversees \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in the state\u8212\'20\'14told local reporters he would like to discuss banning emergency contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 A Louisiana House committee earlier this month passed a bill that said "human personhood" begins at the point of fertilization, which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates like Mrs. Pelosi say could be used to ban intrauterine devices and emergency contraception. The state's Democratic governor said he would veto the bill should it pass.\par \par \uc2 In a concurring opinion last month when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court overturned Roe }}}\uc2 , Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote that if the legal underpinnings of Roe were wrong, then so were the underpinnings of other rights not enumerated in the Constitution that the court recognized in recent decades, such as the right to same-sex marriage and contraception.\par \par \uc2 Teresa Mettela contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Natalie Andrews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Natalie.Andrews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Natalie.Andrews@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins+1@wsj.com."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins+1@wsj.com. }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/birth-control-access-bill-heads-to-house-vote-11658406600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Birth-Control Access Bill Passed by House }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220721ei7l002gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
