\par\par \uc2 Houses of Worship\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and the Grumbling Crowd; The case against denying communion: Jesus broke bread with sinners.\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Martin \par \uc2 854 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2022\par 22:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Should a Catholic politician who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights receive communion? American bishops have been split on the question at least since Sen. John Kerry, a Catholic who strongly supported "the right to choice," ran for president in 2004. The furor died down after Mr. Kerry lost, but the debate returned when President Biden became the first Catholic to occupy the Oval Office since Roe v. Wade in 1973.\par \par \uc2 The communion question, at least for Mr. Biden, seemed settled. Cardinal \uc2 Wilton Gregory\uc2 , the archbishop of Washington, said he wouldn't deny communion to Mr. Biden. The pastor of Holy Trinity Parish in Georgetown, where the president often attends Mass, agreed. But earlier this year, Archbishop Salvatore J. Cordileone of San Francisco {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sfarchdiocese.org/notification-to-the-speaker-of-the-house-of-representatives-of-the-united-states-congress-nancy-pelosi/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declared}}}\uc2  that Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  would be barred from receiving communion in his archdiocese.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Cordileone, along with the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usccb.org/about/leadership/usccb-general-assembly/upload/usccb-forming-consciences-faithful-citizenship-introductory-letter-20191112.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 calls}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the "pre-eminent" moral issue of our time. If a Catholic acts against that teaching or opposes it in the political realm, then that person is not "in communion" with the church. The archbishop has written that a Catholic legislator who supports "procured \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " commits "a manifestly grave sin which is a cause of most serious scandal to others." Universal church law, Archbishop Cordileone pointed out in his declaration, provides that such persons "are not to be admitted to Holy Communion (Code of Canon Law, can. 915)."\par \par \uc2 But there is another approach. Bishop Robert W. McElroy of San Diego, recently nominated by \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  to become a cardinal, has argued against the "weaponization" of the Eucharist. After all, could any Catholic pass the test of worthiness for communion? "It is the moral obligation of Catholics to embrace all the teachings of the church in their entirety," he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americamagazine.org/faith/2021/05/05/joe-biden-communion-eucharistic-exclusion-abortion-us-bishops-240596"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 writes}}}\uc2 . "But failure in fulfilling that obligation in its fullness cannot be the measure of eucharistic worthiness in a church of sinners and questioners, who must face intense pressures and complexities in their daily lives."\par \par \uc2 Bishop McElroy also notes that the focus of these restrictions is often highly selective. Why target only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? There are other important "life issues." Consider former Attorney General William Barr, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americamagazine.org/politics-society/2020/12/23/william-barr-federal-executions-catholic-death-penalty-239582"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  the death penalty, which the "Catechism of the Catholic Church" clearly declares "inadmissible." Yet there was little outcry about Mr. Barr's receiving communion. By focusing only on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pastors risk politicizing something sacred. "The Eucharist must never be instrumentalized for a political end, no matter how important," Bishop McElroy says.\par \par \uc2 Amid these controversies, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  offers the church guidance. The pope, like me and like virtually all Catholic clergy, is pro-life. Yet there is a difference in how the bishop of Rome and the American bishops view \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as related to other life issues. In his apostolic exhortation "Gaudete et Exsultate," \uc2 Pope Francis{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vatican.va/content/francesco/en/apost_exhortations/documents/papa-francesco_esortazione-ap_20180319_gaudete-et-exsultate.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 begins}}}\uc2  his discussion by acknowledging that "our defence of the innocent unborn . . . needs to be clear, firm and passionate, for at stake is the dignity of a human life, which is always sacred and demands love for each person, regardless of his or her stage of development."\par \par \uc2 But he recognizes that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't the only life issue: "Equally sacred . . . are the lives of the poor, those already born, the destitute, the abandoned and the underprivileged, the vulnerable infirm and elderly exposed to covert euthanasia, the victims of human trafficking, new forms of slavery, and every form of rejection."\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  also is clear about the best on-the-ground applications of these teachings. "I have never denied communion to anyone," he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americamagazine.org/faith/2021/09/15/pope-francis-biden-abortion-plane-241435"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  last year. As for Mr. Biden's receiving communion despite his "inconsistency" with church teaching, the pope {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncronline.org/news/politics/communion-politicians-pope-reiterates-bishops-should-be-pastoral"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed}}}\uc2  it a question for Mr. Biden's conscience and his pastors.\par \par \uc2 The best solution may be to observe Jesus in the Gospels. He called people away from sin and to a metanoia\u8212\'20\'14a word usually translated as "repentance" but better understood as a thoroughgoing change of mind and heart rather than solely a desire to repent. Yet during his public ministry, Jesus also regularly dined with "tax collectors and sinners," much to the consternation of not only the crowds but his disciples.\par \par \uc2 In the Gospel of Luke, when Jesus invites himself to dine at the house of Zacchaeus, the chief tax collector in Jericho, "all who saw it began to grumble" (Luke 19:7). The crowd disapproved of Jesus' breaking bread with Zacchaeus, who probably would have been seen as the "chief sinner" in the town thanks to his collusion with the Romans.\par \par \uc2 When I asked the late New Testament scholar Father Daniel J. Harrington about this passage, he pointed to the Greek word panta, which means "all." He says the grumblers "would have included the disciples." Even Jesus' closest advisers were against breaking bread with sinners. He wasn't. It's no surprise that the controversy, and the grumbling, continues.\par \par \uc2 Father Martin, a Jesuit priest, is editor at large of America Media and author of "Learning to Pray: A Guide for Everyone."\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-and-the-grumbling-crowd-biden-pope-francis-communion-eucharist-cordileone-jesus-catholic-church-11658415080"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  and the Grumbling Crowd}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220721ei7l008hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
