\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Biden Is Pressed to Declare Emergencies After Climate,  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Setbacks; Some Democrats and activists want president to use executive powers to unlock more authority and funding; declarations would face criticism and legal challenges\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tarini Parti \par \uc2 1,388 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par 20:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14President Biden, who has at times expressed reluctance to take executive action to get around gridlock in Congress, is coming under increasing pressure by Democratic lawmakers and activists to make greater use of an authority that some in the party have been skeptical of in the past: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-tackle-climate-change-during-visit-to-former-coal-power-plant-11658247924"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emergency powers.  }}}\par \par \uc2 The president is facing calls to declare a public-health emergency to expand access to abortions after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2 . He is also being pressed to use a national-emergency declaration and other emergency powers to tackle climate-related priorities after they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/joe-manchin-wont-support-bill-that-includes-climate-and-tax-measures-11657848978"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were stripped out of Democrats' budget bill  }}}\uc2  because of opposition from Sen. Joe Manchin (D., W.Va.).\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-calls-climate-an-emergency-as-he-prepares-executive-actions-11658347066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stopped short of formally declaring climate a national emergency  }}}\uc2 during an event Wednesday at a former coal-fired power plant in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 "Let me be clear: Climate change is an emergency," he said. "And in the coming weeks, I'm going to use the power I have as president to turn these words into formal official government actions through the appropriate proclamations, executive orders and regulatory power that the president possesses."\par \par \uc2 An emergency order can give the president the ability to act faster and with broader discretion than a typical executive order, experts say. Presidents can declare an emergency to help unlock more federal resources under different laws: the National Emergencies Act, Stafford Act, Public Health Service Act and Defense Production Act.\par \par \uc2 Since the National Emergencies Act was passed in 1976, presidents from both parties have used it to declare a total of 75 states of national emergency\u8212\'20\'1468 of which were used to impose sanctions, according to the Brennan Center for Justice, which conducted a two-year project on emergency powers. Of that total, 41 are still in effect, and 37 of those are sanctions related.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said Tuesday that both actions\u8212\'20\'14a public-health emergency on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/senate-climate-setback-puts-pressure-on-biden-to-take-charge-11657976402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national emergency on climate  }}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14were under consideration, and that each type of emergency declaration would unlock a different set of authorities and funding. Both steps are opposed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Emergency powers have typically been used in response to natural disasters, health crises, foreign attacks and to impose sanctions against a foreign threat. For example, a public-health emergency to deal with the Covid-19 pandemic was implemented under former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and remains in effect, though Republicans and Democrats have argued over {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/is-covid-19-emergency-over-for-both-political-parties-it-depends-on-issue-11650706204"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whether that emergency status is still needed.  }}}\uc2  and remains in effect, though Republicans and Democrats have argued over\par \par \uc2 Presidents from both parties have been accused at times of overstepping their authority with such declarations.\par \par \uc2 "Emergency powers were never meant to address longstanding problems or to implement long-term or semipermanent solutions," said Elizabeth Goitein, an expert on emergency powers for the Brennan Center for Justice. "The purpose is to give presidents a temporary boost in powers to address problems that Congress can't address quickly or are moving too quickly."\par \par \uc2 Several climate groups and some members of Congress, including Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.), support declaring a national emergency on climate. The Biden administration had set a goal of cutting U.S. greenhouse gas emissions by 50% to 52% below 2005 levels by 2030, a goal that analysts say the country is now unlikely to meet without swift action. Industry groups and GOP lawmakers have said the steps that environmental groups have pushed for under a climate emergency could raise consumer prices and hurt U.S. energy companies.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2  (R., W.Va.) said recently that if the president moved forward with the emergency, he would be using "made-up powers to circumvent Congress and govern by executive overreach."\par \par \uc2 The emergency declaration would allow Mr. Biden to take further steps to limit oil exports and drilling, and potentially to shift federal funding to help domestic wind, solar and other clean-technology manufacturing. But it could also open up the administration to legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should President Biden declare national emergencies to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and climate change? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said recently he was prepared to use "every power" he has as president to deal with global warming. Already, he has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-to-waive-solar-import-tariffs-for-two-years-11654525408"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invoked emergency powers  }}}\uc2  to waive tariffs on solar imports and to use the Defense Production Act to boost U.S. manufacturing of solar-panel components, electric heat pumps and other clean-energy technology.\par \par \uc2 On \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the White House has said that declaring a public-health emergency to deal with the Roe ruling is still on the table, but aides have argued that the legal authority and resources it would unlock would be limited. The bulk of the government's existing funding for public-health emergencies has already gone toward tackling the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups, including Planned Parenthood, and several lawmakers have continued to lobby the White House to take that step. In a letter to Mr. Biden, a group of senators said the move could further protect access to medical abortions and allow Medicaid programs to more easily accept out-of-state patients seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's consideration of emergency powers to tackle climate and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes three years after Mr. Trump faced bipartisan criticism and legal challenges, including from environmental groups, when he {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/trump-set-to-declare-national-emergency-at-border-sign-legislation-to-fund-government-11550241799"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 used a national-emergency declaration  }}}\uc2  to pay for a wall along the U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 The move was designed to allow him to access billions of dollars from military-construction projects and other federal sources to build a wall on the border with Mexico after Congress passed a spending bill with $1.38 billion of funding for new wall construction, a fraction of what Mr. Trump had sought.\par \par \uc2 Lower courts found in preliminary decisions that Mr. Trump exceeded his authority in declaring a national emergency to fund the border wall, but in 2020 the Supreme Court by a 5-4 vote permitted construction to move forward while the litigation proceeded. The court's unsigned order created no legal precedent or explanation for the decision, meaning the high court didn't rule on the extent of a president's emergency powers.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Biden took office, he ended the national emergency declared by Mr. Trump for border-wall construction. But some Democrats, who opposed Mr. Trump's emergency declaration, say it set the stage for Mr. Biden to make more use of the authority.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Jeff Merkley\uc2  (D., Ore.) said Mr. Trump's case can now be seen as a "test run" for Mr. Biden to declare a climate emergency, though the court never ruled specifically on emergency powers.\par \par \uc2 Jean Su, a senior attorney for the \uc2 Center for Biological Diversity\uc2  who was involved in litigating the case against Mr. Trump and supports Mr. Biden declaring a climate emergency, said the use of emergency powers in those two scenarios is different because the climate measures would be much more tailored. But Mr. Trump's case did play a role in groups pushing Mr. Biden to declare a climate emergency, she said.\par \par \uc2 "The Trump administration opened up new insights into old laws," Ms. Su said.\par \par \uc2 The efforts around emergency powers for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and climate follow concerns from some Democrats regarding potential abuse of those authorities.\par \par \uc2 Some lawmakers, including \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  chairman \uc2 Jerrold Nadler\uc2  (D., N.Y.), have called for legislation adjusting those powers with more limitations and congressional oversight. The committee held a hearing in May to consider changes to the National Emergency Act. A bipartisan group of lawmakers led by Sens. \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  (D., Conn.) and Mike Lee (R., Utah) also introduced legislation last year that would make changes to emergency powers.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, as a candidate and as president, has also said he prefers congressional action over executive orders. "There's a Constitution," he said in May when asked about more executive actions on restricting guns. "I can't dictate this stuff."\par \par \uc2 Siobhan Hughes and Jess Bravin contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tarini Parti at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Tarini.Parti@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tarini.Parti@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-faces-pressure-to-declare-emergencies-after-climate-abortion-setbacks-11658318400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden Is Pressed to Declare Emergencies After Climate, \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Setbacks  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220720ei7k00233\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
