\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 House Passes Bill to Protect Same-Sex and Interracial Marriages; Democrats say bill is needed after Supreme Court ruling striking down  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Eliza Collins \par \uc2 1,270 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par 00:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The House passed a bill that would provide protection for same-sex and interracial marriage under federal law, with Democratic lawmakers saying {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the recent Supreme Court ruling }}}\uc2  ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/clarence-thomass-abortion-opinion-revisits-same-sex-marriage-contraception-11656161978"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could endanger other precedents }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 267-157 vote on the Respect for Marriage Act comes the same week the chamber is also set to vote on the Right to Contraception Act. Both pieces of legislation would enshrine into law rights not enumerated in the Constitution but that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-in-favor-of-same-sex-marriage-1435180972"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the court has recognized }}}\uc2  in recent decades. Senate Democrats haven't said if they will schedule votes on the House bills.\par \par \uc2 With these votes, Democrats are challenging Republicans to line up behind rights favored by most Americans. On the marriage bill, 47 GOP lawmakers joined with all Democrats in support, while other Republicans dismissed it as a political stunt to protect rights that don't need protecting.\par \par \uc2 Rep. David Cicilline (D., R.I.), a co-sponsor of the marriage legislation who is gay, said "millions of LGBTQ families\u8230\'20\'26are worried about the Supreme Court's intention to rip away more freedoms."\par \par \uc2 Rep. Mike Johnson (R., La.) said that Democrats proposed the legislation so they could run ads in an election cycle. "This bill is just another superfluous exercise, this bill is completely unnecessary," he said on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are seen as the underdogs in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-move-abortion-fight-to-midterm-elections-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656098522"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November's midterm elections }}}\uc2  in the House, and they have been rolling out a series of votes related to hot-button social issues. Many of the votes have been designed to underscore contrasts with Republicans headed into the elections rather than pass new law, as on issues like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  there is little chance for compromise seen in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Many of the Republican "yes" votes came from lawmakers representing competitive districts, while other support came from some conservative members who are from coastal, populous states such as New York, California and Florida.\par \par \uc2 The bill also received the support of two conservative members of GOP leadership: Rep. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York, the conference chairwoman, and \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  of Minnesota, who leads the House GOP campaign arm. It also got the backing of Rep. Scott Perry of Pennsylvania, the chair of the conservative House Freedom Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Republican Rep. \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  of New York said ahead of the vote she would support the bill. In a statement, she said a vote against a bill legalizing same-sex marriage as a state lawmaker was one she deeply regretted. GOP Rep. Nancy Mace (R., S.C.) also said she was a yes.\par \par \uc2 "If gay couples want to be as happily or miserably married as straight couples, more power to them," Ms. Mace said.\par \par \uc2 Democrats continued to say that the Republican Party is extreme.\par \par \uc2 "It's 2022, and 157 House Republicans just voted against marriage equality," {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PeteButtigieg/status/1549524918928285696?s=20&t=3R2fYCtrGlpBzE09KPsNRg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg, }}}\uc2  who is gay.\par \par \uc2 Both interracial marriage and same-sex marriage were established as rights through Supreme Court rulings. Democrats now say they worry those rights could be next to fall after the Supreme Court last month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruled Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 . Last week, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-set-to-vote-on-bills-protecting-abortion-access-11657896570"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the House passed two bills }}}\uc2  to shore up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights but neither bill currently has a path forward in the Senate. One of the bills, on codifying the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through fetal viability, drew no GOP support; the other, on guaranteeing women's right to cross state lines for the procedure, drew three GOP votes.\par \par \uc2 Unlike {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-v-wade-overturned-supreme-court-11656110804"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , which remains divisive politically, expanded marriage and sexual rights have grown broadly popular. Gallup public-opinion polls show interracial marriage and access to contraceptives {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/354638/approval-interracial-marriage-new-high.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 backed by more than 9 in 10 Americans }}}\uc2 , and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/393197/same-sex-marriage-support-inches-new-high.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 same-sex marriage }}}\uc2  supported by more than two-thirds of the country.\par \par \uc2 The Respect for Marriage Act, if passed, would codify into law the ability for same-sex and interracial couples to get married and require states to recognize the marriage. It would also repeal the 1996 Defense of Marriage Act, which defines marriage as between a man and a woman. That law isn't enforced following Supreme Court decisions but remains on the books.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say such laws are needed after the court's ruling in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case. While most of the justices in the majority focused on what they described as an incorrect precedent that established that right in Roe v. Wade in 1973, one justice went further. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote in his opinion concurring with the majority that if the legal underpinnings of Roe v. Wade were wrong, then so were the underpinnings of other rights that the court recognized in recent decades. They include the right to use contraception, the right to sexual activity by persons of the same sex and, in 2015, the right to same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 House Democrats protesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision were arrested Tuesday afternoon in front of the Supreme Court after staging a sit-in that blocked the road. The protesters included Reps. \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2  of New York and \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , another member of the court's conservative majority, wrote a concurring opinion that said overruling the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "does not threaten or cast doubt on those precedents," referring to cases that recognized the constitutional right of interracial and same-sex couples to marry and people to access birth control.\par \par \uc2 Three liberal justices, in dissent, voted to uphold Roe v. Wade, which in 1973 recognized women's constitutional right to end an unwanted pregnancy before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) declined to commit to bringing the bills to a vote during a press conference Tuesday. Either bill would need the support of at least 10 Republicans for passage.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Dick Durbin (D., Ill.), the Senate Democratic whip, said marriage protections and access to contraception were Democratic priorities, but "we have more priorities than we have time." Democrats are in a sprint to finish a prescription-drug pricing bill as well as a package subsidizing semiconductor production before they go home for the August recess.\par \par \uc2 So far, just one Republican senator has signed on to the marriage legislation: Sen. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine.\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans have taken a different approach.\par \par \uc2 Sen. Ted Cruz (R., Texas), in a podcast posted over the weekend, said Obergefell v. Hodges, the case that extended to same-sex couples the same marriage rights enjoyed by opposite-sex couples, "was clearly wrong when it was decided." He said the Supreme Court was overreaching because it should be up to states to decide.\par \par \uc2 In last month's decision ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, all six Republican-appointed justices voted to allow states to ban abortions before viability. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , alone, would have left for another case whether to eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights altogether.\par \par \uc2 The decision ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, called into question the legal underpinnings of other decisions involving privacy rights, and in its next term the Supreme Court is scheduled to consider whether a website designer has a First Amendment right to refuse service to same-sex couples despite state laws requiring equal treatment. No case directly challenging same-sex or interracial couples' right to marry or of individuals to obtain contraceptives currently is in the court's pipeline.\par \par \uc2 Jan Wolfe, Jess Bravin and Siobhan Hughes contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins+1@wsj.com."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins+1@wsj.com. }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-to-vote-on-protections-for-same-sex-and-interracial-marriage-11658239485"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House Passes Bill to Protect Same-Sex and Interracial Marriages  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220719ei7j002s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
