\par\par \uc2 Wonder Land\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 'National Emergency' and the Democrats' Apocalyptic Politics; They insist Biden go into 'Beast Mode' on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  and climate.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Henninger \par \uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 July 2022\par 22:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For Democrats, politics has become showtime.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, 17 House Democrats\u8212\'20\'14including \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  and \uc2 Rashida Tlaib\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14staged a made-for-\uc2 Instagram\uc2  arrest outside the Supreme Court. Then the committee investigating the invasion of the Capitol by Trump supporters went prime time Thursday evening. The committee has created its own logo\u8212\'20\'14"January 6th"\u8212\'20\'14which looms on a gray screen above the committee.\par \par \uc2 Let no one doubt it: Jan. 6, 2021, was an event whose notoriety makes political ramifications inevitable. Most likely the committee's hearings will force rethinking by the Republican Party and the broader voting public about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's future.\par \par \uc2 But sitting in not-so-distant memory is an event of similar political consequence: the summer of 2020, with the past few weeks showing how that remarkable time has transformed the politics of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 After \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 's death that May in Minneapolis\u8212\'20\'14for which Derek Chauvin was later convicted of murder\u8212\'20\'14protests broke out in hundreds of U.S. cities, some accompanied by violence and store lootings.\par \par \uc2 The Floyd protests appear to have permanently altered the Democratic Party's basic political model, moving it away from traditional legislative politics and toward the uncompromising, often theatrical, strategies of activists in the streets.\par \par \uc2 The news has been dominated lately by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/politics-abortion-ocasio-cortez-debate-roe-dobbs-decision-supreme-court-life-11656535292"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats raging}}}\uc2  about the Supreme Court's Dobbs decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Sen. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 's opposition to their climate-control agenda. Many are disgusted that President Biden, whom they lump with traditionalist politicians, won't "fight" for them and "do something" to reverse Dobbs and save the planet.\par \par \uc2 One unavoidable detail must be mentioned about American politics from the aftermath of 2020. Aside from Mr. Trump's complaints about a stolen election, something of real consequence occurred: the Jan. 5, 2021, runoff elections for two Georgia seats in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Amid Mr. Trump's denunciations of the state's Republican leadership, Democrats won both seats, producing a 50-50 Senate. That standoff is the result of millions of votes cast by Americans. Time was that such a democratic voter verdict, however frustrating, would get bipartisan respect. But not in what progressives call "our democracy."\par \par \uc2 The central preoccupation of American politics for the succeeding 18 months has been Mr. Manchin's denial of the vote Democrats need to enact their spending and climate policies. Arizona Democratic Sen. \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  also has opposed much of the party's agenda. But Mr. Manchin has inspired the melodrama and party rage.\par \par \uc2 In what political galaxy would anyone expect a senator from West Virginia, a state synonymous with coal mining, to vote for his party's intention to terminate fossil-fuel production? Yet last week the party and its climate allies went berserk when Mr. Manchin sank their agenda for the umpteenth time.\par \par \uc2 My intention is not to describe paint drying in the Senate but to draw attention to a party that since May 2020 has gradually disconnected from normal political processes.\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Manchin withdrew his support for the climate legislation, citing opposition to new taxes and inflation, former Obama White House counselor \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  wrote the senator had "doomed humanity." Rhode Island's Democratic Sen. Sheldon Whitehouse took to\u8212\'20\'14where else?\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 Twitter\uc2  to liberate himself from Congress: "Free at last. Let's roll. Do it all and start it now,'' Mr. Whitehouse tweeted. "With legislative climate options now closed, it's now time for executive Beast Mode.''\par \par \uc2 Under a new unchecked and unbalanced constitutional system called executive Beast Mode, Mr. Biden would declare "national emergencies" on both \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and climate, presumably leading to an array of sweeping presidential enactments accomplished by Mr. Biden repeatedly producing his signature.\par \par \uc2 The post-2020 Democratic Party's theory of politics appears to be: The system no longer works, so blow up the system by issuing presidential executive orders on climate, education, guns and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; ending the legislative filibuster; packing the Supreme Court; suppressing dissent as "misinformation"; and if necessary, redefining reality, such as the "1619 Project," which rewrote the country's history.\par \par \uc2 Clich\u233\'00\'E9s in politics exist because they're true. Don't promise what you can't deliver. Politics is the art of the possible. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  knew these realities before he was out of sixth grade in Brooklyn. The progressive coterie to which the Senate majority leader pays obeisance now doesn't know any of this because teachers from grade school through university have tutored them in the nonnegotiable demand.\par \par \uc2 These tactics do produce publicity\u8212\'20\'14such as Rep. Ocasio-Cortez's faux handcuffed pose Tuesday\u8212\'20\'14but a problem remains: It isn't sustainable. An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/07/16/democrats-young-voters-abortion-guns-midterms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 article}}}\uc2  in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/07/16/democrats-young-voters-abortion-guns-midterms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington Post}}}\uc2  this week reports that college-age Democrats are pushing away from politics, including this November's elections, done in by the endless, insistent activist wheel-spinning.\par \par \uc2 That is the familiar result of displacing the unavoidably incremental progress of real politics with an antipolitics\u8212\'20\'14street demos, constant moral denunciations, or threats of ostracism from the group for imagined offenses. Whatever else, keeping the apocalypse going is exhausting.\par \par \uc2 People are reordering their post-pandemic lives. Losing one's mind every day over Jan. 6, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  may be what professional Democrats do for a living now. The rest of the country is still looking for that promised, but undelivered, return to normal.\par \par \uc2 Write henninger@wsj.com.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-democrats-apocalypses-emergency-joe-biden-executive-order-climate-abortion-guns-beast-mode-manchin-11658348301"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 'National Emergency' and the Democrats' Apocalyptic Politics}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220720ei7k007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
