\par\par \uc2 Business\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Small Businesses in Texas Plot Next Moves as  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Shifts; Some entrepreneurs say they are weighing expanding healthcare benefits against legal and financial risks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ruth Simon | Photographs by Carter Johnston for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,662 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2022\par 12:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Brian Kropp\uc2  is vice president of research at \uc2 Gartner\uc2  . An earlier version of this article incorrectly spelled his last name as Kopp. (Corrected on July 17)\par \par \uc2 After a draft of the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision to overturn Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  became public, Max Hoberman spoke to attorneys for the independent videogame company he runs about covering travel costs for employees if they needed to leave Texas to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The lawyers warned of legal risks, so Mr. Hoberman made another offer: The company, Austin-based Certain Affinity Inc., would help some employees move out of the state for good.\par \par \uc2 Ron Horne, CEO of Pennco Inc., a Houston-area manufacturer of municipal water treatment chemicals, has made a different decision: He says he is leaving it to employees to cover the cost of treatments\u8212\'20\'14including abortions\u8212\'20\'14that don't already fall under the company's health insurance plan. "If you couldn't get a dental treatment in Texas, I doubt that I would pay for you to get it somewhere else," he said.\par \par \uc2 Small businesses based in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-nearly-century-old-abortion-ban-in-effect-for-now-states-high-court-says-11656780694"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states like Texas  }}}\uc2  where officials are implementing bans on most or nearly all abortions face choices. Given the costs and insurance policies, many smaller companies especially find it a challenge to balance expenses, legal risks and employees with different views on a divisive issue.\par \par \uc2 Some say failing to strike the right balance will put them at a significant disadvantage in recruiting and retaining talent. Some companies have already changed their policies; others don't plan to make any changes.\par \par \uc2 Many companies of all sizes have avoided discussing the issue publicly. Nationwide, about half of medium-size and large companies say they plan to offer some assistance to employees in states that restrict or prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with covering travel expenses the most common option, said \uc2 Brian Kropp\uc2  , vice president of research at \uc2 Gartner\uc2  , a consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 Many of the companies that haven't taken action have told him they are worried about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-roe-v-wade-ruling-companies-reimbursing-medical-and-travel-expenses-for-abortion-risk-blowback-11656109337"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the legal risk  }}}\uc2  or haven't yet decided on their policy, Mr. Kropp said. Some have indicated that they don't want to get involved in political issues, that their CEO opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or that they aren't sure how to provide benefits across their entire workforce.\par \par \uc2 Texas last year banned most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, and allows citizens to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and those who assist them. Since the Supreme Court removed federal constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a ruling in late June, Texas' leaders have moved to enforce laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy. Abortions are now largely unavailable in about a dozen states, either because of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new bans  }}}\uc2  or legal uncertainty. Another half-dozen or more states could ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming days and weeks.\par \par \uc2 "The court is really putting us in a challenging position," said Patrick Curry, CEO of FarBridge Inc., an Austin-based videogame studio with 20 employees, most in Texas. "This issue touches the economic challenges, the medical challenges," he said. "It puts us in a legal quandary. We are having to look at it from all those perspectives, but also from what is the right thing to do."\par \par \uc2 Some companies, such as Dallas-based \uc2 Match Group Inc\uc2  ., began outlining their response last year. The company's then-CEO, \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2  , provided initial financing for a fund, administered by Planned Parenthood of Los Angeles, to assist Texas-based employees or their dependents with travel and related costs associated with getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state.\par \par \uc2 Joanna Rice, head of corporate social responsibility for Match, said she now spends hours every day discussing options with other companies. "Small, midsize, big firms, everyone is trying to figure out what can we do, what do we have to do, what are we able to do," said Ms. Rice. Match, the operator of an online dating site, has about 2,500 employees, including roughly 400 in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Texas has about three million small businesses and they employ about half of the state's private-sector workers, according to U.S. census data. Small businesses typically offer fully insured health plans, which are regulated at the state level. Texas is one of 11 states that bar state-regulated health plans from covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Midsize and large companies generally operate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/on-abortion-coverage-company-health-plans-diverge-11656700014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-funded health plans  }}}\uc2 , in which employers pay workers' health claims with their own money. Those plans are regulated at the federal level, and aren't subject to the state restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Just under half of firms with three to nine workers offer health coverage to their workers, compared with 65% of firms with 10 to 24 workers, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  . Ninety-nine percent of large firms offer health benefits to at least some of their workers, according to Kaiser data.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Huffman, the owner of Nourish Juice & Smoothie Bar, with two locations in Houston, offers health insurance to full-time workers and discounted benefits to part-timers. The company will honor a worker's request for extended time off for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or other reproductive care, as it has with other medical needs, but doesn't have the funds to cover paid leaves, said Ms. Huffman, who has roughly 20 employees.\par \par \uc2 "We just don't have the resources," said Ms. Huffman, who is pregnant. "I am actually uninsured."\par \par \uc2 Certain Affinity, the Austin videogame developer, is offering to relocate employees if the law in their state "restricts access to what a majority of medical experts consider essential care." Mr. Hoberman, the company's founder and CEO, said he crafted the language of his offer so that it addressed not only reproductive care, but also transgender treatments and other medical services. At least two employees have expressed interest in using the relocation benefit, he said.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How should small businesses approach the overturning of Roe v. Wade? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hoberman said that the company hasn't yet created a mechanism for determining what falls under the policy and may have to bring in an outside expert to help. The company, which has about 230 employees, mostly in Texas, had been advised against offering to cover travel expenses in part because of the chance the business could become a target for lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 "Companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2  , \uc2 Meta\uc2  , their leaders have the benefit of not being based here," he said. "We are a lot more vulnerable."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hoberman said the shifting policies are making it more difficult for Certain Affinity, which has offices in Austin and Toronto and remote workers in a half-dozen states and British Columbia, to find job candidates willing to relocate to Texas.\par \par \uc2 "We've had a number of really strong candidates that have applied and they have made it absolutely clear there is no way they will relocate to a state with this policy," he said. "It is happening frequently."\par \par \uc2 Managing competing views on politically charged issues can be particularly challenging for small companies. "When there is a divide between 45 people, it's a lot more obvious," said Sujan Patel, founder and CEO of Mailshake, an Austin-based sales software company with employees across the globe and on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court ruling, Mr. Patel polled employees on whether they knew about Roe v. Wade. He also asked whether they would feel uncomfortable if the company took steps to support female employees, without specifically mentioning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Mr. Patel then followed up individually with female employees, who make up 56% of Mailshake's workforce, letting them know the company would cover travel costs, no questions asked.\par \par \uc2 "I'd rather do this quietly, with one-on-one conversations, than open it up and divide the company," said Mr. Patel. "My wife and I had to go through an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  process. This hits home."\par \par \uc2 Kickstand Communications, an Austin-based digital marketing company with about 50 employees, this summer expanded a family-planning grant program to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. It's up to employees to decide how much\u8212\'20\'14or how little\u8212\'20\'14they want to share about how they plan to use the benefit, said Kickstand co-owner Molly George.\par \par \uc2 "We have a lot more autonomy to make these decisions and execute quickly as well," Ms. George said of being a small business. "The other side of it is we are not some well-funded organization sitting on a giant pile of cash. I don't know which side of the table is the best one to be sitting on."\par \par \uc2 QuestionPro Inc.\uc2  , which operates an online platform for market research and surveys, has told employees to simply submit a report for their travel expenses if they need to travel out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so the company can cover the cost even if it isn't covered by QuestionPro's health plan. The company hasn't yet figured out the best way to protect employee privacy.\par \par \uc2 "Submit it as a reimbursement request and we will approve it," said CEO and founder \uc2 Vivek Bhaskaran\uc2  , who has about 250 employees, including 25 in Texas. "We have a lot of faith in our employees," he added. "We are so small that we trust each other."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bhaskaran moved QuestionPro to Austin from San Francisco in 2019 because, he said, the high cost of living in the Bay area made it difficult to recruit and retain employees. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , never before a topic of discussion, comes up in nearly every recruiting conversation. He worries that it is making it more difficult to attract new employees\u8212\'20\'14or will in the future.\par \par \uc2 Laura Kusisto contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ruth Simon at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ruth.simon@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruth.simon@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/small-businesses-in-texas-plot-next-moves-as-abortion-law-shifts-11658061310"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Small Businesses in Texas Plot Next Moves as \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Shifts  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220717ei7h000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
