\par\par \uc2 Style & Substance\par \uc2 Style and Substance\par \b \uc2 Vol. 35, No. 6: Anonymity\b0\par\par\uc2 1,737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 July 2022\par 21:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 First-name-only should be rare\par \par \uc2 Our coverage of Ukraine and Afghanistan has included compelling tales of people who, out of fear of harm, have made a justifiable request for anonymity. Some are willing to be identified by one name only, a code name or a nickname.\par \par \uc2 The Standards team's guidance on partial anonymity applies to reporting on any topic anywhere: We avoid identifying sources in this way unless they meet two tests in extreme cases\u8212\'20\'14a legitimate reason to fear serious, physical harm and a significant role in the article, video or podcast.\par \par \uc2 Does a one-name identifier help our audience follow the story we are telling? For a piece that mentions and quotes a person several times, the answer is often yes; it might read better with a name rather than a descriptor such as the Afghan spy. In references to other anonymous sources\u8212\'20\'14those who play a lesser role, are mentioned only in passing, or aren't quoted\u8212\'20\'14the answer is usually no. Use a descriptor and no name, even when a person is willing to be called by only one name.\par \par \uc2 When granting partial anonymity in these rare cases, we should be clear with our audience. Include a sentence such as The Wall Street Journal agreed to use only her first name. Otherwise, a reader might think that we are using pseudonyms, which we don't. We generally don't need to say further why we are publishing only one name. The reason is almost always to protect in some way the person who is speaking to us, or that person's family, and doesn't need to be stated explicitly. If the context described in the article doesn't make the reason obvious, an exception can be considered. Consult with senior editors or the Standards team\u8212\'20\'14which, it should be noted, retains the right to ask for sourcing details in every case.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade\par \par \uc2 The recent Supreme Court decision means we need to take added care to ensure we are being accurate and precise when referring to the "right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Here is an explanation, via The Wall Street Journal's Standards editors, that has been added to the stylebook. The second paragraph here was adapted from the existing entry in the stylebook, which will remain:\par \par \uc2 abortion\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision in June 2022, there is no longer a federal constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so be precise in using terms to describe the current legal framework that varies by state in the U.S. Some states now or soon will effectively ban abortions, some will restrict them, and some will continue to offer legal access. Some have a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions. For example, it isn't accurate to talk broadly in our own voice in news articles about "protecting" \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights now that a federal constitutional right no longer exists. Such phrases are acceptable in people's quotations or job titles. It is more accurate to refer to a person who, or position that, supports or advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or for legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If referring to a constitutional right in a state or states, then it is appropriate to refer to the protection of those rights.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights and antiabortion are the preferred adjectives when describing advocates and campaigners on opposite sides of the issue. In U.S. states or countries that outlaw all or most abortions, a campaign is more accurately called a legalized-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. Avoid "pro-life" and "pro-choice," except when quoting someone. Late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in pregnancy are more-neutral terms for what opponents of the practice call "partial-birth abortions."\par \par \uc2 Overdone 'whom'\par \par \uc2 Perhaps out of fear of the grammar police, writers tend to use whom when they have a doubt about the who/whom choice. But often, who was the right call.\par \par \uc2 Two recent examples:\par \par \uc2 * \u8230\'20\'26says Heather Kirkwood, a lawyer in Seattle who helps defend clients, typically pro bono, whom she believes are wrongly accused of AHT [abusive head trauma].\par \par \uc2 * \u8230\'20\'26Abu Hamzah al Yemeni, whom the U.S. Central Command said was a senior leader of the Hurras al-Din organization.\par \par \uc2 In both cases, who was the right call, not whom. That is because in these cases, who refers to the subject. Ignore such distracting phrases as "she believes" in making this decision. The grammar trick is to stick with who if the sentence can be turned around to a question and the word replaced with he, she or they, rather than a him, her or them.\par \par \uc2 So, for the first example: Who is wrongly accused? They are, not them. So it's a who. (On the other hand, Hemingway fans, for whom does the bell toll? For them, of course. So that's why it's a whom.)\par \par \uc2 Rulings & reminders\par \par \uc2 * The new U.S. treasurer will be Lynn Malerba even though her formal name is Marilynn. She does business under the first name Lynn, so we recognize that, as we have for other politicians and officials including Jimmy (Carter) and Bill (Clinton).\par \par \uc2 * Amex, not \uc2 AmEx\uc2 , is how \uc2 American Express Co\uc2 . now capitalizes its abbreviation. (In the past, we worried about mixing up the abbreviations for \uc2 American Express\uc2 , then called \uc2 AmEx\uc2 , and the old \uc2 American Stock Exchange\uc2 , or Amex, but that isn't a worry now.)\par \par \uc2 * We sometimes slip up and use both past and present tenses on verbs within the same article. (Unless, of course, it is intentional to discuss present and past events.)\par \par \uc2 * Himars, not all-caps HIMARS, is how we capitalize the rocket-launch system that the U.S. supplied to Ukraine. It stands for high mobility artillery rocket system. Because it is pronounced (as HIGH-mars) and is more than four letters, it is an example of when we don't use all caps even if the entity that owns the name does it that way.\par \par \uc2 * We now spell terracotta as one word, an exception to Webster's 5th, for the clay or its earthy-tone color, rather than terra cotta or terra-cotta.\par \par \uc2 * Web3, with a capital W, is how we will spell the term for the idea of a new stage of the internet tied to blockchain technology.\par \par \uc2 * Immigrate and emigrate\u8230\'20\'26. We sometimes mix them up in describing the movement of migrants. As our stylebook explains, one who leaves a country emigrates from it. One who comes into a country immigrates. The same holds for emigrant and immigrant. Don't use these terms for people who move within the same country; call them migrants. Speaking of which, the reason that immigration is a political issue isn't because of migrants but because of migrants who cross illegally (sometimes then seeking asylum).\par \par \uc2 Incentivize. Ugh.\par \par \uc2 We must say it again: Incentivize is hackneyed, best left to business jargon.\par \par \uc2 Examples, as noticed by reader David E. Gold, that continue to drag down our prose:\par \par \uc2 * They [SPAC investors] are incentivized to withdraw when share prices are low\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 * Republicans pressed her on further incentivizing fossil fuels in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 * \u8230\'20\'26part of a controversial strategy to disincentivize illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 * PGA Tour commissioner \uc2 Jay Monahan\uc2  plans to incentivize players to stay on the tour.\par \par \uc2 There are plenty of conventional, and stronger, verbs that could be used instead: motivated to/encouraging/to discourage/to persuade.\par \par \uc2 Heads above the rest\par \par \uc2 * "Consumers Are Giving Inflation the Bird\u8212\'20\'14With a Whole Chicken," \u8203\'20\'0B a group effort including Jennifer Levitz and Mitch Pacelle.\par \par \uc2 * "A Survival Guide for Dealing With a Bad Boss / Life is too short to work for jerks. Here's what to do when your boss is a micromanager, bully or completely MIA," another group effort, and particularly noteworthy for the fun deck, by Rachel Feintzeig, Vanessa Fuhrmans and Nikki Waller.\par \par \uc2 Heads that make you go 'hmmm'\par \par \uc2 * Do we overuse "How" headlines? And how! They used to be a fun way to engage the reader, and we've all done them, but they are commonplace now. If the headline works just as well without the How, try to go without it. How a CEO Turned Her Worst Disaster Into Success can be as strong without the word: A CEO Turned Her Worst Disaster Into Success.\par \par \uc2 Notifications above the rest\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the top mobile push alerts, both on WSJ's native app and via Apple News. Our aim is to highlight the storytelling our editors do on locked screens, typically in 140 characters or fewer.\par \par \uc2 * The Supreme Court has overruled Roe v. Wade. The decision eliminates the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that had stood for half a century.\par \par \uc2 * She began to sob at the dinner table. She'd just returned from her daily Covid test, the only time her family could leave their 10th-floor apartment.\par \par \uc2 * One supermarket tried to completely do away with plastic. People stealing steak is where it started going wrong.\par \par \uc2 * America's most expensive homes used to get snapped up in days, but not anymore. The bubble is about to burst in one hot city.\par \par \uc2 * In the widest expansion of gun rights in a decade, the Supreme Court struck down a New York law on concealed weapons permits\par \par \uc2 * These Realtors weren't prepared for what they discovered when they opened the closet\par \par \uc2 Quiz (find the flubs)\par \par \uc2 * A woman called a \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  hotline in 2020 to report allegedly inappropriate and disrespectful conduct by Mr. Chin toward women.\par \par \uc2 * In her mid-30s, breast cancer disrupted what looked like steady progress toward a chief executive appointment.\par \par \uc2 * If you are looking to fill your laser tag party, you could ask each of your close friends to invite another close friend of their own.\par \par \uc2 Answers\par \par \uc2 * Some would argue that if the conduct was only allegedly inappropriate, it's not being reported, only alleged. In any case, it's tighter to say she called the hotline to allege inappropriate and disrespectful conduct.\par \par \uc2 * A dangler. The cancer wasn't someone in her mid-30s.\par \par \uc2 * A close friend of his or her own. Or simply a close friend. Or keep the whole thing plural: \u8230\'20\'26ask your close friends to invite close friends of their own. Our stylebook says the "singular they" (or their) still isn't good grammar except in specific reference to someone who uses gender-neutral pronouns.\par \par \uc2 Send questions or comments to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:william.power@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 William Power}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.hicks@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer Hicks}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ISSN 1054-7041\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/vol-35-no-6-anonymity-11657921267"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vol. 35, No. 6: Anonymity}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220715ei7f0073l\par 