\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b \uc2 U.S. Urges Pharmacists to Fill Prescriptions for Drugs That Can Be Used in Abortions; New federal health guidelines cite possible antidiscrimination violation if patient is refused drugs used for medication  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  and other medical conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Wilde Mathews and Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 1,568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 July 2022\par 21:39 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is warning pharmacists that refusing to dispense medications used in abortions might violate federal protections against discrimination, in its latest move aimed at limiting the impact of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state antiabortion laws }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Department of Health and Human Services' Office for Civil Rights released guidelines on Wednesday that spell out examples when a pharmacist's refusal to dispense a drug to a patient might be a violation of federal antidiscrimination law.\par \par \uc2 Among the situations are cases involving patients seeking drug treatment after a miscarriage and for an ectopic pregnancy, in which a fertilized egg implants outside the uterus.\par \par \uc2 Yet the guidelines stop short of asserting that federal antidiscrimination law broadly protects patients' access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which some state laws ban or restrict. Instead, the document affirms \uc2 HHS\uc2 's role in protecting access to reproductive and other healthcare free from discrimination.\par \par \uc2 The guidance also doesn't focus on another issue that has gained fresh importance since the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision overturning Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2 : whether pharmacists and other healthcare providers can decline to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services because of their religious or moral beliefs.\par \par \uc2 The guidelines aim to address the challenge pharmacies are facing trying to abide by state laws restricting abortions while dispensing drugs like methotrexate and misoprostol, which can be used to end pregnancies but are prescribed for other uses such as treating rheumatoid arthritis.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Charles E. Brown, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Austin, Texas, who is a district chairman for the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , said some pharmacists in Texas have sent letters to doctors saying they won't fill prescriptions for methotrexate even for a legal non-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  use.\par \par \uc2 The pharmacists are concerned, Dr. Brown said, of running afoul of the felony offense under the law, even though it allows the drug's use in other instances.\par \par \uc2 "Why would I think that a police officer or an assistant D.A. is going to understand the subtlety?" he said. "And if they don't understand, they could just arrest me because somebody says, 'I was pregnant and somebody gave me this drug.'"\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Community Pharmacists Association\uc2 , which represents independent pharmacies, said that in the wake of the federal guidelines' release states should issue clear guidelines on how pharmacists are to proceed when state and federal laws are in conflict.\par \par \uc2 "It is highly unfair for state and federal governments to threaten aggressive action against pharmacists who are just trying to serve their patients within new legal boundaries that are still taking shape," said B. Douglas Hoey, who is a pharmacist and the group's chief executive.\par \par \uc2 Spokespeople for \uc2 CVS Health Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Walgreens Boots Alliance Inc\uc2 . said the companies are reviewing the guidelines and declined further comment on them. The biggest pharmacy chains said before the guidelines were released that they are working to ensure access to any medications that can be legally dispensed.\par \par \uc2 CVS said it has begun instructing pharmacists in certain states, before filling prescriptions for methotrexate or misoprostol, to verify that the drugs are not intended to terminate a pregnancy. The chain also is encouraging providers to include their diagnosis on prescriptions to ensure ready access, a CVS spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  said they also have policies allowing pharmacists to decline to fill a prescription for moral or religious reasons as long as they can provide the customer with an alternate means of getting the drug, either by having another pharmacist fill the script or referring the patient to a nearby location.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the attorney general's office in Oklahoma said officials are reviewing the guidelines but they "stand ready to challenge the Biden administration's overreach targeting the unborn." \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in the state except when the mother's life is in danger, or in cases of rape of incest.\par \par \uc2 Several other state attorneys general didn't immediately respond to requests for comment or declined.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has been under heavy pressure from some Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights groups to take action to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision last month overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Last week, President Biden signed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-sign-order-stepping-up-efforts-to-protect-access-to-reproductive-care-11657278000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an executive order }}}\uc2  directing agencies to take steps to protect access to abortions and the privacy of patients seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 Yet legal experts said the administration has only limited power to fill the void left by the court's decision, which ended constitutional protection for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allowed states to pass laws banning or restricting it.\par \par \uc2 "The executive branch doesn't have the power to establish new law," said \uc2 Alina Salganicoff\uc2 , director of women's health policy at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 . "The court has said it is up to the states, the states have this right."\par \par \uc2 "We are committed to ensuring that everyone can access health care, free of discrimination," \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  said. "This includes access to prescription medications for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other types of care."\par \par \uc2 In the pharmacy context, patient advocates fear drugs like methotrexate and misoprostol used in medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be denied to people who are prescribed the same pills for other conditions, or to women who need them for reasons other than to end a viable pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some pharmacists have expressed concerns, however, about running afoul of state antiabortion laws that are in some cases old and broadly written.\par \par \uc2 "We must be conservative in our interpretation to ensure we continue to operate legally and protect our pharmacists from criminal penalties" until the state's pharmacy board provides clear guidance, said Samantha Tally, pharmacist in charge at Peoples RX pharmacy in Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 The pharmacy in December sent a notice to providers that it would no longer dispense methotrexate for the purpose of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including to treat an ectopic pregnancy. Ms. Tally said industry guidance directs that only physicians can dispense the drug. "Peoples does not have the resources to engage separate counsel to help us interpret new regulations and guidelines."\par \par \uc2 Methotrexate is often used to treat rheumatoid arthritis, cancer and other chronic conditions. It is one of the most affordable drugs available to treat rheumatoid arthritis, as well as the one least likely to cause harsh side effects. The drug also may be used to treat an ectopic pregnancy, which can be a life-threatening condition.\par \par \uc2 Misoprostol can be prescribed to women to prepare the cervix for delivery, to induce labor or to manage postpartum hemorrhaging. It also can reduce the likelihood of ulcer complications such as bleeding.\par \par \uc2 Two medications\u8212\'20\'14mifepristone and misoprostol\u8212\'20\'14are typically used in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-is-an-abortion-pill-leaked-supreme-court-decision-11651775689"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  regimen }}}\uc2 . About half of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures in the U.S. are carried out through the pill-based procedure known as medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 The new federal pharmacy guidelines cite a section of the Affordable Care Act and a 1973 law, saying they forbid discrimination on the basis of sex or disability. The guidelines say that discrimination against pregnant people is a form of sex discrimination, and pharmacies can't discriminate against their customers in providing medications, advice or other services.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What should be done to ensure the Dobbs decision doesn't affect access to reproductive healthcare beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 HHS\uc2  official said that state laws could violate federal nondiscrimination laws in their language or their implementation, but "it is always a case-by-case determination." Asked whether pharmacists could be caught between federal and state laws, the official said that the guidance "makes clear that federal law obligations are controlling if there is a conflict with state laws."\par \par \uc2 The guidelines include a case study about a patient who has an early miscarriage and is prescribed mifepristone and misoprostol. If a pharmacy refuses to fill the prescription because the medications could also be used to terminate a pregnancy, it "may be discriminating on the basis of sex," the document says.\par \par \uc2 In another example cited by the guidelines, a patient has a fertilized egg growing in a fallopian tube, and is prescribed methotrexate to halt the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "If a pharmacy refuses to fill the prescription because it will halt the growing of cells and end the pregnancy, it may be discriminating on the basis of sex," the guidelines said.\par \par \uc2 Other examples involve patients with other conditions, such as severe stomach ulcers and rheumatoid arthritis, and the guidance says that refusing to fill their prescriptions might violate antidiscrimination protections for people with disabilities.\par \par \uc2 The guidelines said \uc2 HHS\uc2  is responsible for protecting the rights of women and pregnant people to access care, which includes "their ability to access \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, including prescription medication from their pharmacy, free from discrimination."\par \par \uc2 Regarding pharmacists who decline to services based on their religious or moral beliefs, the document said federal law that protects healthcare personnel from employment-based discrimination tied to such situations will be applied on a case-by-case basis.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anna Wilde Mathews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:anna.mathews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anna.mathews@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Sharon Terlep at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:sharon.terlep@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sharon.terlep@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-urges-pharmacists-to-fill-prescriptions-for-drugs-that-can-be-used-in-abortions-11657730714"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 U.S. Urges Pharmacists to Fill Prescriptions for Drugs That Can Be Used in Abortions }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220713ei7d004mp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
