\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b \uc2 Doctors Struggle With State  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Restrictions at Odds With Federal Law; Law requiring doctors to provide emergency treatment to stabilize certain patients may necessitate performing an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  in some cases\b0\par\par\uc2 By Melanie Evans \par \uc2 1,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 July 2022\par 02:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Under the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, hospitals and physicians face penalties of up to about $120,000 per violation. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said the fines are around $104,000 per violation for hospitals and up to $50,000 per violation for physicians. (Corrected on July 12)\par \par \uc2 Doctors and hospitals are rushing to reconcile laws in their states barring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a federal law that may require the procedure as part of emergency treatment.\par \par \uc2 Since the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned }}}\uc2  Roe v. Wade, laws have taken effect, or soon will, in several {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states that prohibit abortions }}}\uc2  except when necessary to save a pregnant woman's life. Yet under federal law, doctors and hospitals may need to perform the procedure in other medical emergencies, such as for women at risk of kidney failure from an infection.\par \par \uc2 The federal law, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act, doesn't require abortions. Rather, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cms.gov/Regulations-and-Guidance/Legislation/EMTALA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it directs hospital emergency departments }}}\uc2  to provide care necessary to stabilize certain critical patients regardless of ability to pay. That treatment can sometimes involve abortions barred by some state laws, according to lawyers and doctors.\par \par \uc2 To resolve the conflicts, some hospitals have directed teams to review the laws and develop guidelines, while doctors sound out lawyers about what they can do and when.\par \par \uc2 Norton Healthcare system in Kentucky, which owns Louisville's largest hospital, has been providing regular updates to staff and trying to answer questions that arise, a spokeswoman said. BHSH System in Michigan said it decided to keep providing medically necessary abortions as it waits for the courts to clarify discrepancies.\par \par \uc2 "It's really creating a lot of chaos and confusion," said Jen Villavicencio, an official with the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , a professional association that is trying to help doctors sort through the laws. Dr. Villavicencio practices as an obstetrician-gynecologist in Maryland, Virginia and Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 The mismatch leaves hospitals to decide {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/uncertainty-over-abortion-access-grows-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656357143"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 how much legal risk they are willing to face }}}\uc2  by choosing to follow one law over another, doctors and lawyers said.\par \par \uc2 Physicians, meantime, could be left to make difficult decisions about whether to delay emergency treatment until a woman's condition worsens enough to satisfy state or federal law requirements for performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the doctors and lawyers said.\par \par \uc2 "How close to death do you have to be for us to save your life?" said \uc2 Rebekah Gee\uc2 , an OB-GYN in New Orleans who headed Louisiana's health department under a Democratic governor. Dr. Gee has filed a brief in a Louisiana state court supporting a lawsuit to block the state's trigger laws banning abortions.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, which enforces the emergency-treatment law, said in a notice to clinicians last September that its law pre-empts conflicting state laws. The Biden administration issued an executive order Friday pledging to take steps, possibly including updates to clarify doctor responsibilities, to ensure patients have access to the emergency care afforded under the law.\par \par \uc2 At issue is the treatment of pregnant women experiencing conditions that send them to emergency rooms, such as severe bleeding in patients whose cervices have dilated, an ectopic pregnancy that occurs when a fertilized egg implants outside the uterus, or cases in which the protective sac around the fetus ruptures extremely early.\par \par \uc2 If left untreated, the conditions could lead to life-threatening infections or hemorrhaging, according to doctors.\par \par \uc2 Hemorrhage and infection are two of the top five causes of pregnancy-related deaths, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 . For every 100,000 births in the U.S., about 24 women will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/report-section/racial-disparities-in-maternal-and-infant-health-an-overview-issue-brief/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 die of pregnancy-related complications }}}\uc2 , CDC data show. The figures are higher for Black, Native American and Alaska Native women.\par \par \uc2 The Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act was enacted in 1986 to ensure that people would receive emergency treatment even if they can't pay for it. Under the law, hospitals are required to stabilize critical patients, including preventing serious impairment of bodily function and organ dysfunction, in addition to providing lifesaving treatment.\par \par \uc2 Doctors and hospitals can be fined up to around $120,000 per violation.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes hospitals must perform abortions to provide the emergency care mandated by the law, lawyers and federal health officials said. BHSH System said it performed about 60 therapeutic, medically necessary abortions across its 22 Michigan hospitals last year.\par \par \uc2 The system briefly changed its policy after the Supreme Court's decision last month\u8212\'20\'14to comply with Michigan's 1931 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which limits exceptions to procedures that will save a woman's life\u8212\'20\'14but soon reverted to a policy allowing medically necessary abortions. "We reinstated the policy and are not aware of any patient whose medical care was impacted during this brief period of time," a spokeswoman said.\par \par \uc2 Michigan's Democratic Attorney General Dana Nessel "has repeatedly made clear that she will not use the resources of her office to prosecute a provider for performing a medical procedure," a spokeswoman said. The state's 1931 law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/michigan-judge-suspends-enforcement-of-pre-roe-abortion-ban-11652819032"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been temporarily blocked }}}\uc2  by a Michigan judge.\par \par \uc2 Yet some states' laws would bar some of the abortions required under the federal law, according to doctors and lawyers.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, a so-called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger law, which a state judge has temporarily halted, bars the procedure except to prevent a pregnant woman's death or permanent impairment to her life-sustaining organs. The law wouldn't allow an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the Emergency Medical Treatment and Labor Act to prevent bodily impairment.\par \par \uc2 A new law in North Dakota that takes effect July 28 limits an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a medical emergency to saving a pregnant woman's life, but not to protect her organs or bodily function.\par \par \uc2 "The choice is one law or the other," said Melissa Hauer, general counsel and vice president of the North Dakota Hospital Association, which is preparing to review laws and take questions from the state's hospital officials at a meeting later this month.\par \par \uc2 A North Dakota Attorney General's office spokeswoman directed questions regarding EMTALA to the state's hospital association. She referred to state statute for questions about the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Oklahoma risk up to 10 years in prison and fines as much as $100,000 under a law that bars abortions except to save a pregnant woman's life and is scheduled to take effect in late August. Under laws in Idaho and Missouri, doctors may lose their medical license if they violate new state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Doctors in Texas said they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMp2207423"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 delayed medical care in multiple cases }}}\uc2  until a pregnant woman's symptoms of infection worsened because of fear of penalties under the state's 2021 near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , researchers reported in the New England Journal of Medicine last month.\par \par \uc2 Yet doctors said the risks for women rise significantly while waiting for symptoms to worsen. A delay could lead to a woman's death from blood loss or infection, they said.\par \par \uc2 "Our concerns are based on things that we know happen," said Dana Stone, an obstetrician-gynecologist in Oklahoma City. "It's not theoretical for us. We know how terrifying it can be when things get out of hand."\par \par \uc2 Dr. Stone said she has spoken to lawyers about new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protocols, but without clearer laws, she can't be confident which procedures will be legal. "In practice, we will have to pick up the phone and call the attorney before we take care of a woman," she said.\par \par \uc2 The uncertainty could lead to new lawsuits where state and federal laws conflict, said Diana Nordlund, an emergency physician, lawyer and spokeswoman for the \uc2 American College of Emergency Physicians\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The federal emergency-treatment law pre-empts state law, but relying on it won't "eliminate the legal confusion and the legal exposure for physicians," Dr. Nordlund said.\par \par \uc2 In addition, some states have multiple laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that can provide conflicting exceptions and contain different wording, making it difficult to understand their application, said \uc2 Laurie Sobel\uc2 , the associate director of women's health policy at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , who is tracking state laws.\par \par \uc2 Write to Melanie Evans at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Melanie.Evans@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Melanie.Evans@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/doctors-struggle-with-state-abortion-restrictions-at-odds-with-federal-law-11657445400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Doctors Struggle With State \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Restrictions at Odds With Federal Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220710ei7a0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
