\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Will  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Save the Democrats? A new poll says 75% of women favor  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  policies that Roe v. Wade deemed illegal.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2022\par 22:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Four months from the November midterm elections, President Biden is plumbing the depths of unpopularity. Only 39% or so of Americans approve of the job he's doing.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats appear to think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics will save them, as suburban women revolt against the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe v. Wade. They're renewing their campaign to pack the High Court with two or maybe four more Sonia Sotomayors. A member of President Biden's commission on Court reform, former federal Judge Nancy Gertner, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/07/07/joe-biden-supreme-court-commission-00044401"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is saying}}}\uc2  she's "deeply frustrated" with Mr. Biden for failing to meet the moment.\par \par \uc2 Not to spoil the progressive reverie, but look again at the polling, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://harvardharrispoll.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/07/HHP_June2022_KeyResults.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 starting with}}}\uc2  the latest Harvard/Harris survey, which was conducted after the Court overturned Roe. Fifty-five percent of voters, including 61% of women, say they oppose that reversal. Yet many people, as usual, don't seem to understand what exactly Roe enshrined, which was an unrestricted right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before about 23 or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 When, specifically, do the respondents think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal? Starting with the most restrictive, 37% say only after rape or incest. Another 12% say until six weeks of pregnancy, and 23% say up to 15 weeks. Add those figures together and it's nearly three quarters of voters (including 75% of women) who desire a policy that Roe deemed illegal.\par \par \uc2 As for the furious response to the loss of Roe, 59% of voters, including 61% of independents, say it's "wrong" for Democrats to call the Supreme Court "illegitimate." Also, 62% say people shouldn't be allowed to protest outside the Justices' private homes, and 51% believe such activists should be arrested. As for the election, 36% say Roe's reversal makes them more likely to vote for Democrats, 36% say the same for Republicans, and 29% say it won't matter.\par \par \uc2 This is one survey, and the usual caveats apply, but the picture is remarkably consistent. (See our June 10 editorial, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-contradictions-of-abortion-polling-roe-v-wade-11654809065"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "The Contradictions of \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Polling."}}}\uc2 ) Democrats might not respect the median view on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and their bill in Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chuck-schumers-radical-abortion-bill-senate-democrats-roe-v-wade-womens-health-protection-act-11652133702"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggests they don't}}}\uc2 . But many of them seem not even to comprehend it.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-abortion-wont-save-democrats-harris-poll-roe-v-wade-supreme-court-11657229040"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Will \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Save the Democrats?}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220707ei770086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
