\par\par \uc2 Wonder Land\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Saves Politics; The West Virginia and Dobbs decisions free Americans from rule by experts and judges.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Henninger \par \uc2 892 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 July 2022\par 22:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Considering all that's going on in the U.S. now\u8212\'20\'14economic distress, killings, street protests\u8212\'20\'14President Biden's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/speeches-remarks/2022/07/04/remarks-by-president-biden-to-military-families-on-independence-day/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fourth of July remarks}}}\uc2  were remarkably disunifying. "In recent days," he said, "there's been reason to think that this country is moving backward, that freedom is being reduced, that rights we assumed were protected are no longer. A reminder that we remain in an ongoing battle for the soul of America."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is of course referring to one thing\u8212\'20\'14the Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/19-1392_6j37.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs}}}\uc2  decision, which overturned Roe v. Wade, the decision identifying a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 His purpose in great part was partisan. Democrats intend to run on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the midterm elections, though it will be interesting to see how the party fares 50 years after Roe with an argument, made clear the past week, that as an American value, aborting a pregnancy now holds primacy over birth.\par \par \uc2 However mightily contested, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is but one political issue. Another Supreme Court decision, which Democrats aren't likely to elevate as a "battle for the soul of America," is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/20-1530_n758.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 West Virginia v. Environmental Protection Agency}}}\uc2 . But it is West Virginia, not Dobbs, that has the greater potential to improve this country's bitter and corrosive politics.\par \par \uc2 Ears don't normally prick up when the words "\uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 " appear in the name of a Supreme Court case, but every warrior in the political trenches knows this one was different.\par \par \uc2 Writing for the 6-3 majority, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said the \uc2 EPA\uc2  had grossly hyperextended any authority it assumed it had to issue regulations related to climate change. Pushing an "unheralded power" that far, the court said, needed more explicit authorization from Congress.\par \par \uc2 In her dissent, Justice Elena Kagan accused the majority of an "anti-administrative state" agenda. Believe it or not, those are fighting words. In Justice Kagan's wide intellectual and political circles, people more likely would join a march over a threat to the administrative state than to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Perhaps more than any other issue, the rise of the so-called administrative state\u8212\'20\'14which is to say the public sector's detailed oversight of the nation's daily life\u8212\'20\'14is why American politics descended over time into gridlock and polarization. Unlike Roe v. Wade's instant thunderclap in 1973, the political divisions created by the administrative state emerged across a century.\par \par \uc2 After World War I came the Roaring '20s, an era that usually evokes economic vitality\u8212\'20\'14streets filled with cars and people, factory smokestacks, a nation being built. But progressives instead saw an industrial society whose unpredictability was a problem, and that could be made coherent and governable only with guidance given to elected officials by individuals trained in the new complexities. Distrustful of private economic exchange at any level, they promised to "rationalize" it.\par \par \uc2 By no means was this the least respectable idea ever to emerge in American politics. Life was more complex. Market excesses were evident. Incubated by President \uc2 Woodrow Wilson\uc2 , the idea of a widely administered state grew inside FDR's New Deal, then extended to health care and social-welfare policy with LBJ's Great Society. Richard Nixon pushed the bureaucracies deeper into environmental issues.\par \par \uc2 No phenomenon in possession of such vast authority has benefited more from its plain-vanilla title, the "administrative state." Could anything sound more innocuous?\par \par \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was the first president to make a political issue out of the administrative state's own excesses. What began as a theory about governing with the knowledge of experts, Reagan argued, had become coercion by experts. No dopes, the Democrats made these nominally neutral experts a permanent, powerful instrument of achieving their politics through administrative fiat across the half-century that Roe governed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Judges, sympathetic to these goals, affirmed the steady flow of rules.\par \par \uc2 This alliance between the Democratic Party, the courts and an increasingly non-neutral generation of experts, such as Elena Kagan, displaced the give and take of traditional politics as a venue for arbitrating social and economic disagreements. Unresolved, these disputes festered inside a swamp of proceduralism and litigation.\par \par \uc2 Exhibit A: ObamaCare.\par \par \uc2 American healthcare, once known as going to see your doctor, is an area of U.S. life captured completely by academic specialists. Today it's a caricature of complexity. The predictable result of the effort to create ObamaCare was a partisan legislative morass. Republicans defaulted to one option: Vote against the whole thing. Congress's policy-making role was now manifestly dysfunctional.\par \par \uc2 The mediating role of politics declined further with climate disputes and more recently the pandemic. Not content with imposing policy on private life, the administrative apparatus\u8212\'20\'14experts, their scientific publications and media allies\u8212\'20\'14began to suppress dissent within their own disciplines, such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/epidemiologists-stray-from-the-covid-herd-11603477330?mod=e2tw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Great Barrington Declaration}}}\uc2  on Covid-19 policies.\par \par \uc2 The administrative state, operating for so long beyond the reach of politics or serious appeal, has created ideological divides that will take a long time to undo. With these two decisions\u8212\'20\'14West Virginia and Dobbs\u8212\'20\'14the Supreme Court is attempting to revive the role that substantive politics played at the time of the Founding. I'd call it political originalism.\par \par \uc2 Write henninger@wsj.com.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-supreme-court-saves-politics-west-virginia-v-epa-dobbs-abortion-administrative-state-experts-democracy-11657139976"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Supreme Court Saves Politics}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220706ei76007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
