\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates have pushed for expanded access to mifepristone, manufactured by Danco Laboratories. PHOTO: Michael Noble Jr. for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The company's cautious approach has frustrated \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers pushed the company for years to request updates to its label. When the company made the move, it relied on outside studies from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters to show the changes were safe. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  in 2016 approved Mifeprex for use to end a pregnancy up to 10 weeks, three weeks longer than its previously approved use.\par \par \uc2 "They didn't want to rock the boat I think by making changes," said Beverly Winikoff, president of Gynuity Health Projects, which advocates for access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Kirsten Moore, director of the Expanding Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access Project, said she has encouraged Danco to seek \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for Mifeprex to be prescribed for miscarriage. Widening the approved use would dilute its association with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Moore said.\par \par \uc2 "Having this drug labeled for the use of miscarriage could expand the pool of providers who are willing to prescribe it for their patients," she said. "It also complicates the opposition's narrative of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Danco said it is interested in a miscarriage label because there is data to support that use.\par \par \uc2 "We care very much about access to reproductive healthcare and women's health," the Danco spokeswoman said. "But at the same time it's a business, it's a for-profit business that needs to be able to keep its business."\par \par \uc2 Write to Liz Essley Whyte at {