\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Congress Can't 'Codify Roe v. Wade'; The Constitution gives it no authority to overrule state  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Thomas J. Molony \par \uc2 865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 July 2022\par 19:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 President Biden last week urged the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to abolish the filibuster so that Congress can "codify Roe v. Wade." But the filibuster's 60-vote requirement isn't the most serious impediment to the Women's Health Protection Act, a Democratic bill that passed the House, or for that matter to Republican Sen. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 's more modest Reproductive Choice Act. The most serious impediment is the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The Constitution is silent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the Supreme Court held last month in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. But it speaks clearly about the limits on congressional power. The most recent version of the Women's Health Protection Act doesn't cite any source of congressional authority, but earlier versions pointed to Section 5 of the 14th Amendment and Article I's Commerce Clause.\par \par \uc2 Both are dead ends. Section 5 gives Congress the power to enforce the 14th Amendment's Due Process and Equal Protection clauses. But in City of Boerne v. Flores (1997), the court emphasized that Section 5 wouldn't permit Congress to alter the 14th Amendment's substance. The court found that Section 5 wouldn't permit Congress to impose on states the Religious Freedom Restoration Act's rigorous test for regulations infringing on religious exercise.\par \par \uc2 The same rationale precludes Congress from using Section 5 to breathe life back into Roe. In overturning Roe v. Wade (1973) and Planned Parenthood v. Casey (1992), the justices held that the Due Process Clause doesn't guarantee women the right to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dobbs effectively foreclosed Equal Protection challenges to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as well. Thus, with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Constitution leaves the states with the same broad discretion they have to regulate other aspects of medical practice. Congress can't use Section 5 to change that.\par \par \uc2 The Commerce Clause likewise is of no avail. To be sure, the court has interpreted Congress's power to regulate interstate commerce broadly. The justices have upheld, among other things, federal regulation of the price of milk produced and sold exclusively within a single state and restrictions on the production of wheat for a farmer's own use.\par \par \uc2 But the Commerce Clause has limits. In \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2  v. Sebelius (2012), five justices determined that Congress can't use its commerce power to compel individuals to engage in commerce. Congress similarly can't force a state to allow a healthcare provider to offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services on congressional terms. In the License Tax Cases (1866), the court emphasized that "the power to authorize a business within a State is plainly repugnant to the exclusive power of the State over the same subject." States get to decide whether and to what extent they permit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within their borders.\par \par \uc2 In Linder v. U.S. (1925), the court stated that "direct control of medical practice in the states is beyond the power of the federal government." In Gonzales v. Raich (2005), the court held that Congress could regulate the practice of medicine indirectly "as an essential part of a larger regulation" designed to combat drug trafficking, but these \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills attempt to regulate medical practice directly.\par \par \uc2 Federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights legislation would impermissibly encroach on a state's prerogative to define what is criminal within its borders. The plurality in Screws v. U.S. (1945) explained that "under our federal system the administration of criminal justice rests with the States except as Congress, acting within the scope of [its] delegated powers, has created offenses against the United States." The upshot of this is that Congress may not legalize performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a state when the state has decided to punish that conduct as criminal. Legalization isn't regulation.\par \par \uc2 What about restrictions? Could Congress impose a nationwide ban? In Gonzales v. Carhart (2007), the justices upheld a federal ban on partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yet what Linder states about control over medical practice arguably would apply equally to a nationwide ban or lesser restrictions. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , concurring in Carhart, noted that the case didn't raise the question whether the law "constitutes a permissible exercise of Congress' power under the Commerce Clause." He has repeatedly called on the court to reconsider its Commerce Clause jurisprudence, contending in U.S. v. Morrison (2000) that the courts' "rootless and malleable standard . . . has encouraged the Federal Government to persist in its view that the Commerce Clause has virtually no limits." Whether Justice Thomas's colleagues will accept his invitation, and what that might mean for federal laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is an open question.\par \par \uc2 As the court reminded President \uc2 Franklin D. Roosevelt\uc2  in Schechter Poultry Corp. v. U.S. (1935), federal officials "are not at liberty to transcend imposed limits because they believe that more or different power is necessary." The Constitution doesn't empower Congress to force states to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  against their wishes. What Mr. Biden said about the Covid pandemic applies to legislating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights: "There is no federal solution."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Molony is a law professor at Elon University.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/congress-cant-codify-roe-v-wade-abortion-dobbs-14th-amendment-constitution-religious-exercise-justices-filibuster-11656953182"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Congress Can't 'Codify Roe v. Wade'}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220704ei74003s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
