\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Alabama Argues for Transgender Youth Medication Ban, Citing Supreme Court  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling; State is one of first to use Dobbs to advance legal cases not related to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joseph De Avila and Talal Ansari \par \uc2 881 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par 22:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Alabama's attorney general is defending the state's ban on medical treatments for transgender youth by citing the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision overturning Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The state's attorney general's office, led by Republican \uc2 Steve Marshall\uc2  , submitted a brief Monday asking a federal appeals court to remove an injunction blocking Alabama from enforcing the law. The measure bans surgery and other gender-affirming treatments such as hormones and puberty blockers for those who are under 19 years old.\par \par \uc2 In its brief, the attorney's general office made several citations of Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the ruling that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "But no one\u8212\'20\'14adult or child\u8212\'20\'14has a right to transitioning treatments that is deeply rooted in our Nation's history and tradition," the attorney general's office wrote in the brief. "The State can thus regulate or prohibit those interventions for children, even if an adult wants the drugs for his child."\par \par \uc2 That statement echoes the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority opinion  }}}\uc2  issued by the Supreme Court in the Dobbs case.\par \par \uc2 "The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not deeply rooted in the Nation's history and tradition," said the opinion, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/key-excerpts-from-supreme-courts-abortion-ruling-overturning-roe-v-wade-11656092052?mod=livecoverage_web&mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 written by Justice Samuel Alito  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Levi, the transgender rights project director for GLBTQ Legal Advocates & Defenders, said she wasn't surprised to see Alabama relying on Dobbs to defend its ban on transgender medical treatments for children.\par \par \uc2 "We don't think, however, Alabama's use of Dobbs will gain much traction with the appeals court because the Supreme Court majority was so clear that the opinion has no application beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ms. Levi, who is representing the plaintiffs who have sued the state to stop the law from taking effect.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court and lower courts in many cases have declined to recognize new fundamental rights unless they are rooted in U.S. history and tradition, Attorney General Marshall said in a statement. Appellate courts have also repeatedly held that there is no deeply rooted right to novel and risky medications, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Alabama's case should be an easy one," Mr. Marshall said. The question is whether the Constitution demands that states allow these treatments to be administered to children, he said. "And the answer is, of course not."\par \par \uc2 Alabama is one of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arkansas-bans-gender-transition-treatment-for-trans-minors-11617744915"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several states  }}}\uc2  that passed measures aimed at restricting gender-based healthcare to transgender individuals. The law was signed by Republican Gov. \uc2 Kay Ivey\uc2  in April, and punishment for those who perform gender-confirming treatments can include up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 C.P. Hoffman, senior policy counsel for the National Center for Transgender Equality, said politicians in Alabama are trying to criminalize transgender people.\par \par \uc2 "Moves like this hurt transgender youth and their families and go against what doctors and scientists know to be true: gender-affirming care is medically necessary and can be lifesaving for transgender young people," said Mx. Hoffman, who uses the gender-neutral honorific.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ivey's office didn't respond to requests for comment Friday.\par \par \uc2 "I believe very strongly that if the Good Lord made you a boy, you are a boy, and if he made you a girl, you are a girl," Ms. Ivey said in a statement when she signed the bill into law. "We should especially protect our children from these radical, life-altering drugs and surgeries when they are at such a vulnerable stage in life."\par \par \uc2 A coalition of civil-rights advocates filed a federal lawsuit in April challenging the law on behalf of a group of families, a reverend and two healthcare providers affected by it. The plaintiffs argue the law violates their rights of equal protection and due process guaranteed by the U.S. Constitution by intruding into the rights of parents to obtain medically necessary care for their children.\par \par \uc2 The federal district court in Alabama granted the plaintiffs' request for a preliminary injunction, blocking the law from taking effect. The plaintiffs have until August to submit a reply brief.\par \par \uc2 The Alabama case is one of the first examples of a state using Dobbs to advance legal cases not related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Katherine Franke, director of the Center for Gender & Sexuality Law at Columbia Law School. She said states facing lawsuits related to new laws banning healthcare procedures for transgender individuals will likely follow suit.\par \par \uc2 While the Supreme Court's majority opinion on Dobbs said its ruling only applied to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases, the same legal reasoning used by the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022/card/analysis-of-the-supreme-court-s-decision-to-overturn-roe-vs-wade-capital-journal-vEt2oyIawlZa5CO1cD1f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could have implications  }}}\uc2  for a variety of cases, including those involving transgender healthcare, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/clarence-thomass-abortion-opinion-revisits-same-sex-marriage-contraception-11656161978"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 same-sex marriage and contraception  }}}\uc2 , Dr. Franke said.\par \par \uc2 "Conservative advocates have been making this argument for some time. This is not new," Dr. Franke said. "What is new is that the Supreme Court has now given them a green light to lead with this argument, and lower courts may follow."\par \par \uc2 Write to Joseph De Avila at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joseph.deavila@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joseph.deavila@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Talal Ansari at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:talal.ansari@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 talal.ansari@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Talal Ansari at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/alabama-argues-for-transgender-medication-ban-citing-supreme-court-abortion-ruling-11656698904"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Alabama Argues for Transgender Youth Medication Ban, Citing Supreme Court \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220701ei71006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\