\par\par \uc2 Health\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , Target Show Divergence on Companies'  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Coverage; \uc2 Walmart\uc2  doesn't broadly cover abortions for employees. Target does. Other companies scramble to better understand what they cover.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah Nassauer and Anna Wilde Mathews \par \uc2 1,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par 21:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Employees of Target Corp. have access to health-insurance plans that broadly cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Walmart Inc\uc2 .'s health plans don't cover an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless the health of the mother is at risk or the fetus couldn't survive birth.\par \par \uc2 The rival retailers' policies highlight the range of coverage at the largest U.S. employers, whose health plans have come into sharper focus since last week, when the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  and left the question of its legality to the states. Target is among companies adding a benefit that covers travel expenses to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 Walmart\uc2  says it is reviewing its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage.\par \par \uc2 The type of health-insurance plans offered by large and midsize U.S. companies tend to include some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefits. A federal antidiscrimination statute requires all employers that provide health insurance to cover an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the life of the mother is at risk from the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Around 10% of covered American workers are enrolled in plans offered by employers that specifically excluded or tightly restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage, according to a 2019 survey by the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Large-employer plans diverge on whether employees are covered for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when there isn't a health risk in carrying the pregnancy to full term. Smaller companies tend to have a type of insurance that is subject to state laws, which may or may not allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage as part of plans.\par \par \uc2 Around 37% of companies cover at least some form of elective abortions, according to an early June survey of 400 companies of all sizes from consultant Mercer, a unit of \uc2 Marsh & McLennan Cos\uc2 . The survey isn't complete and many companies are only recently reviewing the details of their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage, said some of the Mercer executives involved in the research.\par \par \uc2 "It is hard to get firm data on what percentage of healthcare plans cover elective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and what don't," said Julie Campbell, a principal in Mercer's Health & Benefits group. In recent days, some clients are reaching out to Mercer after discovering their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage is unintentionally narrow, she said.\par \par \uc2 Target, \uc2 Walgreens Boots Alliance Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . offer employee healthcare plans that cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  broadly, according to people familiar with the plans. Large banks and technology companies tend to cover the costs of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care for employees and family members under their plans, including \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 . The companies are among those that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-roe-v-wade-ruling-companies-reimbursing-medical-and-travel-expenses-for-abortion-risk-blowback-11656109337"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have also promised to reimburse employees }}}\uc2  for travel related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other healthcare services.\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's 2022 employee benefits document states: "Charges for procedures, services, drugs, and supplies related to abortions or termination of pregnancy are not covered, except when the health of the mother would be in danger if the fetus were carried to term, the fetus could not survive the birthing process, or death would be imminent after birth."\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the biggest private employer in the country with around 1.7 million U.S. staffers, doesn't cover the cost of emergency contraception such as Plan B unless prescribed by a doctor, according to the documents. Medications such as Plan B, which are intended to prevent pregnancy up to 3 days after unprotected sex, are available for purchase without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 In a memo to staff on Friday, \uc2 Walmart\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2  said the company was getting input from employees and reviewing its policies following the Supreme Court's decision.\par \par \uc2 "No matter what your position on this topic is, we want you to feel respected, valued and supported," he wrote in the memo. "We will share details on our actions as soon as possible, recognizing that time is of the essence."\par \par \uc2 Walmart\uc2 's policies on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage closely mirror \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Arkansas, where the retailer is based. When the Supreme Court overturned the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arkansas-governor-signs-bill-that-bans-most-abortions-11615330493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas's attorney general enacted a so-called trigger law }}}\uc2  that outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in all cases except to protect the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Many executives didn't pay much attention to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  benefits until the last couple of months, after a leaked draft opinion indicated the Supreme Court was preparing to overturn Roe v. Wade, according to \uc2 Laurie Sobel\uc2 , associate director at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , a nonprofit healthcare research organization. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a relatively inexpensive benefit for a company to cover, and employees were less likely to ask about it as part of hiring as they might be for something like in vitro fertilization coverage, Ms. Sobel said. Now companies are digging into the details of their plans, she said.\par \par \uc2 Some 14% of large employers surveyed in May said they had a travel benefit in place or are planning to have one, while another 25% said they were considering it, according to responses from nearly 700 employers from a different survey conducted by benefits consultant Mercer. Employers with at least 20,000 workers were the most likely to provide a travel and lodging benefit for employees traveling to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the survey said.\par \par \uc2 In the U.S., the median self-pay price for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2020 was $560, and the median cost of a first-trimester procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was $575, according to a study published in April in the journal Health Affairs.\par \par \uc2 Many people pay out-of-pocket for abortions, say researchers and consultants, in part because they aim to keep the procedure private from employers or because not all \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers take insurance.\par \par \uc2 Before the new Supreme Court decision, companies and employees navigated a patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  healthcare coverage rules.\par \par \uc2 Many states' Medicaid plans restrict coverage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while federal programs like Medicare pay for it under limited circumstances. States have also restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in private insurance, with 11 of them banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in all plans they regulate, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet state insurance rules don't apply to the type of coverage that is most common among midsize and large employers. Those so-called self-funded plans, in which employers pay workers' health claims with their own money, are regulated at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Weber contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sarah Nassauer at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:sarah.nassauer@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sarah.nassauer@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Anna Wilde Mathews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:anna.mathews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anna.mathews@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/on-abortion-coverage-company-health-plans-diverge-11656700014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Walmart, Target Show Divergence on Companies' \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Coverage }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220701ei71006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
