\par \par \uc2 Mary Lewis of suburban Philadelphia says inflation is of greater concern to her now than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Pennsylvania and the U.S. PHOTO: Scott Calvert/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Some polling in the days after the ruling has found that the court decision is helping Democrats with those who lean Democratic but might need an extra push to turn out in the fall. If sustained, that development could eliminate the advantage in enthusiasm that GOP voters have shown this year.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, interviews in politically competitive states in recent days found that while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are important to many suburban women, they are only one of several issues shaping the decision of how to vote. Some who disagree with the court ruling, and with GOP candidates who say they will move to tighten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, say they plan to vote Republican nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 "They should be trying to figure out how to lower the prices of gas and groceries right now,'' said Mary Lewis, a registered Republican and stay-at-home mother in suburban Philadelphia. She thinks the Supreme Court ruling could lead Pennsylvania lawmakers to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access too severely, in her view, given that some state leaders believe there should be no exceptions for fetal viability. But she also says inflation and pocketbook issues are more important to her. "That's something I'm dealing with on a daily basis,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The suburbs are home to many of the nation's most competitive U.S. House races this year. In 2018, Democrats picked up at least 40 House seats, in large part because many suburban women who had leaned Republican decided to back Democrats. That year and in the 2020 presidential election, suburban women gave about 60% of their vote to Democrats, polling showed.\par \par \uc2 Polling in recent days finds that suburban women disapprove of the court ruling and are particularly animated to vote. In a Marist/NPR/\uc2 PBS\uc2  survey conducted last week, 74% of women who live in suburbs or small cities said they were more likely to vote due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision, compared with 62% of voters overall. More suburban women, some 66%, said they opposed the ruling than did voters overall, by 11 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Some pollsters say that results such as these indicate that Democratic-leaning voters are becoming more committed to their choice of candidates, rather than reflecting a move by independent, Republican or swing voters away from the GOP. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
