\par \par \uc2 Renee Dennis of suburban Philadelphia recently changed her party affiliation to Democrat. PHOTO: Scott Calvert/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Renee Dennis, a 43-year-old artist in suburban Philadelphia, was a registered Republican who had backed \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  for president before supporting Democrats for the White House. On Monday, she changed her party registration to Democratic, due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling.\par \par \uc2 "If you're anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I don't have time for you,'' said Ms. Dennis. "I'm not going to live back in the 1950s.'' She had been planning to back Democratic candidates even before the court ruling, "but this makes me feel like I can never consider a Republican until we fix this.''\par \par \uc2 Diana Garber, 63, a Republican who lives in the Washington, D.C., suburb of Woodbridge, Va., said the Supreme Court "went the right way'' in its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling. But she didn't need any added motivation to vote. She was already committed to backing the Republican trying to defeat Democratic Rep. \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  in one of the year's most competitive House races.\par \par \uc2 "That would not have swayed my vote,'' she said of the court ruling. "The border is number one, inflation is number two." Ms. Garber said that Yesli Vega, the GOP nominee facing Ms. Spanberger, was on the right side of those issues. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
