\par \par \uc2 Johnice Williams said she isn't as concerned about the Roe decision because it wouldn't have much effect in her state of California. PHOTO: Christine Mai-Duc/The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Pat Bezek, 67, a registered Democrat who lives in the affluent suburb of Upper St. Clair, outside Pittsburgh, said she worries that the court ruling will open the door to too many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in her state.\par \par \uc2 "I'm a very involved Catholic at my parish. However, I do feel that women should have a right to have some options," said Ms. Bezek, who works for the Sisters of Mercy, a Catholic religious order. She said she also opposed the ruling because of its potential to spark more divisiveness. "We don't need another reason for people to get upset and be fighting in the streets," she said.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact will the overturning of Roe v. Wade have on the midterm elections? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, Ms. Schultz said she would likely vote for Mr. Kemp even though she doesn't like the law he signed that allows people to carry a handgun in public without a license or background check, and she doesn't like the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction he signed in 2019. The law, which has yet to take effect due to court challenges, would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity is detected. Exceptions are allowed where the mother's life or health are at stake, and for incest and rape if a police report has been filed.\par \par \uc2 Despite her misgivings, Ms. Schultz said of Mr. Kemp: "I thought he did a good job in the last four years.''\par \par \uc2 At the same time, she says she is also likely to support Sen. Raphael Warnock, a Democrat, this fall. And she hopes that if re-elected, Mr. Warnock will vote to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Kris Maher in Upper St. Clair, Pa., and Christine Mai-Duc in Anaheim, Calif., contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Aaron Zitner at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:aaron.zitner@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 aaron.zitner@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Scott Calvert at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:scott.calvert@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scott.calvert@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-ruling-scrambles-voting-calculus-for-suburban-women-11656684001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling Scrambles Voting Calculus for Suburban Women }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220701ei71002ut\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
