\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Florida Pauses on More  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Restrictions Following Supreme Court Decision; Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is appealing judge's decision to block 15-week ban before potentially pushing further\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cameron McWhirter and Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 1,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par 18:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As many states move to restrict or strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last week's overturning of Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  by the Supreme Court, Florida has so far stood out in the Republican-dominated South for its subdued response.\par \par \uc2 Despite being led by a Republican governor who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and having a GOP-controlled legislature, state leaders haven't moved to impose new restrictions on the procedure and didn't have a law in place to ban or further limit the procedure immediately after the Supreme Court eliminated the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Medical providers in Florida performed the third-highest number of abortions in 2020 of any state, behind only Democratic-led New York and California, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 Numerous Republican-led states have quickly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/new-abortion-lawsuits-continue-in-states-cutting-access-11656543956"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moved to ban or heavily restrict most abortions  }}}\uc2  since the Supreme Court's decision through existing trigger laws or court orders. Abortions are largely unavailable in about a dozen states due to these restrictions or uncertainty about whether it will remain legal.\par \par \uc2 Other Democratic-leaning states with leaders who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have introduced new measures cementing access.\par \par \uc2 Florida political leaders say a reason for their caution is due to a legal challenge over the state's ban after 15 weeks of pregnancy slated to take effect July 1. Leon County Circuit Court Judge John Cooper on Thursday said he would issue a temporary injunction barring enforcement of the law because it violates the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What effect do you think the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue will have on Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in particular and the election of 2024 in general? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Florida's constitution has a right to privacy, added in 1980, that has previously been interpreted to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Other factors include Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  's political considerations as he seeks re-election in November and eyes a possible run for the presidency in 2024, political analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The constitution's right-to-privacy clause has helped prevent more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions from being enforced in Florida, said state Rep. \uc2 Anna Eskamani\uc2  , a Democrat who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Christina Pushaw\uc2  , a spokeswoman for Mr. DeSantis, said the state will appeal Judge Cooper's decision in the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case. It could eventually reach the Florida Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 "The struggle for life is not over," Ms. Pushaw said.\par \par \uc2 Democratic state Sen. Lauren Book said she expected that court, which is dominated by conservatives including several appointed by Mr. DeSantis, will rule that the constitution's privacy clause doesn't protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Due in part to the sparring over the constitutional privacy clause, a law requiring patients to visit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers for an in-person counseling period and wait 24 hours for the procedure took effect this year after around seven years of litigation.\par \par \uc2 Republican Sen. \uc2 Kelli Stargel\uc2  said as the 15-week ban was considered earlier this year that she believed, compared with other possible restrictions, it had the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-supreme-court-weighs-abortion-rights-more-states-look-at-15-week-bans-11645641664"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 best chance of standing up to legal challenges  }}}\uc2  if the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  upheld a similar law in Mississippi. The nation's highest court did so last week, but also went further by ending the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely.\par \par \uc2 Florida Democrats said they viewed the 15-week ban as a launchpad for tighter restrictions in the future.\par \par \uc2 Legislators and advocates on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate in Florida say they expect to see action eventually on the issue, but that efforts are currently on hold because of the unresolved legal issue.\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis celebrated last week's Supreme Court decision and is now focused on a battle over the state's 15-week ban, Ms. Pushaw.\par \par \uc2 "Our future legislative action necessarily depends on the resolution of these legal issues," she said. "We are in continuous contact with the legislature as this litigation proceeds, and we look forward to future policy plans to defend the unborn."\par \par \uc2 Aubrey Jewett, a political-science professor at the \uc2 University of Central Florida\uc2  , said Mr. DeSantis could choose to push antiabortion legislation next year when he is potentially preparing to try to appeal to conservative voters in the 2025 Republican presidential primary.\par \par \uc2 "I would bet that he would push forward on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Dr. Jewett said. "It's just a matter of when."\par \par \uc2 Mr. DeSantis's office wouldn't comment on political speculation, Ms. Pushaw said.\par \par \uc2 Currently, Florida allows abortions up to 24 weeks of pregnancy and has 55 clinics that perform the procedure, making it a destination for patients from other southern states with more restrictions and less access, according to advocates.\par \par \uc2 Leaders in the Republican-led Florida legislature have said little about specific future measures.\par \par \uc2 "We're not running out filing bills," said Republican state Sen. \uc2 Dennis Baxley\uc2  . He said meetings among antiabortion politicians and advocates are in the works.\par \par \uc2 John Stemberger, president and general counsel of Florida Family Policy Council, an antiabortion group, said he expected GOP lawmakers to pursue a ban on most abortions after six weeks of pregnancy or a near-total ban on the procedure during the next legislative session in 2023, or possibly in a special session later this year.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hope the likelihood of GOP legislators pushing future stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures will drive more of their voters to the polls this fall in a state that used to be closely divided but has recently trended toward Republicans.\par \par \uc2 "The only thing we can do is work our asses off to bring pro-choice Democrats to Tallahassee," Ms. Book said.\par \par \uc2 Medical providers in Florida performed 77,400 abortions in 2020, up from 72,210 in 2019 and 71,050 in 2017, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across Florida said they have been working to expand clinic hours and staff in anticipation of an influx of patients if Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 "It's really so unpredictable right now," said Kelly Flynn, president and chief executive of A Woman's Choice, which operates a clinic in Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 Write to Cameron McWhirter at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:cameron.mcwhirter@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cameron.mcwhirter@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-pauses-on-more-abortion-restrictions-following-supreme-court-decision-11656591626"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Florida Pauses on More \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Restrictions Following Supreme Court Decision  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220630ei6u00209\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
