\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  Closes Historic Session: Here Are the Notable Opinions; Justices overturned Roe v. Wade as well as issued rulings on gun rights, business law, church and state, immigration and other matters\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Graff \par \uc2 1,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par 19:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  this week closed out one of its most contentious sessions in recent years, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its overturning on Friday of Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2 , the 1973 decision recognizing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here are some notable decisions reached by the court during the session.\par \par \uc2 ABORTION\par \par \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3 on Mississippi's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law; 5-4 on overruling Roe v. Wade\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Alito, Thomas, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett; Chief Justice Roberts concurs on Mississippi law but dissents on overruling Roe\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Kagan, Breyer and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , overruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision  }}}\uc2  and leaving the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's legality to the states. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court's decision  }}}\uc2  on June 24 upheld {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a law from Mississippi  }}}\uc2  that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy. In doing so, the court's conservative majority said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Roe decision was egregiously wrong  }}}\uc2  in recognizing a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an error the court perpetuated in the decades since. "It is time to heed the Constitution and return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the people's elected representatives," Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote for the court.\par \par \uc2 The court voted 6-3 to side with Mississippi, but 5-4 on the broader question of whether to overrule Roe. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  , in a concurring opinion, agreed that the Mississippi law should stand but didn't support rescinding the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether.\par \par \uc2 GUN RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 New York State Rifle & Pistol Association Inc. v. Bruen\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Thomas, Roberts, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Kagan, Breyer and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-strikes-down-new-york-law-on-concealed-weapons-11655995248"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on June 23 struck down  }}}\uc2  New York state's system for issuing concealed-weapons permits, ruling that the century-old law requiring that applicants demonstrate "proper cause" and "good moral character" violates the Second Amendment. The decision marks the widest expansion of gun rights since 2010, when the court applied nationwide a 2008 ruling establishing an individual right of armed self-defense within the home. It puts in question laws in nine other states and the District of Columbia, where authorities hold substantial discretion over issuing concealed-weapons permits.\par \par \uc2 ENVIRONMENTAL RULE-MAKING\par \par \uc2 West Virginia v. \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Roberts, Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissent: Justices Kagan, Breyer and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The court issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-limits-environmental-protection-agencys-authority-11656598034"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a decision limiting the power of regulatory agencies  }}}\uc2  within the federal government, saying the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  overstepped its authority in 2015 when it tried to limit greenhouse-gas emissions from power plants, known as the Clean Power Plan. The court said that when federal agencies issue regulations with sweeping economic and political consequences\u8212\'20\'14in this case, rules to address climate change\u8212\'20\'14the regulations are presumptively invalid unless Congress has specifically authorized the action.\par \par \uc2 CHURCH AND STATE\par \par \uc2 Kennedy v. Bremerton School District\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Gorsuch, Thomas, Roberts, Alito, Kavanaugh and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissent: Justices Sotomayor, Breyer and Kagan\par \par \uc2 Siding with a football coach who knelt in prayer at the 50-yard line, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-says-high-school-football-coach-can-pray-at-50-yard-line-11656339266"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the court ruled 6-3 on June 27  }}}\uc2  that a school district cannot bar him from publicly exercising his faith on the field after the game, continuing a line of decisions lowering the wall between church and state. School officials in Bremerton, Wash., {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-mulls-impact-of-high-school-coachs-50-yard-line-prayers-11650918003"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued that the coach  }}}\uc2 , Joe Kennedy, commandeered the government-owned field to promote his faith to fans and students after the game, implicitly putting the district's imprimatur on his brand of Christianity.\par \par \uc2 Carson v. Makin\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Roberts, Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Kagan, Breyer and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-maine-must-include-religious-schools-in-tuition-program-11655822468"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled on June 21 that religious schools can't be excluded  }}}\uc2  from a Maine program that pays private-school tuition for students in areas that lack public schools, the latest decision by a conservative majority skeptical of precedents that drew a bright line between church and state.\par \par \uc2 IMMIGRATION\par \par \uc2 Biden v. Texas\par \par \uc2 Decision: 5-4\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Roberts, Kagan, Breyer, Sotomayor and Kavanaugh\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch and Barrett\par \par \uc2 In this case, the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  ruled that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-biden-can-end-remain-in-mexico-policy-11656598778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Biden administration could cancel  }}}\uc2  the Trump-era "Remain in Mexico" program, which required authorities either to jail asylum applicants from Central America or deny them U.S. entry until their cases are resolved. The court found the Biden administration acted within its discretion by canceling the program, overturning lower-court rulings.\par \par \uc2 Arizona v. \uc2 City and County of San Francisco\par \par \uc2 Dismissed\par \par \uc2 The court on June 15 dismissed a case regarding the effort by Republican-led states to reinstate a Trump-era regulation penalizing lawful immigrants for using welfare benefits, deciding after the case was argued that it should never have agreed to hear it in the first place. The decision affords the Biden administration a victory in its effort to lower the threshold for using welfare benefits that disqualifies lawful immigrants from obtaining "green cards" for permanent residency. The court's one-sentence order provided no explanation, as is customary when the justices dismiss a case as "improvidently granted."\par \par \uc2 CAMPAIGN FINANCING\par \par \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  v. Ted Cruz for Senate\par \par \uc2 Decision: 6-3\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Roberts, Thomas, Alito, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh, and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Kagan, Breyer, and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 On May 16, the court's conservatives {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-strikes-down-campaign-finance-regulation-11652711749"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a 6-3 decision  }}}\uc2  siding with Sen. Ted Cruz (R., Texas) by striking down a federal campaign-finance regulation limiting politicians from repaying loans to themselves above $250,000 from donations received after Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS LAW\par \par \uc2 Southwest Airlines Co\uc2  . v. Saxon\par \par \uc2 Decision: 8\u8211\'20\'130 (Justice Barrett didn't participate)\par \par \uc2 The court ruled unanimously on June 6 that airline baggage handlers can't be compelled to resolve employment disputes through individual private arbitration, bucking a trend of recent decisions making it easier for employers to avoid class-action lawsuits brought by workers. The case has closely been watched for its implications for other types of workers, such as drivers hired by \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2  . or \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2  . But Monday's opinion stuck closely to the facts of the Southwest case.\par \par \uc2 ELECTION LAW\par \par \uc2 Berger v. North Carolina State Conference of the \uc2 NAACP\par \par \uc2 Decision: 8-1\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Gorsuch, Thomas, Roberts, Alito, Breyer, Kagan, Kavanaugh and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justice Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The court on Thursday said top Republican legislators in North Carolina can step in and advocate for a voter-identification law they believe the state's attorney general, a Democrat, isn't adequately defending in court. The narrowly drawn 8-1 decision by Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  found that Republican leaders of the North Carolina legislature can intervene in long-running litigation over a 2018 law requiring a photo ID to vote in person at the polls.\par \par \uc2 NATIVE RIGHTS\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma v. Castro-Huerta\par \par \uc2 Decision: 5-4\par \par \uc2 Majority: Justices Kavanaugh, Thomas, Roberts, Alito and Barrett\par \par \uc2 Dissenting: Justices Gorsuch, Breyer, Kagan and Sotomayor\par \par \uc2 The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-restores-oklahomas-prosecutorial-power-over-non-tribal-prosecutions-in-indian-country-11656511700"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled on June 29  }}}\uc2  that Oklahoma can prosecute non-Native-Americans in Indian country, clawing back part of its 2020 decision recognizing nearly half of the state as a reservation. The majority opinion by Justice Kavanaugh took a limited view of the sovereignty the tribes retain in 21st-century America. "Indian country is part of the State, not separate from the State," he wrote for the court. The dissent by Justice Gorsuch, who had written the landmark ruling from 2020, McGirt v. Oklahoma, asserted that the court should enforce long-ignored treaties, recounting centuries of broken promises to Native American tribes.\par \par \uc2 Write to James Graff at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:james.graff@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 james.graff@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/whats-ahead-as-supreme-court-nears-historic-sessions-end-11653164313"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Closes Historic Session: Here Are the Notable Opinions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220629ei6t005pl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
