\par\par \uc2 Business\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 States That Ban  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Could Want Your Data. Here's What to Know. Search history, text messages and location data could take on new significance for people seeking information about  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  services\b0\par\par\uc2 By Meghan Bobrowsky \par \uc2 1,345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par 15:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned the landmark case }}}\uc2  known as Roe v. Wade that provided the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on June 24\u8212\'20\'14and on that day, \uc2 Google\uc2  searches for "how to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in Texas, which has moved to bar the procedure, were three times as high as the previous day, according to the company's search data.\par \par \uc2 Such internet searches could take on new significance in the wake of the court's ruling. That is because data such as user search history, text messages and location data could potentially be used to build legal cases against people who are charged with having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in states that have outlawed the procedure, according to former prosecutors and tech policy researchers.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of the issues in this new battlefront that may be opening between governments and tech companies over people's data:\par \par \uc2 What data might state authorities try to use to prosecute people for getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  where the procedure is illegal?\par \par \uc2 People's digital lives generate vast troves of data that could be used to bring a case, but the data most likely to be sought by prosecutors would be text messages, search history and emails, which might contain information such as receipts for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, says Riana Pfefferkorn, a research scholar at the Stanford Internet Observatory.\par \par \uc2 Legal experts think that at least some of the states that have outlawed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will move aggressively to enforce their laws. There are multiple ways authorities can obtain text messages and other data. Law enforcement can serve a search warrant to a tech company like \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 . or \uc2 Alphabet Inc\uc2 .'s \uc2 Google\uc2  to try to get them to turn over the data, or they can go directly to suspects and ask them to surrender their device, known as a consent search.\par \par \uc2 "Prosecutors could use the grand jury process, search warrants and other less formal means to request the information," said Tom Zych, co-chair of law firm \uc2 Thompson Hine\uc2 's privacy and security group. "The amount of information they are required to disclose to the target of the request varies by jurisdiction, so I would anticipate instances where the data holder may not really know the nature of the matter," he added.\par \par \uc2 Authorities may also seek location data from any app that tracks such information, if they believe someone visited an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic to undergo the procedure. They could also request payment-services information from banks and credit-card companies if they believe someone purchased \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills with a credit or debit card.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few months, concerns have been raised about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-period-tracker-apps-treat-your-data-and-what-that-means-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11655561595"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data from period-tracker apps }}}\uc2  that could be used to help identify if someone missed their period, which might then lead law enforcement to believe the person became pregnant. But the data the apps provide isn't as legally compelling as, say, a receipt for buying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online, Ms. Pfefferkorn says. That is because information from period-tracking apps relies on users' inputs and could easily be inaccurate.\par \par \uc2 How will tech companies respond?\par \par \uc2 Big tech companies including \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Meta Platforms Inc\uc2 ., parent of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, each receive tens of thousands of requests for user information from government authorities in the U.S. each year, and they turn over at least some data to the government roughly 80% of the time, according to their transparency reports. Some of that data has been used to help incriminate people for nonabortion-related crimes.\par \par \uc2 Many of the big tech companies, including those three, haven't publicly said how they would specifically respond to subpoenas or search warrants for messaging, search or location data related to abortions.\par \par \uc2 Some of the information that prosecutors might be looking for, such as iMessages on the iPhone, are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/encryption-apps-apple-imessage-meta-whatsapp-telegram-11654293068"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end-to-end encrypted }}}\uc2 . That means \uc2 Apple\uc2  doesn't have access to the content of the messages to turn over to law enforcement. \uc2 Apple\uc2  says {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/period-tracker-apps-aim-for-anonymity-following-roe-v-wade-decision-11656202089"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data from its Health app }}}\uc2 , which some people use to track their menstrual cycle, is also end-to-end encrypted.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2 's iMessage chats can, however, be read by \uc2 Apple\uc2  when they are backed up to the company's iCloud. The company has said previously that it may give users' iCloud contents to law enforcement in response to a legal request.\par \par \uc2 Have text messages or search history been used in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related court cases before?\par \par \uc2 Yes. Text messages, for instance, have been used in the past to charge a woman who was accused of illegally terminating her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In a 2015 case in Indiana, law enforcement charged the woman with feticide, which refers to killing a fetus or inducing a miscarriage. Authorities obtained a search warrant for her phone and said they found messages telling a friend she had bought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing drugs from overseas, according to local news reports. The woman was convicted, and later released from prison after a judge overturned her feticide conviction, according to court documents.\par \par \uc2 Are \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  the only tech companies that may be asked to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related data?\par \par \uc2 Ride-sharing companies like \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 . could also potentially be asked to provide location data, if law enforcement suspects the services were used to transport someone to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  said it remains "fully committed" to protecting users' privacy while following the law.\par \par \uc2 "We will continue to reserve the right to reject requests for a number of reasons, including those that do not have a valid legal basis, are overly broad, vague, otherwise inappropriate, or if we cannot verify the legitimacy of the request," an \uc2 Uber\uc2  spokeswoman said. She said \uc2 Uber\uc2  notifies U.S. users when their data is requested by law enforcement, unless a court order prohibits them from doing so.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  didn't respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Both companies previously have said they would pay the legal fees of any drivers who are sued for providing transport for a person trying to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, such as the one Texas is moving to institute, would bar anyone from helping women receive abortions and would allow for private citizens to turn in others for a cash reward.\par \par \uc2 Are there going to be differences in terms of data collection and turnover by jurisdiction?\par \par \uc2 Possibly. It's unclear at this time whether a tech company would be compelled to turn over data to a state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban if a resident traveled out of state to undergo the operation, says Emma Weil, a policy analyst at Upturn, a nonprofit focused on technology and justice that advocated for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some states that allow abortions, such as California, have said they won't cooperate with states that are seeking to prosecute individuals they believe received out-of-state abortions.\par \par \uc2 Are there ways to limit the exposure of these types of data?\par \par \uc2 Women can use an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/encryption-apps-apple-imessage-meta-whatsapp-telegram-11654293068"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end-to-end encrypted messaging platform }}}\uc2 , the \uc2 Electronic Frontier Foundation\uc2 , a nonprofit digital rights group, says and recommends Signal.\par \par \uc2 Women also can check the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-period-tracker-apps-treat-your-data-and-what-that-means-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11655561595"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 health and privacy policies on their period-tracker app }}}\uc2 . Some are more privacy friendly than others, The Wall Street Journal previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, women who want their search histories to be private can use a search engine such as \uc2 DuckDuckGo\uc2  that, unlike \uc2 Google\uc2 , doesn't save or share user search histories.\par \par \uc2 "We are not yet sure how companies may respond to law-enforcement requests for any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related data," the \uc2 Electronic Frontier Foundation\uc2  said. "But you can do a lot to control who you are giving your information to, what kind of data they get, and how it might be connected to the rest of your digital life."\par \par \uc2 Write to Meghan Bobrowsky at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:meghan.bobrowsky@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 meghan.bobrowsky@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-tech-data-privacy-answers-11656520992"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 States That Ban \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Could Want Your Data. Here's What to Know. }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220629ei6t00439\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
