\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling Prompts Privacy Guidelines From Biden Administration; New guidelines emphasize limits federal rules place on disclosure of patient data as protected by HIPAA, the health-privacy law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Wilde Mathews \par \uc2 1,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par 23:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration released new patient-privacy guidelines, seeking to spell out federal protections for personal medical information that law-enforcement officials could use to investigate potential violations of state antiabortion laws.\par \par \uc2 The new guidelines come in the wake of last week's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court decision  }}}\uc2  overturning Roe v. Wade, which is leading some states to implement laws limiting or outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The guidelines, issued Wednesday by the Department of Health and Human Services' Office for Civil Rights, emphasize the limits that federal rules place on disclosure of patient data, seeking to carve out relatively narrow conditions under which healthcare entities could be allowed to provide patient information under requirements of a state law.\par \par \uc2 For instance, the guidance document says, a hospital employee who suspected a patient of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a state where it is illegal couldn't report that to law-enforcement personnel unless a state law specifically required such reporting.\par \par \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  has called the court decision despicable and said his department would do all it can to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 In a separate guidance echoing longstanding concerns from some privacy advocates, \uc2 HHS\uc2  listed types of data that aren't protected under the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act, or HIPAA, including most data collected by phones and apps such as those that some women use {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-period-tracker-apps-treat-your-data-and-what-that-means-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11655561595"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to track their periods  }}}\uc2 , internet search history and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/tech-health-firms-race-to-help-consumers-manage-personal-data-11554197400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other digital breadcrumbs  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Health-privacy experts said that even with the new guidelines, federal rules leave substantial openings for state laws to require healthcare providers to disclose some patient information. "If people are expecting HIPAA to adequately protect their health information from state antiabortion laws, they are sorely mistaken," said Joy Pritts, a consultant who is a former health-privacy official. "HIPAA often defers to state law," she said, and the new federal guidance doesn't change that.\par \par \uc2 It couldn't be determined what might happen if state courts and law-enforcement agencies {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/uncertainty-over-abortion-access-grows-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656357143"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seek patient data  }}}\uc2  while enforcing antiabortion laws. Privacy experts said such situations would likely generate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-judges-are-in-spotlight-after-supreme-court-overrules-roe-v-wade-11656444559"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court battles in states  }}}\uc2  that would define how the rules work in practice.\par \par \uc2 "All of it is up in the air right now," said Kirk Nahra, an attorney at \uc2 WilmerHale\uc2  who focuses on privacy and cybersecurity. "This is designed to clarify some of the ambiguity, but it is not intended to, and won't, clarify all of it." Many questions would hinge on specifics of state laws, rather than the federal privacy rule, he said.\par \par \uc2 HIPAA generally protects patient privacy, in addition to some states' own privacy requirements. HIPAA applies to physicians and healthcare companies, including insurers and employer-health plans. It includes significant exceptions that allow disclosure under certain circumstances, including, in many cases, a state statute or court order.\par \par \uc2 Seventeen states have criminal antiabortion laws, either dating from before Roe v. Wade or triggered by its demise, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  , but when they will go into effect varies. Some have been tied up in court since the Supreme Court's decision, while a Michigan antiabortion statute, not included in the Kaiser tally, was earlier blocked by a court. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
