\par\par \uc2 Wonder Land\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Bullhorn Politics of Roe v. Wade; Democrats always seem on the edge of pushing politics into a state of civil unrest.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Henninger \par \uc2 895 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par 22:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 At times like these, when an issue has pushed American political life to a white heat, some sage often steps forth to restate a truism: All politics is local. Not anymore. Not when Sen. Elizabeth Warren, Democrat from Massachusetts, demands that in response to a "national health emergency," President Biden set up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on federal lands (an idea that Rep. \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  unfortunately called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/seven-ways-democrats-can-fight-back-against-roe-v-wade-being-overturned-aoc-1719398"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "the babiest of baby steps"}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 For the progressives running the Democratic Party, all politics is national. Whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand, gun bans, eliminating fossil fuels, whatever, the view of the Democrats is that the locals (rhymes with yokels) are just along for the ride. Get over it. And if progressives don't get what they want, they head to the streets.\par \par \uc2 After the demonstrations and end-of-days statements from elected officials following the Supreme Court's Dobbs case, one almost forgets that the decision came with a reasoned opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , with concurrences by Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Days before, Justice Thomas provided a 56-page explanation for affirming the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 But who cares about the details of judicial opinions anymore?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tomselliott/status/1541760654243143680"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hillary Clinton's}}}\uc2  reductio ad absurdum on Justice Thomas this week: "He's been a person of grievance for as long as I have known him\u8212\'20\'14resentment, grievance, anger."\par \par \uc2 After a draft of Justice Alito's opinion was leaked, sidewalk protesters besieged his and his colleagues' homes. A high fence rings the Supreme Court building and won't come down anytime soon. The fence and the site where rioters breached the Capitol building are D.C.'s newest tourist attractions.\par \par \uc2 We'll focus on two statements in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/19-1392_6j37.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Justice Alito's decision}}}\uc2  that take the discussion past \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if that's possible.\par \par \uc2 The opinion quotes from Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 's dissent in Planned Parenthood v. Casey (1992). Scalia wrote that Roe v. Wade "destroyed the compromises of the past, rendered compromise impossible for the future, and required the entire issue to be resolved uniformly, at the national level" (my emphasis).\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito described what Roe did to the country's social cohesion. It "sparked a national controversy that has embittered our political culture for a half century." That is, Roe caused America's long culture wars.\par \par \uc2 "Culture wars" was a term conservatives coined to describe the post-Roe conflict between traditional beliefs and progressive heterodoxy. The previously nonpolitical Christian right emerged. The left has never worried much about the half century of cultural embitterment described by Justice Alito. It has dismissed the battles over the culture as a political invention or an obsession of right-wing extremists (known more recently as the "deplorables").\par \par \uc2 A stark irony sits in the middle of this week's uproar: Obergefell v. Hodges. That is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/14pdf/14-556_3204.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court's 5-4 decision}}}\uc2  in 2015 recognizing the legal validity of gay marriage. Many Americans disagreed with it. But it was accepted. No fence went up around the court. Scalia's concerns about the death of compromise looked premature. And it won't be overturned.\par \par \uc2 But about a month after Obergefell, the transgender rights issue erupted, focusing\u8212\'20\'14incredible in retrospect\u8212\'20\'14on bathroom access. Despite Obergefell's welcome modus vivendi, the culture war reignited, extending more recently even to primary education. Which is why Justice Alito could cite a bitter politics at 50 years, and still going.\par \par \uc2 For progressive Democrats, every waking moment is Armageddon. Aimee Allison, founder of She the People, says {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/critics-inside-and-outside-white-house-push-biden-for-more-forceful-abortion-response-11656447283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "the future of the Democratic Party is at stake."}}}\uc2  And maybe it is.\par \par \uc2 Public protests are part of politics in any free country, a First Amendment right repeatedly affirmed by the out-of-favor Supreme Court. But the average American voter must be wondering whether the Democratic Party's politics is about anything other than these street protests.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported this week that congressional progressives are upset that Mr. Biden said people should express opposition to the court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision by voting in November, which Reps. Ocasio-Cortez and \uc2 Cori Bush{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/06/27/democrats-angry-party-leaders/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say is inadequate}}}\uc2 . According to the Post, some Democratic lawmakers and activists "criticize the notion that it is on voters to turn out in November when they say Democrats are unwilling to push boundaries and upend the system in defense of hard-won civil liberties."\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats in office today were community organizers. Going into the street to "upend the system" with an apparently unlimited rights agenda is what professional activists do for a living. This now-constant style of bullhorn politics\u8212\'20\'14with its shaken fists and denunciations of normal deliberation and process\u8212\'20\'14is defining the public's impression of who the Democrats are.\par \par \uc2 By aligning so closely with street protests and apocalyptic claims about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , climate and gender, the Democrats have created a significant perception problem for the party heading into the midterm elections and perhaps for years. With these allies, the party always seem to be living at the edge of civil disturbance. Now they have repudiated the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14in toto.\par \par \uc2 Come November, I expect most voters will elect not to live in a state of constant political rage and moral chaos.\par \par \uc2 Write henninger@wsj.com.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/politics-abortion-ocasio-cortez-debate-roe-dobbs-decision-supreme-court-life-11656535292"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Bullhorn Politics of Roe v. Wade}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220629ei6t006y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
