\par\par \uc2 Business\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 P&G\uc2 , Target to Cover Employee Travel for Abortions; Big employers in Midwest states join tech giants and big banks in adding benefit after Supreme Court decision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep and Sarah Nassauer \par \uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 June 2022\par 20:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Procter & Gamble Co\uc2 . and \uc2 Target Corp\uc2 . said they would cover travel expenses for employees who can't access abortions near where they live, joining other companies that have taken the step after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court overturned the constitutional right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 P&G\uc2 , unlike many of the technology and banking giants that had previously announced similar policies, is based in a Midwestern state with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. A federal judge in Ohio issued an order Friday allowing the state's six-week ban to take effect. A few other big employers in Ohio, including grocer \uc2 Kroger Co\uc2 ., have also said they would provide benefits for workers who need {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to travel for abortions }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 P&G\uc2 , which is based in Cincinnati and has 26,000 U.S. employees, said Tuesday its new policy is an extension of coverage already in place when travel is necessary to treat conditions such as organ transplants. Starting Jan. 1, this will include most covered medical procedures, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if a treatment isn't available within 50 miles of where an employee lives. About a half-dozen of \uc2 P&G\uc2 's manufacturing facilities are in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 "This is an important issue to many people, and we recognize their broad range of views," a \uc2 P&G\uc2  spokeswoman said. "As we have for many years, \uc2 P&G\uc2  supports our employees in having access to a wide range of healthcare options so they can determine what's best for them and their families."\par \par \uc2 Target, which has around 450,000 U.S. employees, said in a note sent Monday to staff that starting in July the company will reimburse travel expenses related to any healthcare that is needed and covered, but not available in a worker's community.\par \par \uc2 Target\uc2  started reviewing the policy a few months ago, the company's human-resources chief, \uc2 Melissa Kremer\uc2 , wrote to workers. "This effort became even more relevant as we learned about the Supreme Court's ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given that it would impact access to healthcare in some states," Ms. Kremer wrote.\par \par \uc2 Target\uc2  is based in Minneapolis. Last week the state's governor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mn.gov/governor/news/executiveorders.jsp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed an executive order }}}\uc2  that aims to protect the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and recipients from antiabortion laws in neighboring states. \uc2 Target\uc2  has around 2,000 stores across the country.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , which has around 420,000 U.S. workers and is also based in Cincinnati, said it provides employees enrolled in health plans with up to $4,000 to cover travel costs for a range of medical treatments, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fertility treatments. A spokeswoman said \uc2 Kroger\uc2  informed covered employees about the benefit on June 14 but didn't say how long the benefit has been in effect.\par \par \uc2 Other consumer companies that have announced similar moves recently include \uc2 CVS Health Corp\uc2 ., \uc2 Estee Lauder Cos\uc2 . and \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods Inc\uc2 . Earlier this year, after a leak of the draft Roe v. Wade decision, companies including \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 ., \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2 . said they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-companies-paying-travel-costs-for-abortions-tesla-amazon-citi-on-small-but-growing-list-11651873562"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 would reimburse travel for medical services }}}\uc2  including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Bank of America Corp\uc2 ., \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 ., \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2 . recently told employees that they will pay for travel if people have to travel far from home for abortions. The banks, which employ tens of thousands of people around the country, described their decisions as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-roe-v-wade-ruling-companies-reimbursing-medical-and-travel-expenses-for-abortion-risk-blowback-11656109337"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ensuring equal access to healthcare }}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/p-g-target-to-cover-employee-travel-for-abortions-11656449107"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 P&G, Target to Cover Employee Travel for Abortions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220628ei6s006mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
