\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , a critic of the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade, is calling for an expansion of the high court. PHOTO: Allison Shelley for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing out of the White House almost feels like we're dealing at the margins of the problem instead of attacking the problem head-on," said Aimee Allison, founder of She the People, describing the interest within the party to forcefully fight the ruling. "This is a time to lead like the future of the Democratic Party is at stake."\par \par \uc2 Progressive activists have bristled at comments from Mr. Biden and others that Democrats should respond to the ruling by voting in November. Leaders of the party should take action now, activists argue, faulting officials for not codifying Roe into law sooner.\par \par \uc2 The ruling has thrust the president into the center of an issue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-at-center-of-fight-over-abortion-after-career-of-reservations-11651921202"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 he has long approached with reservations }}}\uc2 , and on which he has previously espoused more centrist views than many other Democrats. For most of his presidency, he had not spoken the word "\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Some in the administration have questioned whether Mr. Biden's discomfort with the subject is keeping the White House from a stronger response, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks between the leak of the draft opinion and the final ruling, Mr. Biden held no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related events. He didn't give a speech, attend a rally or meet with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers or advocates. White House officials repeatedly said during that period they wanted to wait for a final ruling before detailing their next steps on protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , a more vocal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate than Mr. Biden, in an interview Monday with CNN, said the administration would do "everything within our power" to ensure women have access to medication and freedom of travel, echoing the president's previous comments.\par \par \uc2 But she didn't detail new policy proposals in response to the ruling, avoided making a case for eliminating the filibuster in the Senate even though she has supported the idea in the past, and declined to commit to a push by activists and some progressive lawmakers to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access on federal land\u8212\'20\'14in and near states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned. "It's not right now what we are discussing," she said.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  reiterated those reservations Tuesday. "We understand the proposal is well intentioned but here's the thing, it could actually put women and providers at risk," she said, warning that the proposal wouldn't shield women from being prosecuted.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists want Ms. Harris to go further in explaining next steps, but people familiar with the discussions say Ms. Harris doesn't want to get ahead of the president or the administration's message on policy questions, though she has been pushing for more specificity in actions and messaging from top White House officials.\par \par \uc2 "The President and the Vice President are partners working closely together to explore a wide range of options to protect women's rights and defend access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care," said Harris spokeswoman Kirsten Allen.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Biden administration, there has been frustration from some officials with the pace and lack of details on how Mr. Biden intends to proceed on the issue, according to people familiar with the matter. Some administration officials worried that the president and his senior aides were underestimating the level of frustration being directed at them by progressives for not mounting a more forceful response, some of the people said.\par \par \uc2 White House officials have been discussing possible policy responses for weeks, according to administration officials and others with knowledge of the matter, including whether to declare a public health emergency. A White House official said the administration is discussing a range of additional policy options.\par \par \uc2 Some officials said privately that their options for taking immediate, sweeping action are limited and worried that progressives were raising expectations for what could be accomplished. Aides have cautioned that no policies from the executive branch can fill the void left by the court's decision.\par \par \uc2 Though they had long been planning for the ruling, officials were caught off guard when the decision was announced on Friday, aides said, because many thought it wouldn't be issued until this week.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Becerra pushed back on criticism that the administration hasn't moved swiftly or forcefully enough. "It was a long decision and it did upend 50 years of precedent and you want to make sure what you do is within the confines of the law," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's ruling Friday overturned Roe, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the decision, a majority of the court said there was no provision in the Constitution that implicitly protected the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , returning the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has said it would take steps to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, saying women should be able to travel to another state for the procedure. It has also said it would protect access to contraception and medications used to treat miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What would you like to see from the Biden administration following the Supreme Court's reversal of Roe v. Wade? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Shaunna Thomas, the executive director and co-founder of UltraViolet, noted the administration had a roughly eight-week advance warning of how the court might rule because of the leak of a draft opinion published by Politico.\par \par \uc2 "They promised a whole-of-government response and I think we were expecting to see the details of that, the scale of that, right away," Ms. Thomas said. "It's possible, plausible, that they are planning to do more. I think the American public\u8212\'20\'14they need to see that."\par \par \uc2 The White House is trying to refocus attention on Republicans, pointing to suggestions from House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy (R., Calif.) and former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  that the GOP would seek nationwide bans or restrictions on abortions if the party won control of Congress in the fall elections.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has sought to use the ruling as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022/card/harris-calls-for-voters-to-boost-candidates-who-back-abortion-rights-uBFfYteWBK6D8bKlpSeA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catalyst for Democratic turnout in the elections }}}\uc2 , saying a large response from voters would give the party the seats it needs in Congress to codify the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights of Roe into law.\par \par \uc2 "This goes one of two ways: We either have a House and Senate that puts Roe into federal law, or a House and Senate that push ultra-MAGA policies that strip women of their rights in all 50 states. Congress will either protect women everywhere, or strip away their rights everywhere," Jen Klein, the director of the White House's Gender Policy Council, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McCarthy praised the ruling when it was handed down, saying it "affirmed that the power to protect unborn life is 'returned to the people and their elected representatives.' The people have won a victory."\par \par \uc2 Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (R., Ky.) played down the likelihood of a congressional ban during an appearance Monday in his home state. Such action on the issue is "almost entirely likely to occur at the state level," he said.\par \par \uc2 "Let me tell you why: In the Senate, most things require 60 votes," Mr. McConnell said. "Neither side of this issue has come anywhere close to having 60 votes."\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Armour contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Ken Thomas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:ken.thomas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ken.thomas@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Tarini Parti at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Tarini.Parti@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tarini.Parti@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Andrew Restuccia at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:andrew.restuccia@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 andrew.restuccia@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/critics-inside-and-outside-white-house-push-biden-for-more-forceful-abortion-response-11656447283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Critics Inside and Outside White House Push Biden for More Forceful \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Response }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220628ei6s006bt\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
