\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 What Does Overturning Roe v. Wade Mean? What to Know About the Supreme Court  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling; After changes in the court's membership, conservative justices built a majority to overrule precedent they viewed as egregiously wrong\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 974 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 June 2022\par 16:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Friday upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Mississippi and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliminated the constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  that was first established in the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision. Here is a look at the court's journey from establishing the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly 50 years ago to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 withdrawing it now }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What is Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 The Roe case involved a pregnant Texas woman who sought a judicial declaration that state laws making it a crime to procure or attempt an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were unconstitutional. The Supreme Court agreed by a 7-2 vote and found that the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a fundamental liberty protected by the 14th Amendment, which says the states can't deprive individuals of life, liberty or property without due process of law. The decision was written by a Nixon appointee, Justice Harry Blackmun.\par \par \uc2 The case was the product of a time in which the court took an expansive view of individual liberties protected by the Constitution. The ruling built upon other high court decisions of that era, including Griswold v. Connecticut in 1965, which struck down a state ban on contraceptives.\par \par \uc2 Why was Roe so controversial?\par \par \uc2 One reason of course is the subject matter: \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an issue on which passions run high. More broadly in the legal world, Roe became part of a decadeslong debate over when and whether it's appropriate for the Supreme Court to recognize new individual rights under broadly written provisions in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Roe didn't become a lightning rod overnight. It wasn't until the Reagan administration that the decision became a rallying cry for conservatives who made it a long-term aspiration to reshape the courts with judges and justices who would reject Roe-era thinking.\par \par \uc2 How is it that the court can just change its mind?\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's respect for its own precedents has been a hallmark of the U.S. legal system. The court as a general matter has believed that it is important to abide by past rulings, even if later groups of justices might have decided a case differently. That approach promotes stability in the law and avoids societal disruptions that can come with giant swings in how the court decides an issue.\par \par \uc2 That said, the court does overrule past precedents on occasion if a majority of justices believe they have especially good justifications for doing so. Some of the court's most famous decisions have come in cases where it has changed its mind, such as Brown v. Board of Education in 1954, which ended racial segregation in public schools and overruled Plessy v. Ferguson, the 1896 ruling that allowed state segregation laws.\par \par \uc2 Justices have long argued over what special factors should be present for the Supreme Court to overrule a past decision. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 That kind of jousting was a central theme }}}\uc2  in the decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 Had Roe been under fire before now?\par \par \uc2 Yes, especially after a series of appointments to the Supreme Court by Presidents \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and George H.W. Bush in the 1980s and early 1990s. The 1992 case of Planned Parenthood v. Casey, examining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in Pennsylvania, gave the court the opportunity to overrule Roe, but three of those GOP appointees, Justices \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 , \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  and David Souter, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-foes-to-take-new-aim-at-roe-v-wade-1530223760"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jointly wrote a controlling opinion }}}\uc2  that instead chose to reaffirm the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those justices cited the importance of respecting precedent and said overruling Roe would inflict "unnecessary damage to the court's legitimacy, and to the nation's commitment to the rule of law."\par \par \uc2 Why did Roe fall now?\par \par \uc2 In large part because the high court's composition has changed. The three Republican appointees who controlled the Casey decision have all retired. The court became more conservative when Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 author of the Dobbs ruling }}}\uc2 , succeeded Justice O'Connor in 2006, and again in 2018 when Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , another member of the Dobbs majority, succeeded Justice Kennedy, a maverick conservative who had long been a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-foes-to-take-new-aim-at-roe-v-wade-1530223760"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 key vote for preserving \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the most pronounced ideological shift came when Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , a leading liberal and champion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, died in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/death-of-justice-ruth-bader-ginsburg-affects-everything-in-last-stretch-of-campaign-11600481798"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 weeks before the 2020 election }}}\uc2 . Then-President \uc2 Donald Trump{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/amy-coney-barrett-set-to-be-confirmed-as-supreme-court-justice-11603721947"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quickly filled the vacancy }}}\uc2  with Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , a conservative whose appointment {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-we-know-about-amy-coney-barrett-views-opinions-and-experience-11601154646"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 put Roe in deep jeopardy }}}\uc2 . She provided another key vote in the Dobbs ruling.\par \par \uc2 Who voted to overturn Roe?\par \par \uc2 Justices Alito, Kavanaugh, Barrett, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  voted in favor of upholding a Mississippi law that prohibited \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, but he didn't support overruling Roe.\par \par \uc2 The court's three liberal justices\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14dissented.\par \par \uc2 Will \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  be illegal in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 It depends on where you live. With the Supreme Court withdrawing the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , states now have wide latitude to restrict or allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they see fit. Roughly half the states are expected to prohibit many or most abortions, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 state laws are evolving by the day }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 State courts could play a larger role in determining what types of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations are permissible. It is also possible the Supreme Court could face future \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases even after the overruling of Roe, particularly if some states adopt total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that make no exceptions for rape, incest or the health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Brent.Kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brent.Kendall@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-does-overturning-roe-v-wade-mean-explained-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What Does Overturning Roe v. Wade Mean? What to Know About the Supreme Court \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o0035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
