\par \par \uc2 The message inside St. Patrick's Cathedral in Manhattan avoided direct reference to the Supreme Court's ruling, while a sign outside addressed it.. PHOTO: GINGER ADAMS OTIS/THE WALL STREET JOURNAL\par \par \uc2 Lauren Holtzblatt, co-senior rabbi of \uc2 Adas Israel Congregation\uc2  in Washington, D.C., said some congregants felt fear or stress, especially given Jewish precepts that put a pregnant woman's health ahead of that of her fetus.\par \par \uc2 "I say it is OK to feel grief," said Rabbi Holtzblatt, who spoke at Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 's memorial service. But "if we're wallowing in despair, we're not going to be able to help other people.\par \par \uc2 In the decadeslong fight to overturn Roe v. Wade, churches and other Christian organizations opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  often played an outsize role, and some are gearing up to continue advocating for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions at the state and local level.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , among the country's largest Protestant denominations, said it would seek a ban on most abortions while supporting crisis pregnancy centers and foster-care and adoption agencies.\par \par \uc2 Still, many congregations are split on aspects of the debate. At the Washington church often attended by Justice Kavanaugh, parishioner Jim Cruller, a commercial real-estate agent who was baptized and married in the church, said he didn't agree with the court's rejection of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think overturning it is going to unify people," Mr. Cruller said.\par \par \uc2 Another parishioner, risk-management consultant Ginienne Santoro, said she supported the Roman Catholic Church's stand against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though it is unpopular in liberal areas such as Washington.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of anger and frustration towards Catholics and women who are against abortions, and that's not a good thing," said Ms. Santoro.\par \par \uc2 Majorities of U.S. Christians across several categories\u8212\'20\'14including white evangelical Protestants, Black Protestants and Catholics\u8212\'20\'14report positions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  falling somewhere between absolute prohibition and unrestricted access, the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  found in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2022/05/06/americas-abortion-quandary/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a survey of U.S. adults }}}\uc2  conducted in March.\par \par \uc2 Among white evangelical Protestants\u8212\'20\'14the group generally most opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Pew's survey\u8212\'20\'14almost a quarter said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly or always legal, while 53% said it should be mostly illegal but allowed in some circumstances. About two-thirds of Black Protestants said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly or always legal, Pew found.\par \par \uc2 In general, Christians in the survey were more likely to favor legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when a pregnancy is the result of rape or threatens a woman's health.\par \par \uc2 "The vast majority of Americans have nuanced ideas about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ziad Munson, a \uc2 Lehigh University\uc2  sociology professor who has written books about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and politics.\par \par \uc2 Kristin Kobes Du Mez, a historian who studies religion and politics at Calvin University in Grand Rapids, Mich., said Christians opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't necessarily ready for the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-opponents-consider-how-far-to-press-after-end-of-roe-v-wade-11656273349?mod=Searchresults_pos1&page=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 post-Roe world }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There is no unity at all as far as what comes next, from what I can tell," said Dr. Du Mez, author of "Jesus and John Wayne," a book that describes the role of white evangelicals in Mr. Trump's political success. "There's a lot of division within evangelicalism\u8212\'20\'14but a lot of joy."\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Ginger Adams Otis and Francis X. Rocca contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Theo Francis at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:theo.francis@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 theo.francis@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Kate King at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Kate.King@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kate.King@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/churches-move-cautiously-on-abortion-and-call-for-calm-post-roe-11656268174"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Churches Move Cautiously on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  and Call for Calm Post-Roe  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220626ei6q001p5\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
