\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b \uc2 After Roe v. Wade Ruling, Employers Weigh In on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and Benefits\u8212\'20\'14Cautiously; Businesses with health plans covering  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  now are weighing whether and how to pay for employees to travel to a state where the procedure is legal\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Wilde Mathews, Kathryn Dill, Emily Glazer and Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,687 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 02:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision overturning Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  creates new challenges for employers and health insurers covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, especially whether and how to pay for travel to states where the procedure remains available.\par \par \uc2 Companies including \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2  ., \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2  . and \uc2 Amazon.com Inc\uc2  . have said they would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-companies-paying-travel-costs-for-abortions-tesla-amazon-citi-on-small-but-growing-list-11651873562"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reimburse travel for medical services including \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Just Eat Takeaway.com N.V .'s \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  and \uc2 Dick's Sporting Goods\uc2  said they would provide up to $4,000 to cover expenses traveling for services not available in their home state.\par \par \uc2 "We recognize people feel passionately about this topic\u8212\'20\'14and that there are teammates and athletes who will not agree with this decision," Dick's Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Lauren Hobart\uc2  wrote in an email to employees on Friday.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 How should employers and insurers cover reproductive care in a post-Roe era? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Thorny benefits issues pose a fresh challenge for many companies already struggling to navigate divisive social and political issues.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-debate-puts-corporate-initiatives-in-the-spotlight-11651687778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Companies that choose to cover \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14and any travel necessary to get one\u8212\'20\'14have to balance alienating employees agitating for their employers to take a stand versus the risk of legal attacks or political blowback in states that have banned the procedure.\par \par \uc2 "Benefits are suddenly starting to become very political, and it's forcing employers to take a position," said \uc2 Michael Turpin\uc2  , executive vice president at USI Holdings Corp., an insurance brokerage.\par \par \uc2 There are also questions of how much employers, especially smaller ones, can afford to invest, according to human-resources executives, CEOs and insurance experts.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, some executives quickly issued public statements regarding the Supreme Court's decision. \uc2 Yelp Inc\uc2  . CEO \uc2 Jeremy Stoppelman\uc2  , who has been vocal on \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a statement that business leaders needed to speak out against a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans expected to be triggered by the decision. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
