\par \par \uc2 A patient without health insurance could expect to pay a median $560 for a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2020, a recent study found, and $575 for a first-trimester procedural \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . PHOTO: Michael Noble Jr. for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Many employers, especially the largest national companies, have been covering the procedures. Some 10% of American workers are enrolled in plans offered by employers that specifically excluded or tightly restricted it, according to a 2019 survey by the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 As it became clear that the Supreme Court would overturn Roe, many other businesses have been working quietly behind the scenes to have a response ready to roll out when the court acted, according to benefits consultants.\par \par \uc2 Some 14% of large employers surveyed beginning in early June said they had a travel benefit in place, while another 25% said they were considering it, according to responses from nearly 300 employers to a survey being conducted by benefits consultant \uc2 Mercer LLC\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Employers with at least 20,000 workers were the most likely to provide a travel and lodging benefit for employees traveling to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the survey said.\par \par \uc2 Major insurers that administer health benefits for big employers, including \uc2 UnitedHealth Group Inc\uc2  .'s UnitedHealthcare, \uc2 Cigna Corp\uc2  . and \uc2 CVS Health Corp\uc2  .'s \uc2 Aetna\uc2  , had been confirming to self-insured clients they can likely facilitate travel offerings, benefits consultants say.\par \par \uc2 Cigna\uc2  and CVS said they are looking to support the coverage needs of their clients and customers. \uc2 UnitedHealth Group\uc2  said it remains committed to helping members and patients get access to their benefits and services.\par \par \uc2 Draft language that \uc2 UnitedHealthcare\uc2  recently sent to an employer for potential use in its plan document describing covered benefits, which was viewed by The Wall Street Journal, said the health plan would cover travel and lodging to a nearby state to get a covered healthcare service that isn't legally available where the employee lives.\par \par \uc2 Given the sensitivities, many employers have been parsing state and local laws that relate to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, as well as administration of company health benefits that are self-funded versus covered under a fully-insured plan, insurance experts, CEOs and HR executives said.\par \par \uc2 "Companies are figuring this out as it happens. It is a complex landscape ahead of us," said Michele Ruiz, CEO of BiasSync, a Los Angeles-based software maker.\par \par \uc2 Employers will likely be careful in how they communicate about their plans, and how they design any new travel benefit. They will have to navigate various wrinkles, including that a generous travel benefit might result in taxable income for patients.\par \par \uc2 Also, to avoid estranging anyone, companies may structure the benefit to be broader than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , benefits experts said.\par \par \uc2 "It's not singling out \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is included," said \uc2 Brian Marcotte\uc2  , a healthcare consultant and former CEO of the Business Group on Health. Companies will "make the communication as an enhancement to their travel benefit, so they are addressing it but not directly, and not creating an uproar."\par \par \uc2 Companies that say they are committed to providing such benefits say the legal complexities of ensuring coverage are fraught.\par \par \uc2 Yet covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for employees who live in states where it is banned, and particularly facilitating access with services like travel, could create legal jeopardy for employers and the insurers that administer their health plans\u8212\'20\'14and even potentially for individual employees, corporate officers or directors, legal experts said.\par \par \uc2 "You could have someone who goes after them for aiding and abetting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said David Cohen, a law professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  . "Employers are going to be put in the legal crosshairs, too."\par \par \uc2 Before the new Supreme Court decision, governments had created a patchwork of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -coverage rules.\par \par \uc2 Many states' Medicaid plans severely limit coverage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while federal programs like Medicare pay for it under very limited circumstances.\par \par \uc2 States have also restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in private insurance, with 11 of them banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  coverage in all plans they regulate, according to the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  . Yet state insurance rules don't apply to the type of coverage that is most common among midsize and large employers, known as self-funded plans.\par \par \uc2 Self-funded plans, in which employers pay workers' health claims with their own money, are regulated at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Federal regulations for self-insured plans don't spell out specific rules around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , lawyers say. A federal antidiscrimination statute requires all employers to provide health coverage to pay for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the life of the mother is at risk from the pregnancy, said \uc2 Seth Perretta\uc2  , an attorney at Groom Law Group who advises employers and health insurers.\par \par \uc2 If states do decide to enforce or enact laws that affect self-insured employers, they will set up court clashes. Employers will likely argue that state authority is pre-empted by federal laws that govern their plans, but lawyers said they don't know what will happen if states seek to use criminal laws against employers.\par \par \uc2 Criminal laws that are broadly written may be able to overcome a pre-emption challenge, lawyers said.\par \par \uc2 "This is going to raise a lot of constitutional and legal issues," said Kerri Willis, a senior vice president at \uc2 Aon PLC\uc2  's U.S. Health Solutions unit. "We're going to be in years of litigation over these issues."\par \par \uc2 Bank of America Corp\uc2  ., \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2  ., \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group Inc\uc2  . and \uc2 JPMorgan Chase & Co\uc2  . already cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for workers who have their medical benefits. The banks recently told employees that they will pay for travel if people have to travel far from home to be treated. The banks, which employ tens of thousands of people around the country, described their decisions as ensuring equal access to healthcare.\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said it would start covering travel costs for all covered healthcare procedures on July 1, according to an internal memo. The bank had previously covered travel expenses only for limited cases, such as organ transplants and bariatric surgery. The benefit covers the cost of traveling more than 50 miles.\par \par \uc2 Heather Haddon contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anna Wilde Mathews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:anna.mathews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anna.mathews@wsj.com  }}}\uc2 , Kathryn Dill at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Kathryn.Dill@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kathryn.Dill@wsj.com  }}}\uc2 , Emily Glazer at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:emily.glazer@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emily.glazer@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Chip Cutter at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:chip.cutter@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chip.cutter@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Chip Cutter at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-roe-v-wade-ruling-companies-reimbursing-medical-and-travel-expenses-for-abortion-risk-blowback-11656109337"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 After Roe v. Wade Ruling, Employers Weigh In on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  and Benefits\u8212\'20\'14Cautiously  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o007n1\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
