\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Opinion Revisits Same-Sex Marriage, Contraception; Many conservatives say prospect of court taking another look at widely accepted decisions is remote, calling them more settled than Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Wolfe \par \uc2 1,079 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 12:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14When the Supreme Court removed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 constitutional right to an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , most of the justices in the majority focused on what they described as the "egregiously wrong" ruling that established that right in Roe v. Wade in 1973.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  took a broader view. In his opinion concurring with the majority, he wrote that if the legal underpinnings of Roe v. Wade were wrong, then so were the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-v-wade-overturned-supreme-court-11656110804"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 underpinnings of other rights }}}\uc2  not enumerated in the Constitution that the court recognized in recent decades. They include the right of married couples to use contraception, the right to same-sex romantic relationships and, in 2015, the right to same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas's stance raised the prospect that new challenges would soon be mounted against those rights, keying off the decision in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 The possibility that Friday's ruling will have much greater applicability is an argument central to the dissent by the three liberal-leaning justices. Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan wrote: "Today's opinion will fuel the fight to get contraception, and any other issues with a moral dimension, out of the Fourteenth Amendment and into state legislatures."\par \par \uc2 Latest Updates\par \par \uc2 Get the latest updates on the overturning of Roe at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WSJ's Live Blog }}}\par \par \uc2 Read the decision: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization }}}\par \par \uc2 A number of conservative legal scholars argued, as Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  did in his opinion, that Roe was a special case. "Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the justice wrote.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Parshall Perry, a lawyer at \uc2 the conservative Heritage Foundation\uc2  who worked in the Trump administration, said she sees no appetite among the other conservatives on the bench for revisiting rights that have become more accepted and entrenched in American life than Roe ever was.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think there are the votes for re-examination of those other precedents," she said. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is inherently different\u8230\'20\'26Roe has always stuck out as a particularly egregious decision."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Perry said repealing Roe has always been a singular focus for conservatives because of how poorly reasoned they think the ruling was and because they see it as bearing on the right not only of a consenting individual, but also on the fetus's right to a life.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Perry said she expects that the court will be petitioned to revisit the issues in cases mentioned by Justice Thomas but that the current composition of the court suggests those petitions will be quickly turned away. "I'm virtually certain we won't see the court granting them," Ms. Perry said.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  wrote a concurring opinion that distanced him from Justice Thomas.\par \par \uc2 "I emphasize what the Court today states: Overruling Roe does not mean the overruling of those precedents, and does not threaten or cast doubt on those precedents," Justice Kavanaugh wrote.\par \par \uc2 In his concurrence, Justice Thomas referred specifically to three cases that he considered to be on the same shaky constitutional footing as Roe: Griswold v. Connecticut (1965), which established the right of married couples to use contraception; Lawrence v. Texas (2003), which protects the right to same-sex romantic relationships; and Obergefell v. Hodges (2015), which establishes the right to same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas said those cases\u8212\'20\'14which some call "substantive due-process" cases\u8212\'20\'14were wrongly decided because the court interpreted the 14th Amendment, adopted in 1868 to assure "due process of law" and "equal protection of the laws," as establishing rights that the nation's founders never intended.\par \par \uc2 The justices therefore have a duty, he wrote, to "correct the error" established in those precedents.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas's views on those cases are no surprise to those familiar with his judicial thinking, said David Upham, a legal scholar at the \uc2 University of Dallas\uc2  who publicly disagreed with the 2015 decision that recognized same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 "He is saying things he has said in the past," Mr. Upham said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Upham said that, as social mores have shifted, the political will for revisiting Griswold has vanished.\par \par \uc2 "The contraceptive decisions are untouchable," Mr. Upham said. "It's not about constitutional law. It's about the political reality that there aren't a substantial number of people who would like to see that case revisited."\par \par \uc2 Kris Ullman, president of the conservative Eagle Forum advocacy group, said: "There is a huge difference between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception. This idea that all these other rights are in danger\u8212\'20\'14that's a scare tactic by the left."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What does the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision mean for other granted rights? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Some antiabortion groups, such as Students for Life of America, consider "Plan B" emergency contraceptives and intrauterine devices, or IUDs, to be abortifacients and have pushed to limit access to them. Where that line is drawn in jurisdictions across the country could give rise to legal battles, as could questions of which circumstances of a mother's pregnancy qualify as exceptions to state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 "I do think this opens up new battlegrounds for the court; it could be open season on what rights are protected by the Constitution," said Joseph Thai, a \uc2 University of Oklahoma\uc2  law professor. Conservative views that once were confined to dissenting opinions, he added, "now can become majority opinions if you can count to five votes."\par \par \uc2 "Today, Justice Thomas is an island unto himself," said Jessica Levinson, a professor at Loyola Law School in Los Angeles. "While the majority goes to pains to say this is just about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and nothing else, the majority's reasoning calls into question as to whether that is truly the case. Thomas could soon have visitors on his island."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Upham dismissed that view, saying he sees no desire among lawmakers or the court's conservative justices to push for overturning Obergefell, the same-sex marriage decision.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think it's likely, and I say this as someone who would like to see these precedents revisited," Mr. Upham said.\par \par \uc2 At this point, he said, there is strong support for same-sex marriage among many Americans\u8212\'20\'14and general acquiescence by people who once opposed it.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/clarence-thomass-abortion-opinion-revisits-same-sex-marriage-contraception-11656161978"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Clarence Thomas's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Opinion Revisits Same-Sex Marriage, Contraception }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220625ei6p001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
