\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling Highlights Diverging Paths of Justices \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 ; Chief Justice's vision of Supreme Court stands at odds with majority opinion's author\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jan Wolfe \par \uc2 1,285 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par 09:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has long pursued two goals on the Supreme Court: move its jurisprudence incrementally to the right and insulate the institution from the heat of Washington politics.\par \par \uc2 The court's historic decision on Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to overturn Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  shows how hard that has become.\par \par \uc2 The court overturned the 1973 precedent that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a decision }}}\uc2  written by his fellow Republican-appointed colleague, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , and joined by the court's four other conservatives.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the concurring opinion written by Justice Roberts? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization left Chief Justice Roberts alone with his more incremental approach to the case. He expressed support in a concurring opinion for the Mississippi law banning abortions after 15 weeks, the proximate issue of the court's case. But he sharply disagreed with his fellow conservative justices' wholesale elimination of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right, which had been in place for almost a half-century and affirmed in a post-Roe case, Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v. Casey, in 1992.\par \par \uc2 "The Court's decision to overrule Roe and Casey is a serious jolt to the legal system\u8212\'20\'14regardless of how you view those cases," the chief justice wrote."A narrower decision\u8230\'20\'26would be markedly less unsettling, and nothing more is needed to decide this case."\par \par \uc2 Artemus Ward, a professor at \uc2 Northern Illinois University\uc2  whose research focuses on the Supreme Court, said Chief Justice Roberts was now marginalized "and he's calling out Alito and the other conservatives, saying, 'You didn't need to do this. You didn't need to go this far.'"\par \par \uc2 The chief justice was no less disturbed by the position of the three liberals on the bench. "Both the Court's opinion and the dissent display a relentless freedom from doubt on the legal issue that I cannot share," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The split on Roe v. Wade illustrates the diverging approaches to jurisprudence taken by Chief Justice Roberts, 67 years old, and Justice Alito, 72, since 2005, the year they were both nominated for the court by former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Throughout their careers, Chief Justice Roberts has played the long game, while Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  prefers to move fast and break things, urging maximalist rulings that move the law far to the right," said David Gans, a lawyer at Constitutional Accountability Center, a left-leaning think tank. "These differences play out in Dobbs."\par \par \uc2 When Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  retired from the Supreme Court in 2005, Messrs. Roberts and Alito, both sitting on intermediate appeals courts, were on Mr. Bush's shortlist of potential justices.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove, a senior Bush adviser, wrote in a 2010 book that Mr. Roberts came across during an early job interview as "restrained and guarded." Mr. Bush, impressed by Mr. Roberts's precision and intelligence, concluded he would be a natural "leader on the Court," Mr. Rove wrote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Alito, on the other hand, came across as "painfully shy," Mr. Rove recalled, adding: "But boy, was he smart," noting the judge's ability to explain complicated legal concepts in a clear way.\par \par \uc2 When Chief Justice William Rehnquist died a few weeks after Mr. Roberts had gotten the nod for Justice O'Connor's seat, Mr. Bush nominated him instead for the chief position.\par \par \uc2 After the president's nomination of his friend and White House counsel, Harriet Miers, faltered among criticism particularly from conservatives, Mr. Bush settled on Mr. Alito to fill Justice O'Connor's seat. The move drew praise from antiabortion advocates who had pushed for a staunch conservative.\par \par \uc2 During their first years on the court, Chief Justice Roberts and Justice Alito were closely aligned.\par \par \uc2 In 2007, they joined a majority opinion that upheld a federal ban on late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though seven years earlier the court had struck down an almost identical state law as a violation of Roe. That same year, in another 5-4 case, the two justices both said race can't be a factor in the assignment of children to public schools.\par \par \uc2 Liberal Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , finding himself on the losing side of a number of 5-4 decisions, noted the impact his new conservative colleagues. "It is not often in the law that so few have so quickly changed so much," he wrote in summarizing his dissent to that school decision.\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  (D., N.Y.) said in a 2007 speech that Democrats had been "duped" by Chief Justice Roberts's lofty words about stability and incrementalism during his confirmation hearings. "We were promised an era of gentility and consensus if the affable \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  were confirmed as Chief Justice," Mr. Schumer said. "There is no doubt we were hoodwinked."\par \par \uc2 The chief justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chief-justice-draws-republican-fire-for-supreme-court-health-care-ruling-1435261862"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reframed his legacy }}}\uc2 , however, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/14pdf/14-114_qol1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his 2012 ruling }}}\uc2  that largely upheld then-President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's Affordable Care Act, the law known as Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 Liberals hailed the decision, though seasoned court-watchers saw it as an act of pragmatism. By deferring to Congress, Chief Justice Roberts avoided roiling a major part of the U.S. economy and sidestepped a fight that could have harmed public perceptions of the court.\par \par \uc2 The pivotal vote ushered in an era during which the chief justice {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/chief-justice-roberts-moves-to-man-in-the-middle-on-the-supreme-court-1530569142"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined his liberal colleagues in occasional high-profile rulings }}}\uc2 , while still incrementally moving the court to the right on many fronts. In 2019, the chief justice blocked the Trump's administration's effort to include a question on citizenship in the 2020 census. Then in the span of two weeks in 2020, he voted to strike down a Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, protect the young immigrants known as "Dreamers," and prohibit employment discrimination based on sexual orientation or gender identity\u8212\'20\'14each time joining the court's liberals.\par \par \uc2 "Roberts picks his spots," Mr. Ward said. "In a high-profile case, he will often move to the center. In a high-profile case, he's going to be more concerned about seeking consensus."\par \par \uc2 While the chief justice was polishing his reputation as a consensus-seeker, Justice Alito was burnishing his conservative credentials\u8212\'20\'14and making known his displeasure with Chief Justice Roberts's jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-alito-responds-to-liberal-critics-says-covid-19-is-constitutional-stress-test-11605244977"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an unusually political speech }}}\uc2  to the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  in 2020, Justice Alito expressed dismay at the state of college campuses and workplaces, saying conservatives were being maligned for holding views that recently had been mainstream.\par \par \uc2 "You can't say that marriage is a union between one man and one woman" any more, Justice Alito said. "Until very recently, that's what the vast majority of Americans thought. Now it's considered bigotry."\par \par \uc2 Tensions between the two justices were evident in June 2021, when Chief Justice Roberts wrote a narrowly drawn majority opinion in a religious-freedom case.\par \par \uc2 "The Court has emitted a wisp of a decision that leaves religious liberty in a confused and vulnerable state," Justice Alito wrote in a concurring opinion. "Those who count on this Court to stand up for the First Amendment have every right to be disappointed\u8212\'20\'14as am I."\par \par \uc2 The chief justice's decision for the majority, Justice Alito wrote, "might as well be written on the dissolving paper sold in magic shops."\par \par \uc2 The two men's split on the fate of Roe v. Wade puts those differences in starker relief than ever, and it leaves in peril the chief justice's efforts to use his position to forge a court based in consensus and continuity.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-ruling-highlights-diverging-paths-of-justices-john-roberts-samuel-alito-11656149400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling Highlights Diverging Paths of Justices John Roberts, Samuel Alito  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220625ei6p000ma\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
