\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Fight Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Now Moves to Statehouses Nationwide; Overturning of Roe v. Wade by Supreme Court opens new political battlegrounds across U.S.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Catherine Lucey and Laura Kusisto \par \uc2 1,495 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 18:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania Attorney General \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  is running for governor of the state. An earlier version of this article incorrectly referred to his bid for governor as a reelection campaign. (Corrected on June 24)\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruling Roe v. Wade  }}}\uc2  establishes new political battlegrounds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across the 50 states, many of which could see lengthy clashes over restricting or protecting access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 By eliminating a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the high court's ruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-abortion-is-legal-and-where-it-loses-protections-without-roe-v-wade-11656080346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 returns the issue to the states  }}}\uc2 , and about half of them, mostly led by Republicans, have been poised to ban many or most abortions if Roe was wiped away. Other Democrat-led states are moving to protect access to the procedure, in some cases {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-providers-in-illinois-colorado-and-florida-prepare-for-patient-influx-if-roe-is-overturned-11655560800"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preparing for visitors  }}}\uc2  from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be unavailable. And in politically diverse states with divided government, clashes over the path forward on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy could continue for years.\par \par \uc2 "This is going to put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward the center of our politics for the foreseeable future," said Steven Greene, a political-science professor at \uc2 North Carolina State University\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Advocates on both sides of the issue said that the ruling would place additional focus on state and local elections, because governors, state lawmakers and attorneys general will hold new power to enact and enforce a broader array of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. That means those contests could see additional funding and support from national groups and donors.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to be decided, not just state by state, but county by county," said Amanda Litman, of Run for Something, a group that recruits progressive candidates to run for local offices. She said local prosecutors could have discretion over enforcing state laws.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, which supports antiabortion candidates, is currently expanding into states to influence elections and policy-making, creating five new positions focused on state policy and increasing their budget. The group is investing in Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, New Hampshire, Nevada and is on the ground in Georgia, North Carolina and Arizona, all states where the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is expected to be contentious.\par \par \uc2 "Everything we've done on the federal level, we want to replicate that on the state level," said Marjorie Dannenfelser, the group's president. "Now we've gone from one major, big front to 50."\par \par \uc2 Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania could present some of the more heated early battles. All have Democratic governors that so far have blocked antiabortion laws from Republican-led legislatures, although that could change based on the results of November's elections. Michigan and Wisconsin also have \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans on the books that predate Roe v. Wade, as well as Democratic attorneys general who will play a role in deciding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-abortion-restrictions-loom-some-officials-promise-not-to-enforce-them-11653235200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whether those bans get enforced.  }}}\par \par \uc2 Conversely, in Virginia, Democrats hold a narrow majority in the state senate, which makes it difficult for Republican Gov. \uc2 Glenn Youngkin\uc2  and his party to enact restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "There are going to be states where partisan control can change and that can completely change the ability of a woman to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that state," Mr. Greene said.\par \par \uc2 Kansas will be home to one of the first tests of whether the end of Roe energizes voters. A constitutional amendment on the Aug. 2 primary ballot would specify that the state's constitution doesn't protect the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Early voting on that measure begins in mid-July.\par \par \uc2 The proposed amendment seeks to undo a 2019 ruling by the Kansas Supreme Court, which held that the state's constitution protects access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A successful ballot initiative would clear the way for the state's Republican-led legislature to ban the procedure, without the possibility of a veto from a Democratic governor. If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal in Kansas, the state is expected to become a destination for women traveling from states in the South and Midwest, where it will likely be banned.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is also poised to be a battleground over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The state's Republican-led legislature is in the process of putting forward a constitutional amendment for a future ballot that would decree there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's constitution.\par \par \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  , the Democratic candidate for governor in Pennsylvania, opposes the amendment and has sought to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central issue in his campaign. So has Michigan Democratic Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  , who is up for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say most voters have made up their minds on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that other issues will remain bigger priorities this year and beyond. "The most persuadable voters are deeply concerned with the damage being done to their financial security," \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2  spokesman Jesse Hunt said, adding that inflation, supply-chain issues and economic concerns will "continue to drive voters away from Democrats."\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, State Sen. \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  , the Republican nominee for governor, has proposed a bill in the legislature that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state starting around six weeks and has called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  "science-denying genocide." At a debate in May, Republican contenders looking to challenge Michigan Gov. Whitmer said they supported banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some said there should be exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Down the ballot, Democrats are eyeing a few state houses as opportunities to flip seats\u8212\'20\'14or possibly even change control\u8212\'20\'14aided by frustration over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Those include the closely divided state Senate in Minnesota, as well as both legislative chambers in Michigan and New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 "We have a reinvigorated mission to elect Democrats to state houses all across the country to protect Roe," said Jessica Post, president of the Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee, which works to elect state legislators.\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Post said it was hard to know the full effect of the Supreme Court's decision on the current campaign cycle, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates said some states may be longer-term projects.\par \par \uc2 "That's the thing about this state work. It does take time. We are working to build infrastructure," said Sarah Curmi, vice president of state and local campaigns for Emily's List, which supports female candidates that back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. "It can't all be done in one cycle."\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups said they plan to push for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans everywhere but know that more moderate or liberal states are longer-term projects. "Some states are going to protect unborn children through the law. Others have a long way to go," said Carol Tobias, president of National Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 In politically moderate states such as Florida, North Carolina and Virginia, Republican leaders thus far have been circumspect about whether they favor strict bans on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting in the first trimester of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A recent Wall Street Journal poll showed that more than two-thirds of Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/upholding-roe-v-wade-is-supported-by-most-americans-wsj-poll-finds-11654162200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 want to uphold Roe v. Wade,  }}}\uc2  and most favor women having access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason. The findings were from a Journal poll conducted with NORC at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  , a nonpartisan research organization that measures social attitudes.\par \par \uc2 The survey showed that on banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, 34% of respondents were in support, 43% were in opposition and 21% said they neither supported nor opposed such a move. The numbers were similar for a ban after six weeks, with 30% of respondents in favor, 49% opposed and 19% with no view.\par \par \uc2 Legal and political analysts said Republican legislators could face new challenges now that the antiabortion laws they pass are more than symbolic gestures. "For the longest time antiabortion legislators have been able to pass the most extreme bills without having to worry about whether they're going to take effect," said David S. Cohen, a professor at \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  's \uc2 Thomas R. Kline\uc2  School of Law in Philadelphia\par \par \uc2 Likewise, analysts said there are political risks to state Democrats if they push laws allowing broad access to the procedure throughout pregnancy or offering taxpayer-funded assistance programs that could help nonresidents.\par \par \uc2 New York and Colorado both have recently passed bills expanding or protecting women's rights to obtain abortions late in pregnancy. California and New York are also among the states taking steps toward directing new public funding to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, in part to help cope with increased demand from women traveling from out of state.\par \par \uc2 "It's clearly true that where blue states are on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is to the left of most Americans and where red states are is to the right of most Americans," said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2  , who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Findell, Christine Mai-Duc and Jimmy Vielkind contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Catherine Lucey at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:catherine.lucey@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 catherine.lucey@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:laura.kusisto@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laura.kusisto@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Laura Kusisto at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-courts-abortion-ruling-puts-states-in-spotlight-11656082938"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fight Over \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights Now Moves to Statehouses Nationwide  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o003jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
