\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Providers in Illinois, Colorado and Florida Prepare for Patient Influx if Roe Is Overturned; Antiabortion advocates will focus efforts in states close to ones that may outlaw or limit abortions following an expected Supreme Court ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Wernau, Dan Frosch and Jennifer Calfas  | Photographs by Taylor Glascock for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 14:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 An earlier version of this article misspelled the surname of Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis as DeSeantis. (June 24)\par \par \uc2 FAIRVIEW HEIGHTS, Ill.\u8212\'20\'14The future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is beginning to take shape in places like this southern Illinois town, which is expected to become a destination for women from nearby states poised to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe v. Wade is overturned.\par \par \uc2 Bracing for an influx of patients, Kawanna Shannon, director of patient access at Planned Parenthood's regional logistics center in southern Illinois, said she and colleagues are trying to hire 15 more employees and stay open seven days a week, compared with six currently.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 draft decision leaked last month }}}\uc2  indicated that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  may end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a decision expected to come in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, 13 states are poised to implement so-called trigger laws to quickly or immediately outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most cases. They include Missouri and Kentucky, which each have one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and currently have more restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws than neighboring Illinois.\par \par \uc2 "We are already an oasis in a vast \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  desert," said Jennifer Welch, president and chief executive officer of Planned Parenthood of Illinois, referring to neighboring states that already have robust restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The anticipated decision\u8230\'20\'26doesn't change that, it just increases the size of the desert."\par \par \uc2 Other states that could become more popular destinations for abortions in a post-Roe world include Colorado and Florida, which both border states with more restrictive regimes.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion activists said they are focusing more of their resources in those places as well, recruiting volunteers, increasing fundraising and building out vans they can park outside clinics with services intended to convince women to remain pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood of Illinois said it expects an additional 20,000 to 30,000 patients a year to enter the state seeking abortions if Roe, the 1973 decision establishing a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is overturned. Planned Parenthood of the Rocky Mountains, which operates clinics in Colorado as well as New Mexico and Nevada, projects it will receive an additional 10,000 patients seeking abortions annually, up 80% from the current volume.\par \par \uc2 Colorado is seeing an uptick in abortions already, according to the Cobalt \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund, a Denver-based group that provides financial assistance for people seeking abortions. The organization said 32% of its appointments from January to May of this year were for out-of-state patients. That is up from 17% last year.\par \par \uc2 Three quarters of the patients the group assisted with appointments this year came from Texas, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-new-texas-abortion-ban-law-takes-effect-providers-rush-to-adjust-11630521388"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which passed a law last year banning abortions }}}\uc2  after the sixth week of pregnancy. In 2021, 26% of the out-of-state patients Cobalt helped with appointments came from Texas.\par \par \uc2 "We're very good at getting people from out of state. What's new is the number of people, and that they need access to care much earlier in their pregnancy," said Karen Middleton, Cobalt's president.\par \par \uc2 Others come from nearby states including Wyoming, where what would have been the state's only facility to offer surgical and medical abortions was set on fire May 25, weeks before it was set to open, according to Julie Burkhart, the clinic's president. Casper police and the \uc2 Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms and Explosives\uc2  have said they are investigating the blaze as arson.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  said Thursday it is investigating a series of attacks and threats around the country {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/house-republicans-request-investigation-into-reports-of-vandalism-arson-at-antiabortion-offices-11655340778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against centers that try to convince women not to terminate pregnancies }}}\uc2  and offices of antiabortion groups.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups are mobilizing alongside the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. Colorado-based Save the Storks is currently adding 30 buses to a fleet of 75 nationwide that provide medical services to pregnant women and are placed near \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and college campuses, said Chief Executive Diane Ferraro. That is the most it has ever had in production in a single year.\par \par \uc2 Amy Gehrke, executive director of Illinois Right to Life, said her organization has been visiting community centers and churches around the state to recruit volunteers and expand its lobbying power.\par \par \uc2 "The pro-life people of Illinois are becoming very keenly aware that we are going to be going to be ground zero for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Ms. Gehrke.\par \par \uc2 Coalition Life, an antiabortion group that operates just across the Illinois border in St. Louis, is expanding an internship program that pays people to visit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to try to counsel women before they go inside.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What is the future for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the U.S.? Join the conversation below.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Florida say they are preparing for a potential influx of patients from Southern states with more restrictive laws. Planned Parenthood's Tallahassee clinic is preparing to expand the days it provides abortions to five days from three. The organization's Jacksonville clinic added a second clinician to accommodate an increase in patients from out of state after a six-week ban in Texas took effect last year.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/florida-gov-ron-desantis-signs-15-week-abortion-ban-into-law-11649961049"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ban on most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy }}}\uc2 , which has been challenged in court, is slated to take effect July 1 in Florida. But providers said they worry the state's Republican leaders may try to institute tighter limits or outlaw abortions entirely if Roe is overturned, ending Florida's status as a destination in the region for women seeking to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Florida Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  said the Republican "has always been pro-life," but declined to comment on potential legislation, as did incoming Republican leaders of the state Senate and House, which are expected to reconvene in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Write to Julie Wernau at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Julie.Wernau@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Julie.Wernau@wsj.com }}}\uc2 , Dan Frosch at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:dan.frosch@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dan.frosch@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-providers-in-illinois-colorado-and-florida-prepare-for-patient-influx-if-roe-is-overturned-11655560800"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Providers in Illinois, Colorado and Florida Prepare for Patient Influx if Roe Is Overturned }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220618ei6i001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
