\par\par \uc2 Essay\par \uc2 Life\par \b \uc2 The Controversial Economics of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; The Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade will bring new scrutiny to research over the past several decades studying the socioeconomic effects of  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  liberalization.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jon Hilsenrath and Amara  Omeokwe \par \uc2 1,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 19:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade will ripple across society and the economy in coming years. The question is how.\par \par \uc2 In the field of economics, a controversial thread of research over the past three decades found that the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1970s led to a range of positive economic outcomes for women, such as increased schooling and better employment prospects. Some studies argued that legalization also helped to reduce national crime rates, teen drug use and other social ills.\par \par \uc2 With Roe now overturned, some economists argue that these benefits are at risk. But the research behind their conclusions has been inconsistent and often challenged, and is now likely to come under even more scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 "It's like we start to have two countries: The places where the providers go away and the places where they don't," said \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  economics professor Caitlin Myers, who studies reproduction and the economy, in an interview prior to the Supreme Court's ruling in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Competing views on the economics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were a part of the Court's considerations. A group of 240 female researchers who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  filed an amicus brief in the case, arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access had set women back after Roe. Legalization, they argued, coincided with more women falling into poverty, women reporting lower levels of happiness in surveys and fewer women saying they were in satisfying long-term relationships.\par \par \uc2 An opposing group of 154 economists, led by Prof. Myers, filed their own amicus brief in response, pointing to "a substantial body of well-developed and credible research" that contradicted the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  brief. They argued that in giving women more control over their childbearing preferences, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legalization led to a range of social and economic benefits for women, particularly related to education and work.\par \par \uc2 Research on these questions hinges on the fact that a number of states legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the Supreme Court did so nationally with its 1973 decision in Roe. Economists saw an opportunity to examine the economic and social effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access by looking at the states that had legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 1970\u8212\'20\'14Alaska, California, Hawaii, New York and Washington\u8212\'20\'14and several others that had liberalized restrictions. By comparing them to states that legalized later, they had a natural experiment.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should socioeconomic considerations play a role in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Joshua Angrist\uc2 , a professor of economics at MIT, won the Nobel Prize in economics last year for pioneering such work, which examines the cause-and-effect relationships of different social policies. In an influential paper published in 2000, he found that in states that had liberalized their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws before Roe, Black teen women experienced declines of more than 4% in childbearing and out-of-wedlock birth, with associated increases in schooling and employment. But white women didn't see the same effects, and even for Black women the benefits didn't look as powerful in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became legal after Roe.\par \par \uc2 In their Supreme Court brief, the pro-Roe economists cited later studies that built on Prof. Angrist's techniques, arguing that the expansion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the 1970s reduced teen motherhood by 34% and teen marriage by 20%, and also made women more likely to attend college and enter professional occupations. Overturning Roe, the brief argued, threatened to reverse those and other benefits.\par \par \uc2 Prof. Angrist was asked to participate in the competing legal briefs but declined. "I didn't feel like I had a dog in that fight," he said, adding that his original study "wasn't a clean set of findings."\par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  has weighed in on different sides of the issue. As a professor at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\uc2 , she and her husband \uc2 George Akerlof\uc2 , another Nobel Prize-winning economist, wrote in the 1990s that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legalization helped to explain an overall rise in U.S. out-of-wedlock births. Men took less responsibility for childbearing with the advent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception, they argued, which reduced "shotgun" marriages tied to pregnancy and led to more single-parent births.\par \par \uc2 The anti-Roe brief filed in the Dobbs case cited Prof. Yellen's research, though the paper was nuanced. It also argued that in economic terms \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had produced "both winners and losers," and that rolling back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access would be counterproductive. As Treasury Secretary, Ms. Yellen has said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions would set women back in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 The most controversial study on the economics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was published in 2001 by \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  economics professor Steven Levitt and \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  law professor John Donohue, who argued that the legalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1970s led to lower crime rates in the 1990s. The study theorized that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prevented the birth of many unwanted children who would have become troubled adolescents especially prone to crime.\par \par \uc2 It was an explosive conclusion, reviled on the right as supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on the left as reminiscent of eugenics. Prof. Angrist said that he was puzzled by the study's conclusion, given the mixed results of his own study, and several other researchers challenged the findings on technical grounds. Still, Profs. Levitt and Donohue stood by their work.\par \par \uc2 "Now that we have 20 years of data, it is a highly statistically significant finding," said Prof. Donohue, who signed the amicus brief in support of Roe, though it doesn't cite his research. He said that overturning Roe would have significant consequences for women and society, particularly in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is rolled back. Prof. Levitt declined to discuss his research and didn't sign the brief.\par \par \uc2 Prof. Myers, who has periodically worked as an expert consultant and witness for Planned Parenthood and the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  in legal cases concerning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, pointed to her own research on the effects of a 2013 Texas law that resulted in the closing of several of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities. The closing of clinics increased the travel distance to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  facilities for women in some parts of the state but not others, allowing her and her colleagues to study how the change affected different areas.\par \par \uc2 They found that when the distance a woman would have to travel to the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic rose from under 50 miles to between 50 and 100 miles, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates fell by 16%. Based on these and other findings, Prof. Myers estimates that in the first year after the overturning of Roe, about 100,000 women nationwide who wanted the procedure would be unable to reach an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic. She said that those most affected would likely be poor women, predominantly young and people of color, in the deep South and the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Pakaluk, an associate economics professor at \uc2 Catholic University\uc2  who signed the brief in favor of overturning Roe, argued that this whole approach to the issue is incomplete, because it avoids considering the costs and benefits of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for one group she finds highly significant: those who are never born because of it. She says that the economists who cite other social or economic benefits should "say upfront that what we're about to tell you depends upon neglecting the costs to the unborn child because we don't think his or her costs count."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jon Hilsenrath at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jon.hilsenrath@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jon.hilsenrath@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Amara Omeokwe at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:amara.omeokwe@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amara.omeokwe@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-controversial-economics-of-abortion-law-11656012556"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Controversial Economics of \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220623ei6n0070t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
