\par\par \uc2 Health News\par \uc2 Health\par \b \uc2 Fertility Doctors Move Embryos, Expecting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Changes; IVF companies and patients are lobbying for state protections from  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dominique Mosbergen and Natalie Andrews | Photographs by Kayana Szymczak for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 15:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Fertility companies and patients have been moving embryos and making contingency plans, anticipating that if {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v. Wade were overturned }}}\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in some states could extend to protect eggs fertilized in laboratories.\par \par \uc2 More than 2% of 3.7 million babies born in the U.S. in 2019 were conceived through in vitro fertilization, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/art/reports/2019/pdf/2019-Report-ART-Fertility-Clinic-National-Summary-h.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the latest federal data show }}}\uc2 . Many embryos created through IVF aren't viable, fertility specialists said, and those that aren't ultimately transferred into a uterus may be discarded.\par \par \uc2 Some fertility and legal experts said the loss or discarding of embryos could be criminalized by statutes that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of fertilization, or that grant personhood rights to embryos.\par \par \uc2 "There could be unintended consequences on IVF from these laws aimed at restricting abortions," said Alan Penzias, a reproductive endocrinologist at Boston IVF, a Massachusetts fertility company, and an associate professor at \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Boston IVF operates in 11 states, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 states where \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is expected to be banned }}}\uc2  after the Supreme Court on Friday overturned Roe v. Wade, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the 1973 decision }}}\uc2  that established a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We're definitely keeping our ear to the ground," said Dr. Penzias, who has been lobbying legislators to protect IVF from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Angeline Beltsos\uc2 , chief executive officer of clinical at Kindbody, which has clinics in 12 states and the District of Columbia, said some patients had asked doctors to move embryos out of state in case Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 "We've started the process of moving embryos from states like Missouri to states that patients think may be safer for their decision-making," Dr. Beltsos said. Those decisions include the option to freeze embryos for storage, a process that can cause damage to some embryos, and to discard embryos if they aren't viable or ultimately transferred.\par \par \uc2 Shady Grove Fertility, a fertility company with clinics in eight states and D.C., including Texas and Georgia, is devising workarounds and has encouraged patients to tell their state representatives to protect IVF services, said medical director \uc2 Eric Widra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We're certainly nervous about what will happen in Texas and Georgia and we're going to be cognizant of these laws as we think about future expansion plans," Dr. Widra said.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, where lawmakers are considering an amendment to the state constitution that would protect the life of unborn children from conception, Shady Grove Fertility sent mailers this month asking patients to contact their state representatives. The amendment isn't expected to pass, but Maureen Kelly, a Pennsylvania-based reproductive endocrinologist, said she is nonetheless advising patients that clinics could limit the number of embryos created in an IVF cycle to avoid any being discarded.\par \par \uc2 "I've been bringing it up with patients\u8230\'20\'26that laws may evolve," Dr. Kelly said.\par \par \uc2 A bill that Oklahoma Gov. Kevin Stitt, a Republican, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/oklahoma-governor-signs-abortion-ban-11653529937"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed into law last month }}}\uc2  banning most abortions from the point of fertilization explicitly excludes contraceptives including so-called morning-after pills but not IVF, said Michelle Banker, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and health litigation at the \uc2 National Women's Law Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I. Glenn Cohen, a deputy dean at Harvard Law School, said that the bill likely wouldn't affect IVF but that the language was vague enough to raise questions. "If lawmakers don't want to touch IVF, they should be clearer in their language," he said.\par \par \uc2 Eli Reshef, a reproductive endocrinologist in Oklahoma City, said the ambiguity has concerned many of his patients. "I cannot tell you how many inquiries I've received in the last two weeks," said Dr. Reshef. "They're asking, 'Should I even bother scheduling my IVF?'"\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for Mr. Stitt said concerns about the bill's impact on IVF were a fabrication by Democrats. Oklahoma state Rep. Wendi Stearman, who wrote the law, said the bill clearly defines \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the termination of a pregnancy in a woman. "A petri dish in a lab is in no way defined as termination of a pregnancy in a woman," she said.\par \par \uc2 If laws against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extend to the protection of all embryos, Dr. Penzias said Boston IVF physicians might only create embryos for immediate transfer into a patient's uterus, reducing the potential rate of success. Alternatively, sperm and eggs might be collected in-state and shipped to Boston IVF's headquarters in Massachusetts to be fertilized and stored, he said.\par \par \uc2 "Adding this workaround would add yet another burden," he said. "It may make it unaffordable."\par \par \uc2 Egg harvesting, fertilization and transfer can cost $10,000 to upward of $20,000 per cycle, according to the \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine\uc2 . Several cycles are often necessary for a successful pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some people might pay to store embryos indefinitely or transport them out of state to be discarded if embryos created through IVF become protected where they live, said Dr. Penzias.\par \par \uc2 "The extra time, cost and psychological and logistical burden could be crushing and put parenthood out of reach for many," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Dominique Mosbergen at Dominique.Mosbergen@wsj.com and Natalie Andrews at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Natalie.Andrews@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Natalie.Andrews@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/fertility-doctors-move-embryos-to-other-states-in-case-of-roe-v-wade-impact-11656063000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fertility Doctors Move Embryos, Expecting \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Changes  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o000xf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
