\par \par \uc2 Andrea Gallegos, an executive administrator at a clinic in Oklahoma and one in San Antonio, says, 'We're going to be forcing more and more women to remain pregnant.' PHOTO: Michael Noble Jr. for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 A recent analysis by \uc2 Middlebury College\uc2  economics professor Caitlin Myers found that about 100,000 women trying to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be able to access an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider because of the increased distances they would have to travel in the year after the court's decision. About 75,000 of those women would likely give birth as a result, she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say they will work to ensure women are better supported when they choose to keep a baby, and to prevent motherhood from compromising women's other ambitions. Some see the end of Roe as potentially a moment to push for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-says-child-care-assistance-will-help-economy-grow-11623231001"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 better access to daycare }}}\uc2  on college campuses and elsewhere, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/debate-over-paid-family-leave-is-louder-than-ever-11635564747"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid family leave }}}\uc2 , housing assistance, and making resources about adoption more widely available.\par \par \uc2 "You have a whole generation of young women who have never lived in an America without legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so some of them are very scared," said Kristan Hawkins, president of Students for Life of America, a national antiabortion group. "This is a very progressive, radical view that she shouldn't have to choose the life of her child or her education or her career," she said.\par \par \uc2 In some parts of the U.S., clinics that perform \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have served several other roles, including providing access to contraception, sexually transmitted disease testing and wellness visits. In states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the closure of clinics in rural areas could further limit access to medical care.\par \par \uc2 Pregnancy-care centers, which oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and offer services including free ultrasounds, pregnancy tests, diapers and baby clothes, said they are preparing to fill some of these gaps. Save the Storks, a nonprofit that operates mobile pregnancy-care clinics for women in some 28 states around the country, is planning to widen its services. The group just expanded into Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Diane Ferraro, the group's chief executive, said it intends to deploy more of its buses in states that are poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where she expects an increased need for maternal support services. Save the Storks is funded primarily by individual donors and doesn't receive public funding, so initially Ms. Ferraro anticipates its 32 staff members, as well as volunteers and staff at local pregnancy-care centers, might have to work longer hours.\par \par \uc2 "We need to unify and help these women," said Ms. Ferraro. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
