\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Key Excerpts From Supreme Court's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling Overturning Roe v. Wade; High court strikes down 1973 decision establishing constitutional right to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Landers \par \uc2 775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 17:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturned both the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision }}}\uc2  establishing a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and a 1992 opinion that reaffirmed Roe, called Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pennsylvania v. Casey.\par \par \uc2 Below are excerpts from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/how-the-supreme-court-overturned-roe-v-wade-and-abortion-rights-11656081705"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the majority opinion }}}\uc2  by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 ; concurrences by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 ; a concurrence in the judgment by Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  that takes issue with the majority's approach; and a dissent jointly written by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan. For the full text of these opinions, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/dobbs-v-jackson-womens-health-organization-6-24-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click here }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Majority opinion by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\par \par \uc2 "Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. Its reasoning was exceptionally weak, and the decision has had damaging consequences. And far from bringing about a national settlement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue, Roe and Casey have enflamed debate and deepened division." (p. 6)\par \par \uc2 "[A] right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not deeply rooted in the nation's history and traditions. On the contrary, an unbroken tradition of prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on pain of criminal punishment persisted from the earliest days of the common law until 1973." (p. 25)\par \par \uc2 "[A]s the Court has reiterated time and time again, adherence to precedent is not 'an inexorable command.' There are occasions when past decisions should be overruled." (p. 37)\par \par \uc2 "[W]e emphasize that our decision concerns the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and no other right. Nothing in this opinion should be understood to cast doubt on precedents that do not concern \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (p. 66)\par \par \uc2 "The Constitution does not prohibit the citizens of each State from regulating or prohibiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Roe and Casey arrogated that authority. We now overrule those decisions and return that authority to the people and their elected representatives." (p. 79)\par \par \uc2 Concurrence by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas summarized the conclusions of three previous cases: "right of married persons to obtain contraceptives" (Griswold v. Connecticut); "right to engage in private, consensual sexual acts" (Lawrence v. Texas), and "right to same-sex marriage" (Obergefell v. Hodges).\par \par \uc2 "[I]n future cases, we should reconsider all of this Court's substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell." (p. 3)\par \par \uc2 Concurrence by Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\par \par \uc2 "[T]he Court's decision today does not outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the United States. On the contrary, the Court's decision properly leaves the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the people and their elected representatives in the democratic process." (p. 3)\par \par \uc2 Concurrence in the judgment by Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\par \par \uc2 "The Court's opinion is thoughtful and thorough, but those virtues cannot compensate for the fact that its dramatic and consequential ruling is unnecessary to decide the case before us." (p. 2)\par \par \uc2 "A narrower decision rejecting the misguided viability line would be markedly less unsettling, and nothing more is needed to decide this case." (p. 11)\par \par \uc2 "Both the Court's opinion and the dissent display a relentless freedom from doubt on the legal issue that I cannot share." (p. 12)\par \par \uc2 Dissent by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan\par \par \uc2 "[A]fter today's ruling, some states may compel women to carry to term a fetus with severe physical anomalies\u8212\'20\'14for example, one afflicted with Tay-Sachs disease, sure to die within a few years of birth. States may even argue that a prohibition on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  need make no provision for protecting a woman from risk of death or physical harm.\u8230\'20\'26Most threatening of all, no language in today's decision stops the federal government from prohibiting abortions nationwide." (p. 2-3)\par \par \uc2 "Those responsible for the original Constitution, including the Fourteenth Amendment, did not perceive women as equals, and did not recognize women's rights. When the majority says that we must read our foundational charter as viewed at the time of ratification (except that we may also check it against the Dark Ages), it consigns women to second-class citizenship." (p. 15)\par \par \uc2 "[T]oday's opinion will fuel the fight to get contraception, and any other issues with a moral dimension, out of the Fourteenth Amendment and into state legislatures." (p. 28-29)\par \par \uc2 "[W]eakening stare decisis in a hotly contested case like this one calls into question this court's commitment to legal principle. It makes the court appear not restrained but aggressive, not modest but grasping. In all those ways, today's decision takes aim, we fear, at the rule of law." (p. 57)\par \par \uc2 Write to Peter Landers at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:peter.landers@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 peter.landers@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/key-excerpts-from-supreme-courts-abortion-ruling-overturning-roe-v-wade-11656092052"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Key Excerpts From Supreme Court's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling Overturning Roe v. Wade  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o005bp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
