\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 The Conservative Legal Push to Overturn Roe v. Wade Was 50 Years in the Making; An increasingly influential movement questioned the view of constitutional rights underpinning the decision\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 2,644 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 22:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overruling of Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  was 50 years in the making\u8212\'20\'14the culmination of a conservative judicial movement that rejected the interpretation of constitutional rights underpinning that 1973 Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 It took far longer than many conservatives expected.\par \par \uc2 The majority opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, first disclosed in draft version {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510?mod=series_scotusroe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 by an extraordinary leak }}}\uc2  in May, declared that Roe and later \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights precedents have no basis in the Constitution. "The Constitution makes no reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and no such right is implicitly protected by any constitutional provision," Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote for the court, joined by Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett.\par \par \uc2 Even as a young lawyer, Justice Alito had looked for ways to push back on the reasoning behind Roe going back to the 1980s, when he worked in the Reagan Justice Department. In a May 1985 memo, he sketched out opportunities "to advance the goals of bringing about the eventual overruling of Roe v. Wade."\par \par \uc2 Former Attorney General \uc2 Edwin Meese III\uc2  championed the conservative jurisprudence during the Reagan years and promoted the young lawyers\u8212\'20\'14Justice Alito among them\u8212\'20\'14who would rise to influence through successive Republican administrations.\par \par \uc2 "It really has been a matter of pretty clear record for a long time that [Roe] was wrong," he said.\par \par \uc2 Because the Constitution doesn't expressly grant women a right to end a pregnancy, many conservatives, like Mr. Meese, have said the court erred by construing a right to privacy that allows for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at least in the earlier stages of gestation. That originalist legal view overlapped with the convictions of a broader set of people who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on what they considered moral grounds as the taking of a life.\par \par \uc2 Friday's liberal dissenters pointed to a different constitutional tradition, one that has seen rights expand since the country's beginnings. The framers "understood that the world changes. So they did not define rights by reference to the specific practices" of their time but "defined rights in general terms, to permit future evolution in their scope and meaning," Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan wrote in a joint opinion.\par \par \uc2 For the antiabortion movement that has helped power Republican political success\u8212\'20\'14including the 2016 election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who as president appointed three justices who were in the Dobbs majority\u8212\'20\'14the end of Roe was long a key goal. With states now free to regulate the procedure, most abortions likely will be outlawed or at least curbed in about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 half the states }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But for the conservative legal movement, "this was not a matter of deciding whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a good idea or a bad idea," said Mr. Meese, now 90 years old. "It's a matter of the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 Counterrevolution\par \par \uc2 That Roe would stoke a legal counterrevolution leading to its own undoing was far from evident in January 1973, when by a 7-2 vote the Supreme Court recognized a woman's right to terminate a pregnancy before fetal viability, or the capacity to live outside the womb. The decision invalidated dozens of state laws banning or restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many dating from the 19th century.\par \par \uc2 The decision followed a line of cases that had steadily removed the government from regulation of family life and sexual practices. In the Roe opinion, Justice Harry Blackmun cited a series of earlier decisions. It began in the 19th century, he wrote, when the court rejected \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2 's demand that a female passenger, who was suing the railroad for negligence after an upper berth fell on her, submit to a surgical examination.\par \par \uc2 "No right is held more sacred, or is more carefully guarded by the common law, than the right of every individual to the possession and control of his own person," the court said in 1891, a year after Louis Brandeis, a future justice, co-wrote a seminal article in the Harvard Law Review, "The Right to Privacy."\par \par \uc2 Justice William O. Douglas had invoked that legal tradition in Griswold v. Connecticut, a 1965 decision striking down an 1879 state law banning contraception. The "marriage relation" involves "a right of privacy older than the Bill of Rights, older than our political parties, older than our school system," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Justice Hugo Black was among those who disagreed. "I like my privacy as well as the next one," he wrote in his Griswold dissent, "but I am nevertheless compelled to admit that government has a right to invade it unless prohibited by some specific constitutional provision." The lack of such a named provision has been underlying judicial opposition to Roe v. Wade ever since.\par \par \uc2 In a passage that Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  later called "garbage," Justice Douglas wrote that "specific guarantees in the Bill of Rights have penumbras, formed by emanations from those guarantees that help give them life and substance."\par \par \uc2 In that context Justice Blackmun wrote in his 1973 Roe decision that the right of privacy was not only grounded in the Constitution, but also "broad enough to encompass a woman's decision whether or not to terminate her pregnancy."\par \par \uc2 That right wasn't absolute, he added, and "at some point in pregnancy" government may "assert important interests" that include "protecting potential life." Following a 1972 lower court decision invalidating Connecticut's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Roe drew the line at viability, generally seen as between 22 and 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 While even some conservative commentators praised the decision, the legal substance of the ruling came under some criticism\u8212\'20\'14including from some liberal-leaning scholars who supported a woman's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Like Justice William Rehnquist, who dissented from Roe, Yale professor John Hart Ely likened the decision to the 1905 case of Lochner v. New York, which struck down a state law limiting working hours for bakers with the argument that it violated a different unenumerated right the court found implicit in the Constitution: the "liberty of contract."\par \par \uc2 That precedent, which jeopardized a swath of state laws over workers' safety and fair treatment, had been effectively abandoned by a series of decisions over the ensuing half-century. "Roe may turn out to be the more dangerous precedent," Ely wrote, adding: "I suppose there is nothing to prevent one from using the word 'privacy' to mean the freedom to live one's life without governmental interference. But the Court obviously does not so use the term. Nor could it, for such a right is at stake in every case."\par \par \uc2 In response to Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents initially focused on amending the Constitution. Rep. \uc2 Larry Hogan Sr\uc2 . (R., Md.), the father of Maryland's current governor, proposed within days of the Supreme Court's opinion an amendment extending due-process and equal-protection rights to "any human being, from the moment of conception"\u8212\'20\'14effectively equating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with murder. When such proposals died in Congress, activists turned to the states. By 1981, more than a dozen legislatures, including Massachusetts and Mississippi, had passed resolutions calling for a constitutional convention to consider a human-life amendment. The movement stalled short of the 38 necessary states.\par \par \uc2 Remaking the judiciary became a central strategy for reversing Roe when \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  became president in 1981, amid a broader effort to move federal courts against what Mr. Meese called the "radical egalitarianism and expansive civil libertarianism" the justices had embraced in the 1950s and '60s. In that era, the court under Chief Justice Earl Warren took steps to abolish racial segregation, end government censorship, extend voting rights and increase protections for criminal defendants, as well as rulings like Griswold that defined a broader concept of privacy and individual rights.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives argued that in those decisions the justices sometimes overstepped their authority to remake society as they pleased.\par \par \uc2 'Policy choices'\par \par \uc2 As an adviser and attorney general, Mr. Meese, who had been an aide to Reagan since his tenure as governor of California, was the president's point man in trying to reverse that pattern. In a 1985 speech to the \uc2 American Bar Association\uc2 , Mr. Meese called for "a jurisprudence of Original Intention" to counter Supreme Court decisions he said were "more policy choices than articulations of constitutional principle."\par \par \uc2 That tenet drew the support of the law students and professors who in 1982 founded the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 . "There has been a war going on for 40 years, since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  became president, over the role of the Supreme Court in making up constitutional rights," said one of them, Steven Calabresi, now a law professor at \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 . "The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right was the quintessential made-up right."\par \par \uc2 Courts have always looked to the framers' understanding of constitutional provisions, and not always to conservative ends. The liberal-leaning Justice Black, a Franklin Roosevelt appointee, argued that the 14th Amendment was intended to hold states to the same civil liberties rules that bound the federal government.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Meese's arguments found few takers on the left. Justice Brennan, who had been appointed by President \uc2 Dwight D. Eisenhower\uc2  and became a liberal stalwart on the court, dismissed the originalist idea several months later in an address at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . "It is arrogant to pretend that from our vantage we can gauge accurately the intent of the Framers on application of principle to specific, contemporary questions," he said.\par \par \uc2 For judicial appointments, Mr. Reagan looked first to tested veterans of the Nixon administration, including \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  and Robert Bork, who were named to appeals courts in anticipation of Supreme Court vacancies. He was aided in that by the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2 , along with other conservative legal groups, succeeded both by popularizing its ideas and preparing its adherents to implement them, said Amanda Hollis-Brusky, a professor of politics at Pomona College. "There was a very concerted focus on how to mainstream ideas about originalism, and conservative and libertarian beliefs about the regulatory state," she said.\par \par \uc2 By building chapters at law schools and promoting lawyers through judicial clerkships with like-minded judges, conservative activists prepared a cadre to challenge prevailing directions of constitutional law, Ms. Hollis-Brusky said.\par \par \uc2 In 1986, the Senate confirmed Justice Scalia by a 98-0 vote, including all 47 Democratic senators. Mr. Reagan's nomination of Judge Bork the following year was a jolt to liberals. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , then chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , fought to defeat the nomination, principally because Judge Bork rejected recognition of rights such as privacy that aren't explicitly mentioned in the Constitution. The nomination failed on a 58-42 vote, with six Republicans joining 52 of 54 Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The bitter partisan battle over Judge Bork, who had addressed the Federalists' founding convention, forged deeper cohesion among socially conservative jurists and set the stage for more politicized battles over Supreme Court nominations.\par \par \uc2 Roe's end\par \par \uc2 In 1992, when a challenge to Pennsylvania \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations reached the court, Roe's end appeared possible. Only Justice Blackmun remained from the Roe majority, alongside the two dissenters, Chief Justice William Rehnquist and Justice Byron White. Two recent nominees of President George H.W. Bush joined four other Republican-appointed justices.\par \par \uc2 Instead, in Planned Parenthood v. Casey, a plurality including three of those justices\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2 , \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  and David Souter\u8212\'20\'14stepped in to rescue Roe, curbing the decision's scope but leaving intact the right to end a pregnancy before viability.\par \par \uc2 While expressing concerns over Roe's reasoning, they argued that after nearly 20 years, the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right was too firmly established to rescind outright.\par \par \uc2 "An entire generation has come of age free to assume Roe's concept of liberty in defining the capacity of women to act in society, and to make reproductive decisions," the plurality said, over a four-justice dissent. "The ability of women to participate equally in the economic and social life of the Nation has been facilitated by their ability to control their reproductive lives."\par \par \uc2 "Now, just when so many expected the darkness to fall, the flame has grown bright," Justice Blackmun wrote in concurrence.\par \par \uc2 During the 1990s, as President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  filled the next two high-court vacancies with \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  and \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , both committed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and its allies at law schools and on the bench prepared for the future.\par \par \uc2 By the time Republicans regained the appointment power under President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the conservative legal movement had become a mature pillar of the legal establishment. \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2  has grown from its law-school origins into a Washington-headquartered nonprofit with more than 60,000 members and annual revenue of more than $22 million, 90% of which it says comes from contributions from individuals and foundations. President Bush appointed Justice Alito to the Supreme Court, as well as Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  would go on to appoint two liberal judges, Justices Sotomayor and Kagan. Following the death of Justice Scalia in February 2016, the Republican Senate majority refused to hold a hearing or vote on the nomination of Mr. Obama's next judicial nominee, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . The seat went unfilled until the following year and the next presidential administration, when President Trump nominated \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , who joined the court in April 2017.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't until the retirement of Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  the following year, however, that Roe's reversal became plausible. For years the court's swing vote, Justice Kennedy had permitted some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions to take effect while retaining the view that the Constitution affords women some power over whether to continue a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump had expressly promised to select his successor from a list vetted by the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Justice Kennedy's successor, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , had praised Justice Rehnquist's Roe dissent. But even as he appeared to tip the balance, Chief Justice Roberts, who has expressed a stronger respect for precedent than other conservative justices in recent years, appeared reluctant to rescind a right recognized for nearly half a century. In June 2020, he voted with the court's liberals to strike down a Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation, writing that a 2016 precedent compelled that result even though he had dissented from the prior case.\par \par \uc2 The following September, the death of Justice Ginsburg allowed Mr. Trump to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-trump-pick-amy-coney-barrett-could-mean-for-future-of-the-supreme-court-11601155192"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appoint Justice Barrett }}}\uc2  shortly before he lost the presidential election to Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 Until then, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents had followed a tactic of seeking to erode \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, since abolishing them appeared out of reach. In March 2020, when Justice Ginsburg still sat on the court, Mississippi had appealed lower-court decisions striking down its 2018 law banning most abortions after 15 weeks\u8212\'20\'14an assault on the viability line established in Roe and Casey that advocates hoped could garner five votes.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, with Justice Barrett on the court, Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch brought in a new state solicitor general to argue the case. Scott Stewart, a former law clerk to Justice Thomas who had served in the Trump Justice Department, recalibrated the state's arguments to aim at the ultimate goal.\par \par \uc2 "Roe and Casey are egregiously wrong," his brief for the state said. "The conclusion that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a constitutional right has no basis in text, structure, history, or tradition."\par \par \uc2 That the Supreme Court has now agreed with that statement was no accident, said Mr. Meese, hailed by Mr. Calabresi as "the unsung hero" of the conservative legal movement. It was the fruition of seeds Mr. Meese said he helped plant long ago: "We've got people appointed [to the Supreme Court] who are definitely constitutionally oriented lawyers."\par \par \uc2 Brent Kendall contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-v-wade-overturned-supreme-court-11656110804"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Conservative Legal Push to Overturn Roe v. Wade Was 50 Years in the Making }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o0086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
