\par\par \uc2 Midterm Election 2022\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Democrats Focus on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight in Midterm Elections After Supreme Court Ruling; Republicans say the economy will be the dominant issue in November\b0\par\par\uc2 By Joshua Jamerson \par \uc2 1,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 21:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court's decision {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-eliminates-constitutional-right-to-abortion-11656080124"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to overturn Roe v. Wade }}}\uc2  reverberated on the campaign trail Friday as Democrats sought to reframe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/news/types/midterm-election-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the midterm elections }}}\uc2  around protecting {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/supreme-court-decision-roe-v-wade-6-24-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  and Republicans touted a watershed accomplishment in the antiabortion movement.\par \par \uc2 "This cruel ruling is outrageous and heart-wrenching," House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said Friday. "But make no mistake, again: It's all on the ballot this November."\par \par \uc2 President Biden, speaking from the White House, also pointed to the midterms, saying, "This decision must not be the final word."\par \par \uc2 GOP leaders largely stuck to praising the outcome Friday.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  (R., Ky.) called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the decision }}}\uc2  a "historic victory for the Constitution and for the most vulnerable in our society." Sen. Steve Daines (R., Mont.), chairman of the Senate Pro-Life Caucus, said moving forward, "the American people begin a new chapter in which they, through their elected representatives at the state and federal level, have the power to end the violence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Democrats have cast the 6-3 conservative majority of the court as an extension of the GOP, with some on Friday calling it the "Republican Supreme Court." Several top Democratic groups\u8212\'20\'14including the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  and the campaign organizations for Senate and House Democrats\u8212\'20\'14released a joint statement describing the decision as central in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 "The 2022 election will now determine whether new, cruel, and punishing restrictions will be put in place on women and families," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Looking to energize voters on the issue, the DNC\u8212\'20\'14along with the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, the House Democratic campaign arm, and Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee, its Senate arm\u8212\'20\'14launched a new website Friday that connects volunteers with the organizers in their state to make calls and send text messages to potential voters about protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, meanwhile, overwhelmingly praised the decision, saying Roe wrongfully took away states' rights to protect life. While GOP officials said they doubted the midterm elections would move away from an economic focus, they said conservative voters would be happy with the decision and eager to block Democratic efforts to undermine it.\par \par \uc2 "It is a triumphant day," said Carrie Severino, president of the conservative Judicial Crisis Network, which spent tens of millions of dollars to support the confirmation of conservative justices to the Supreme Court during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Severino cited former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's victory in the 2016 presidential election, when the Republican-controlled Senate kept vacant a seat on the court, as an example of judicial issues motivating the right more than the left.\par \par \uc2 "Historically, this has been something that has been much more energizing to voters on the right," she said. "I definitely understand why [Democrats] want to change the subject from the economy\u8230\'20\'26I don't think they're going to be able to change the subject completely."\par \par \uc2 Sen. \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  (R., Mo.), who has been wading into competitive primaries in battleground states with endorsements, told reporters the decision wouldn't impact the midterms. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a top issue for you, this won't change that," he said.\par \par \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican nominee for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Nevada running against Democratic Sen. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , called the ruling a historic victory. But he said voters there shouldn't be concerned with it given state law allows abortions during the first 24 weeks without restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "The Court's decision on Roe doesn't change settled law and it won't distract voters from unaffordable prices, rising crime or the border crisis," he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , who is one of the most vulnerable incumbent Senate Democrats this year, responded on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that "this fight is not over," and urged supporters to volunteer for her campaign.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Kim Schrier\uc2  (D., Wash.), a physician who was first elected in 2018, faces a tough re-election battle. Her race outside Seattle is rated a tossup by the nonpartisan Cook Political Report.\par \par \uc2 In an interview, she said she would focus on both the economy and her record in Congress as well as the fight to create new federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 "Holding the line in the House and growing our majority in the Senate are critical to protecting a woman's right to make her own healthcare decisions," Ms. Schrier said. "It will be very clear that in this race, with my constituents who care a lot about this, that I am the only candidate who will trust women and stand up for a woman's right to make her own healthcare decisions."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Schrier is in a nonpartisan primary slated for Aug. 2, where the top-two candidates advance to November, and several Republicans are running against her.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats control the 50-50 Senate with the vice president's tiebreaking vote and have a narrow majority in the House. The hope, some Democratic candidates said Friday, is that with a stronger Senate majority, they could earn the 51 votes needed to remove or revamp the filibuster rule that requires 60 votes to advance most bills. That would allow the party to push through an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights bill with a simple majority.\par \par \uc2 Cheri Beasley, the Democrat running for an open Senate seat in North Carolina, told supporters at a rally Friday she would vote as a senator to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-senate-filibuster-explained-11642618860"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end the filibuster }}}\uc2  and codify a nationwide right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "The Senate really must act," said Ms. Beasley, the former chief justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court. "Our rights are on the ballot in November," she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Beasley is facing Republican Rep. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  this fall in a matchup the Cook Political Report rates as leaning in the GOP's favor. Mr. Budd, through a spokesman, declined an interview request but pointed to a tweet in which the congressman called the victory a historic ruling for Americans who believe every life deserves protection.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have certainly moved to the center of races for governor, given states now have broad power to restrict abortions, said Terry Madonna, senior fellow for political affairs at Millersville University and the former director of \uc2 Franklin & Marshall College\uc2 's Keystone Poll in Pennsylvania. He cited the gubernatorial race in Pennsylvania between Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and Republican \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who has said he believes life starts at conception and as a state lawmaker sponsored a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Madonna added that he also thought the issue would play out in congressional races. "This ratchets this issue up in importance," he said, noting that the general election is a long way off and a lot could change in the economy.\par \par \uc2 In a WSJ/NORC poll conducted after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/leaked-draft-of-supreme-court-opinion-indicates-roe-v-wade-may-be-overturned-11651554510"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the draft Supreme Court opinion leaked }}}\uc2 , more than two-thirds of Americans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/upholding-roe-v-wade-is-supported-by-most-americans-wsj-poll-finds-11654162200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said they wanted to uphold }}}\uc2  Roe v. Wade, and most favored women having access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason. In the survey, 68% of respondents said they wouldn't like to see the court completely overturn Roe, while 30% said they support that move.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump, whose conservative appointees to the court cleared the way for Friday's decision, has privately expressed concern that the decision could motivate Democrats ahead of the midterm elections, according to a person familiar with his thinking. At the same time, Mr. Trump sees the issue as rallying Republicans in battleground states such as Michigan with strong antiabortion sentiment, the person said.\par \par \uc2 "I think, in the end, this is something that will work out for everybody," Mr. Trump told Fox News after the decision came down. "This brings everything back to the states where it has always belonged."\par \par \uc2 Natalie Andrews, Alex Leary and Siobhan Hughes contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Joshua Jamerson at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joshua.jamerson+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democrats-move-abortion-fight-to-midterm-elections-after-supreme-court-ruling-11656098522"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrats Focus on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Fight in Midterm Elections After Supreme Court Ruling }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o0063h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
