\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Goes Back to the People; In Dobbs, the Supreme Court finally corrects its historic mistake in Roe v. Wade.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 1,144 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 22:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Can America still settle its political conflicts democratically, and peacefully? We're about to find out after the Supreme Court Friday overturned Roe v. Wade and returned the profound moral issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states and democratic assent, where it has always belonged.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the Court's 6-3 decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization is rule by unelected judges. But Roe was the real "exercise of raw judicial power," as Justice Byron White put it in dissent in 1973. That's when seven Justices claimed to find a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is nowhere mentioned in the Constitution and had no history in American common law. The Court on Friday finally corrected its mistake, which has damaged the legitimacy of the Court and inflamed our politics for 49 years.\par \par \uc2 The Justices in the majority deserve credit for sticking with their convictions despite the leak of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 's opinion in May. The leak was probably intended to create a furor to pressure the Justices to change their mind, and it has led to protests in front of their homes and even an apparent assassination attempt against Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . By holding firm, they showed the Court can't be intimidated.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito's majority opinion hews closely to his draft, and it is a careful, thoughtful survey of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law and its history in the constitutional order. His opinion takes apart, brick by logical brick, the reasoning of Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the other main \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedent the Court overrules in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 The central point, underscored by Justice Kavanaugh in his concurrence, is that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be found nowhere in the Constitution. The parchment is neutral on the issue. The supporters of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right claim to have found it in the due process clause of the 14th Amendment, which was ratified in 1868. But until the latter part of the 20th century, the idea of a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be found nowhere in American law. No state constitutions included it, and until shortly before Roe no court had recognized such a right. Justice Harry Blackmun ignored that history and invented the right in Roe.\par \par \uc2 Casey entrenched Roe in 1992, yet it did so without considering that history, while asserting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was part of a gauzy right to privacy that includes "intimate and personal choices." The three controlling Justices in Casey sought to balance that right against the "potential life" of a baby in the womb.\par \par \uc2 But their judgment of how to strike that balance supplanted the moral choices of millions of Americans. "Our Nation's historical understanding of ordered liberty does not prevent the people's elected representatives from deciding how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be regulated," writes Justice Alito.\par \par \uc2 The three dissenting Justices claim the majority has steamrolled the doctrine of stare decisis, or respect for precedent. But Justice Alito's opinion deals step by step with the Court's traditional stare decisis analysis, and his most telling point is that Roe and Casey haven't come close to settling the issue.\par \par \uc2 The controlling Justices in Casey went so far as to make an essentially political plea that Americans let their ruling settle the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue. It was a futile attempt to end debate on a question that touches people at their deepest moral convictions. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to roil American politics, and states continue to pass laws challenging the logic of both opinions. When a ruling is still controversial and unworkable after five decades, that is compelling evidence it was wrongly decided.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  writes in a concurrence that the Court did not have to overturn Roe to uphold Mississippi's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks in this case. He says this would have been more judicially modest and less jolting to the public.\par \par \uc2 We agree on his point about upholding the Mississippi law, but such a halfway ruling would only have been a legal holding action. More states would have written more laws that would have challenged Roe and Casey, and sooner or later the Court would have had to overrule both or uphold some remnant of them as settled law. Better to take this opportunity to return the issue to the states sooner rather than later.\par \par \uc2 The political left is making much of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 's argument in a concurrence that the Court should revisit all of its precedents that are based on the use of substantive due process to find rights in the Constitution. That includes precedents on contraception and gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 Substantive due process is a long-time preoccupation of Justice Thomas, and we respect him for it. But the doctrine is also deeply embedded in countless Court precedents that have far better stare decisis claims than does Roe. Overturning the Obergefell ruling on gay marriage, for example, would jeopardize hundreds of thousands of legal marriage contracts. That's the definition of a reliance-interest justification for upholding a precedent. Justice Thomas also acknowledges in his concurrence that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is different from these cases, and note that no other Justice joined his opinion.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Which brings us back to the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and democracy. The debate will now shift from courts to the political branches, which should be healthy for the judiciary. Democrats made clear on Friday that they will make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a major campaign theme in the midterm elections, and President Biden declared that "this is not over."\par \par \uc2 Fair enough. Both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate will now have to achieve their policy goal the old-fashioned way\u8212\'20\'14through persuasion, not judicial fiat. Some in the pro-life movement want Congress to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide. But that will strike many Americans as hypocritical after decades of Republican claims that repealing Roe would return the issue to the states.\par \par \uc2 A national ban may also be an unconstitutional intrusion on state police powers and federalism. Imposing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  values of Mississippi or Texas on all 50 states could prove to be as unpopular as New York or California trying to do the same for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 One tragedy of Roe is that it pre-empted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate that was moving in the states a half century ago. That debate can now resume. Some states will ban it in most cases, while others like California may seek to pay for the abortions of women from other states.\par \par \uc2 It will take awhile, and more than one election, but we hope that eventually the public through its legislators will find a tolerable consensus, if not exactly common ground. That's the best we can ask for in our imperfect republic, if we can keep it.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-goes-back-to-the-people-supreme-court-roe-v-wade-dobbs-v-jackson-samuel-alito-11656107148"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Goes Back to the People}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
