\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in the States After Roe v. Wade; The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  has some surprising findings on the number and availability of abortions.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 22:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Now that the Supreme Court has overturned Roe v. Wade, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could soon become illegal in half of the country. Or so Democrats warn. But it's impossible to know how the debate will play out in many states. And a study this month by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, suggests that the practical consequences for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be far less severe.\par \par \uc2 Many GOP states have increased \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation in recent years, including parental consent and notification requirements for minors and mandated waiting periods. Yet abortions increased 8% nationwide between 2017 and 2020, according to Guttmacher's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider survey.\par \par \uc2 More abortions aren't something to celebrate, especially as births simultaneously declined 6% in the same period. But Guttmacher claims it may be a positive development "if it means people are getting the health care they want and need." This is a tacit admission that stricter laws in GOP states haven't stopped women from obtaining abortions.\par \par \uc2 One reason is that left-leaning states have expanded access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Abortions increased by 25% between 2017 and 2020 in Illinois as more pregnant women came from surrounding states with more restrictive laws. Illinois in 2018 also began covering abortions with state Medicaid funds. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers responded to increasing demand by opening more clinics.\par \par \uc2 Progressives claim traveling to other states to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a grave hardship for women. But the Guttmacher report suggests that thousands of women already do so. Many employers in recent months have also offered to cover the travel costs of employees in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Abortions have also increased as "local and national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds increased their capacity and helped even more people pay for their abortions," the report says. Many pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have used the potential demise of Roe to boost their fundraising. The Supreme Court's ruling on Friday will be a fundraising boon for Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 States that are more likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  already regulate it more strictly, so the Court's decision may have a smaller effect in those states. The Guttmacher report notes that because restrictions in recent years "were adopted in states generally considered hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights already, they may not have played as much of a role as the measures expanding access" in other states.\par \par \uc2 This evidence from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will still be widely available in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision on Roe v. Wade. States will respond to the Court's decision in different ways, and that's the beauty of the U.S. federalist system.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-in-the-states-after-roe-guttmacher-institute-supreme-court-dobbs-v-jackson-11656109885"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  in the States After Roe v. Wade}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220624ei6o007y5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
