\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 If Court Overturns Roe v. Wade, More Infants Might Be Placed for Adoption; Adoption centers aren't ramping up staffing just yet as any increase could be uneven\b0\par\par\uc2 By Clare Ansberry \par \uc2 1,151 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2022\par 23:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Infant adoptions have been on the decline in the U.S. Some sociologists and adoption specialists expect that number to increase {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/roe-wade-abortion-supreme-court-explainer-11651601806"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 if Roe v. Wade is overturned }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Adoptions have been falling due to better and more accessible contraception, more parents choosing to raise a child alone and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/state-barrier-abortion-roe-v-wade-11654170644"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . The number of private, as opposed to public, adoptions in the U.S.\u8212\'20\'14which includes noninfants and stepparent adoptions of older children\u8212\'20\'14decreased in 2020 to 39,601, a 20% drop from 2019, according to a recent report from the \uc2 National Council for Adoption\uc2 . Private domestic adoptions had reached close to 60,000 in previous years, the council said. Public adoptions, which make up more than half of all adoptions, are managed by the child-welfare system.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/livecoverage/roe-v-wade-supreme-court-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A draft decision leaked last month }}}\uc2  indicated that the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  might overturn Roe v. Wade, which would end the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If that happens, states would be free to apply their own laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/where-state-abortion-laws-stand-if-roe-v-wade-is-overturned-11651614828"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twenty-two states have laws }}}\uc2  that could ban or curb most abortions, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 With more restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, many adoption agencies, sociologists and larger non-profit organizations \u8211\'20\'13 as well as parents hoping to adopt \u8211\'20\'13 say they expect more babies would ultimately be available for adoption in the next few years. Not all adoption centers and organizations agree, with some saying there is no guarantee the number of adoptions will increase.\par \par \uc2 If there is an increase, it likely would be gradual and vary across the country, depending in part on state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, say those who study and work in adoptions. While a rise in adoptions might eventually spur groups to hire more staff and build out their operations, adoption agencies and the prospective adoptive parents who use them are in a wait-and-see mode for now.\par \par \uc2 Staffing up would be premature, says \uc2 Ryan Hanlon\uc2 , chief executive of the \uc2 National Council for Adoption\uc2 , an adoption advocacy nonprofit organization. "I don't think it's clear what the impact will be on the adoption community," he says.\par \par \uc2 Gretchen Sisson, a research sociologist at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who studies the ways women make decisions about pregnancy, expects the number of private infant adoptions nationwide to pick up by 50% in the next two to three years. She estimates that there are about 18,000 to 22,000 private infant adoptions a year and that another 10,000 would be added if Roe v. Wade is overturned.\par \par \uc2 "We don't know how rapidly the legal landscape will change in different states," says Dr. Sisson.\par \par \uc2 Nine out of 10 pregnant women who are denied abortions end up parenting the child, says Dr. Sisson, whose data comes from a study she and others published in 2016. Those who do place an infant up for adoption often had planned to raise the child, but lacked family or partner support to do so, and contact adoption agencies late in the pregnancy or after delivery, according to a 2022 study, which looked at 8,658 private adoptions that occurred between 2011 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 Suzie Williams, executive director of Open Adoption & Family Services in Portland, Ore., where state law allows for late-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offers counseling on adoption, parenting and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The agency completes open adoptions, in which birth parents and adoptive families maintain an ongoing relationship.\par \par \uc2 She is unsure whether more infants will be placed into adoption amid a potential rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, but she does expect pregnant women living in states where abortions are illegal to seek counseling regarding their options.\par \par \uc2 "If we need to staff up, we will," she says, adding that the agency has contracts with licensed social workers who can step in if needed. The agency recently started seeking grants to help fund services, including all-options counseling.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Busch, who is on the board of Open Adoption & Family Services, was 15 when she found out she was pregnant. She planned to raise her daughter with her partner at the time, but opted for adoption when she was seven months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 "It's one of the hardest decisions I have ever made in my life," says Ms. Busch, a 40-year-old nurse, who is running for Oregon State Senate.\par \par \uc2 She picked the adoptive parents and maintains a close relationship with them and her biological daughter, now 25, but says that isn't always the case.\par \par \uc2 "I know plenty of birth parents and adoptive families and adoptees who didn't get that level of openness and honesty and commitment," she says.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Nehrbass, president of Nightlight Christian Adoptions, an open adoption agency with offices in 10 states, says he is expecting and is fully staffed for an increase in adoptions over time.\par \par \uc2 His agency, which charges $30,000 for domestic adoptions and gets most of its referrals from crisis pregnancy centers or people googling Christian adoptions, has about 110 prospective parents in 10 states on a waiting list.\par \par \uc2 It takes about 18 months for those on his list to be matched with a birth mother, he says. He estimates that the number of hopeful adoptive parents exceeds the number of available babies by 50 to one.\par \par \uc2 "I do think there will be more babies not aborted and placed for adoption," he says.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 If Roe v. Wade is overturned, what impact will it have on adoptions? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Brock Martin, 31, and his wife have two children and want more, but struggle with infertility. Mr. Martin, director of regional evangelization at \uc2 Fellowship of Catholic University Students\uc2  in Denver, said they are on a "word of mouth" waiting list\u8212\'20\'14meaning their friends and contacts know they are interested in adopting. They aren't on an official waiting list at an adoption agency because the fees are high and their household income stands at about $60,000.\par \par \uc2 "I know dozens of couples, who want to stand in the breach and be the solution for women in crisis," he says.\par \par \uc2 Heidi Gonzalez, executive director of Adoptions From The Heart, based in Wynnewood, Pa., says she is concerned that some pregnant women could be pressured into placing a child up for adoption. Her agency offers counseling to help women make a choice, whether that means parenting a child or placing a child in an open adoption.\par \par \uc2 "Carrying a baby for nine months, going through labor and delivery, and then handing that baby over to another family can be a traumatic experience," she says.\par \par \uc2 Write to Clare Ansberry at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:clare.ansberry@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clare.ansberry@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/agencies-researchers-expect-rise-in-infant-adoptions-if-court-overturns-roe-v-wade-11655990810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If Court Overturns Roe v. Wade, More Infants Might Be Placed for Adoption }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020220623ei6n002xl\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 rst=wsjo AND (abortion OR "reproductive rights" OR "reproductive health") AND (atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive rights"))\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adju